      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THERE is more history to be made than ever was</p>
        <p>Written. A cumors sociological fact—that the Old Girl frequently develops into the New Woman. MONEY would be more enjoyable if it took people as long to spend it as it does to earn it. LITTLE Freddie, in a dark cellar with his uncle, clinging to him in great fear, said, "We ain't afraid, are we, Ulicle Tom?" "I LOST my head completely. And then I kissed her !" "I don't quite see how you managed it !" .</p>
        <p>WIFEY (who makes her own bread) : "Do you like brown bread, clear ? Hubby (thoughtfully) "Ye-es ; light brown bread !" "I FELT like a tin soldier in that riot," said the militiaman. " When the bricks began to fly, really it made my heart zinc. ' A woman never uses her husband's meerschaum pipe for a hammer more than once. "Name 12 animals of the polar regions," said the professor, and the despairing student wrote: "Six seals and six polar bears.' CHICAGO MAN (politely) : " Ab, allow me to hand you your hat, sir. This wind is terrible" -- St. Louis Man: "Yes, I saw you pick it up. Glad. you voufessed having it. I shall not forget your honesty, sir." F-',loNrs : • Miss Pinkley has great will power, don't you think?" B'Jinks : "Yes. It's too bad it should go to waste " B'Jones : " How SO?" B'Jinks : "She has nothing to will and nobody to will to."</p>
        <p>FISH STORIES. "Truth lies at the bottom Of the well" if you wish, Which is doubtless the reason No one goes there to fish.</p>
        <p>A READY ACQUIE;CENCE. —Twynn (after setting forth his plans) : "Don t you think my idea ought to he carried out?" Triplett (not favuurably impressed) : "Yes—and buried." DESIRABLE PERSONAGES.— Hanks: "The Chinese :should engage some New York boarding-house keepers trs sail cal their men of-war." Tanks: "Not to serve meals for them, I hope?" Banks: "No; to repel boarders." BENEFITS OF HIGH BUILDINGS.—LittIe Miss Freckles: "Did youse move up to the top floor because you is gettin' poor?" Little Miss Mugg : "Course not. Pa has got interested in astronomy, and wanted to be where he could see the moon close."</p>
        <p>THE PHILOSOPHER STUMPED. "1 oannot answer," said the sage, "Though wise in lore of every age. Grant me, 0 Fate, some easier task Than answering questions kids can ask I"</p>
        <p>No EYES FOR NIAGARA.—Farmer Oatcake: "I hear ye went to Niagary Falls on your wetidin' trip, Hiram. What d'ye think of 'ern?" Hiram Harrow (modestly) : "I couldn't exactly tell ye, squire. Ye see, I had Lucindy on my arm all the time, and I didn t have no eyes for no Falls." No SENSE OF HEMOLTR.—LittIe Johnny: "That young man who comes to see you must be pretty poor company. He hasn't any sense of humour." Sister: " Why d ) you think so" Little Johnny: "I told him all about the funny way. you rush around and hang doors when you get mad, and he didn't laugh a bit." IGNORANT BLISS.—" The other day I was walk. ing beside ti railway line with a man who was very hard of hearing. A train was approaching, and as it rounded the curve the whistle gave one of those ear-destroying shrieks which seem to pierce high heaven. A smile broke over the deaf man's face. 'That is the -first robin,' said he, that I have heard this spring.' " 'Boy, which is the quickest way for me to get to the railway station?" Accommodating boy: "Ron."</p>
        <p>PITTITE (in a London theatre during interval) : Waiter, you supply refreshment ?" Waiter: "Yes, sir." Pittite : " Will you oblige me by stepping to the door and getting me a glass of fresh air ?"</p>
        <p>t2HOOSING A NAME—A. : "What a splendid library you have got ! All novels, I declare ! " B. : "Yes, my wife bought them, to pick out a nice name for our li tie daughter." A. : "And what name did you fix upon ? ' B. : "Mary." IN THE COUNTF.Y.—A .trapping country lass, when talking about a Paririan lady who had taken her into her service, sail among other things : "In fact, she is so weak that, when she feels warm, she never fans herself, for fear of being thrown backwards to the ground." A SorrEtimo HEART.—Little Dick: "Mamma, may I go and play with Robby Upton, and stay there to dinner if they ask me?" Mamma : "I thought you didn't like Robby Upton." "1 .didn't; but as I passed his house just now my heart softened towards him." " Did be look lonely?" " No'm ;he looked happy." " What about?" "He said his mother was makin' apple dumplings." "A Goop DEFINITION.—Aunt (to little Oscar) : "What dress had your mother on when she drove off to the party this evening?" Oscar: "A long, white, short dress." Aunt : "Nonsense, it could not be long and short at the same time." Oscar: "Yes, aunty. It was long at the bottom and short at the top." .„</p>
        <p>THE BOST AND THE WORST. —Traveller : "Where is the best hotel in this place?' Porter: Do you see tha house over yond r ? That is the worst." Traveller : "1 don?t want the worst; it is the best hotel I want." Porter : " Can't tell you, I'm sure ; that's the only one we've got." MINISTER : " Will you keep this woman, in sickness and in health, so long as you both live?" Backwood Bridegroom: " Lookee bar, preacher, wouldn't it be better ter promise ter keep 'or in lots o' grub instid o' sickness ?" FIT FOR DESPERATE ENTERPRISES.—GoIightIy " What makes you so certain that your eleven will win the football game?" Captain Kickhard "I know my men. Every one of them has been rejected by the girl he loves." Too GOOD FOR FARM—Bioko : "Sad affair, Goodman's death. I don t believe he had an enemy in the world." " Jinks :"I don't believe he had, either. Why, even his relatives always spoke well of him !" "Are you interested in golf, Mrs. Jennings?" asked young Simpkins, after racking his brains for some time for something to say. "I'm not familiar with his works at all," replied the old lady. "What has he written?" "THAT is an enormoas pyramid you are build- ing," observed a royal visi or at Egypt's court; "but it seems a purposeless kind of structure. What is it for?" " When the top stone is laid," replied King Cheops, guardedly, you will see the point.' A WARNING—Ada: "I don't think Blanche cares nearly as much for her husband as she did." Ida: "Well, she has reduced him to complete sub- mission; and now, of course, she finds married life insipid." RHODES: "Do you think, Faith, dear, that your father will consent to our marriagi, ? " Faith : "Of course papa will be very sorry to lose me, but I suppose he will give his consent." Rhodes : "But I will tell him that instead of losing a daughter he will gain a son." Faith: "I wouldn't do that dear, if you really want me. Papa has three sons living at home now, and he's a little touchy on that point." ONE Lirrix MISTAKE. —She had attended the ambulance classes, and obtained the certificate. The street accidenr, she had earnest'y prayed for took place. A man had broken his leg. She confiscated the walking-stick of a passer-by and broke it in three pieces for splints. She tore up her skirt for bandages. When all was completed he summoned a cab and took her patient to the hospital. " Who bandaged this limb so credit- ably? " inquired the surgeon. " I did," she blushingly replied. " Well, it is most beautifully —most beautifull:t done but you have made, I find, one little mistake. You have bandaged the wrong leg." A FAVOURITE TOPIC.--Moses Mendelssohn was at first einp,oyed is tutor, and afterwards as book- keeper, by Mr. Bernhard, the wealthy silk manu- facturer. When 'he latter died, his widow pro. moted the philosop ier to a partnership in the firm. One day, as Men elssohn was passing down the street with his tnend Leasing, they overheard a couple of ladies talking about Mendelssohn. "There, you see,' said Leasing, "your writings are even popular with the lathes." "Oh ! dear no," was the modest reply, "the ladies are only talking about the new samples I have just sent them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>ALTEOCK'S CORN EXTRACTOR.</head>
        <p>ALTEOCK'S CORN EXTRACTOR.</p>
        <p>A Corn Plaster Combined (Patented), a wonderful improve ment. This Plaster is the well-known Allcock's Corn Pl:vter with a little die added that in a few days extracts the Corn and relief is felt as soon as applied; for all pressure of blot iS removed. D n't he put tiff with any other remedy as none other will give the immediate comfort. Palen 71ct and ls.lirt., per box of all Chemists. Sold by :-</p>
        <p>R. YOUNG, Pharmaceutical Ch (mist, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>The horseless carriage promises_ to become as popular as the bicycle in France. It is stated that orders have been given to the manufacturers for a number of these petroleum vehicles. The Mayor of Tunbridge Wells has purchased one in Paris, and proposes to hold an exhibition of the carriages in England about the middle of next month. Many of the competitors in the race between Paris and Bordeaux have promised to seni over their machines, and English people will have an opportu- nity of judging of the merits of the much-talked. of-coaches. The only difficulty in the way of the employment of the vehicles is that of providing a " coach-house." But this want is to be supplied in Paris by Comte de Dion, who proposed to organise a club for the use of the owners of the horseless vehicles. There is to be " stalling "for nearly one thousand of the machines, in addition to reading, dining, and smoking rooms for the members. It is reported that the principal newspaper proprietors in Chicago are arranging for a petroleum carriage race next month, and the first four winners are to receive prizes valued at £1.,500.</p>
        <p>The American War Office has received from an Austrian manufacturer 20 novel coats for soldiers. When not worn on the back they can be used as tents, two of them being sufficient to shelter two soldiers. They are curious-looking things,it is said. Holes are cut into the waterproof material for the arms, and around the fringe are rings, with cords and buttons, for fastening it when used either as a tent or as an overcoat. Two of the coats are hooked together when used as a shelter, the ends being fas- tened to the ground by ten pins ; while a rifle is placed in a middle ring.and serves the purpose of a tent-pole. The combined ccat and tent is reeem- mended for foreed marches or picket duty, but the officers of the United States War Office are not in- clined to look with favour upon the invention.</p>
        <p>It may now be regarded as practically settled that the next President of the Wesleyan Conference will be the Rel,..Marshall Rindles, of Didsbury College. His election to that distinguished position will be a fitting recognition of long and valued ser- vices rendered, not only in the Theological Chair, but in other important positions in Methodism. By those ministers who have had the advantage of be- ing trained under him his election will be hailed with satisfaction.</p>
        <p>There is one fact in connection with the new departure at the Trade Unions Congress at Cardiff which will be very gratifying to those who are desirous of preventing a schism in the ranks of labour. Certain opponents of the policy associated with the name of Mr. Burns have been talking loudly about the desirableness of starting an organisation to run in rivalry with the present Trades 11-ingress. They have also advised that, as a preliminary effort, those unions that disapprove of the policy adopted should at once take steps, even to the ex- tent of threatening withdrawal, in order to empha- sise their position. However, none of the real leaders in the movement are in sympathy with these proposals. Mr. Havelock Wilson, M.P..is per- haps the only politician who has publicly rebuked thembut the majority of really representative men who disspnrove of the new policy will do nothing that will affect the representative character of the Congress. Mr. Burns from the first anticipated this, and to-day he is persuaded that in a few month. all the bitterness will have practically disappeared.</p>
        <p>The immense sum of money left by the late Mr Edward Kensington has been the subject of a rood deal of talk. Not very many years ago Mr. Ken-• sington who was only fifty-five when he died, was managing-clerk in an advertising agent's office in the city. He was then in receipt of a salary of £2OO or £3OO per annum. But he went into busi- ness on his own account, got a large connection with company promoters, and died worth more than a quarter of a million—this in spite of the fact that his personal expenditure in his latter years was prodigal, and that it was a delight to him to spend gold where other men spent silver.</p>
        <p>The official announcement of the engagement of the Duke of 'Marlborough to Miss Consuelo Vander- bilt was anticipated by the New York journals some days ago. The lady is described by American jour- nals as " shy, tender, tall, and dark," hut they do not agree a to her sue, one giving it as 18 and another as 25. The. former is probably nearer the mark. as she only made her formal appearance in society last season. The Duke of Marlborough is 24 years old, and succeeded to the title in 1893 Lest month, on the opening of Parliament,he moved the Address in answer to the Speech from the Throne, and made a speech of considerable A tall, handsome youth, soldier-like in appearance, he i 9 popular alike in the Oxfordshire Yeomanry, in which he is a lieutenant, and at Blenheim, where much of his life bee been 'pen'. His father married fwice,his second wife being an American lady; and his uncle, Lord Randolph, also married an American heiress.</p>
        <p>Ever since the Rome Secretary promised to in- quire into the Mnyhrick case" with an open mind' the prisoner's friends have heen busily collecting and prepnr'ng evidence which they firmly believe will secure her release. The documents are already of a voluminous nature, and some of the state- ments, if true, are highly important. Before Sir Matthew White Ridley consented to consider the case, it will he remembered that Peeernl American Indies had arrived in England with a view to renew the agitation tor the release of the prisoner, and they are naturally pleased at the opportunity of nuhmitting, to the Government any proof they may have of the woman's innocence. But many of the Persons taking part in the appeal on Mrs. May- brick's behalf are not quite satisfied with the promise to irquire into the case. They fear that justice cannot be done unless the whole of the evi- dence is produced in open court,rind they are really ngitating for the establishment of a court of crim- inal appeal.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ruskin has cynically described photographers as second-band artists. That this is a libel On amateur w will he acknowledged by nil who see the photographs displayed at the Dudley Gallery. There the photographic salon will open its third exhibition on Thursday. The collection is in every respect superior to that of previous years. Por- traits are not numerous, and those exhibited do not display any exceptional reptit. But there is a good one of Mr. Ruskin, and another of Mr. S. Pope, QC., while a third of Mr. Crockett is exceedingly tine. The best " sun pictures." however, are of ' landscapes and rural nooks. Here and there one finds evidence of too carPful " retouching," which in many cases, instead of improving, mars the picture.</p>
        <p>There was a crisis in silver on Wednesday, and the market cvas for a time figitnted. For on Wednesday morning while the man and hoy in charge of a van laden with a considerable amount, of bar silver were refreshing themselves in a coffee- house at St. Pancras. horse end cart end silver dis- appeared bodily. The guardians of the treppure were left lamenting, and the owners of the silver were sharers in their grief, until the van was found at Islington intact. It was a mere freak of the horse.</p>
        <p>An advance copy of the annual report, of the London Wesleyan Methodist Mission gives detailed statements, supplied by the Rev. IT. Price Hughes, the Rev. Peter Thompson, and the other chief mis- sinners, ohowing• progress in every branch, and the latest numerical returns give a total membership, ineluding young persons and those on ,trial, of nearly seven thousand, viitherel for the moot part from the non-church-goinz population. The cost of the maintenance of the central branches is, RA might he supposed, very heavy, the general slate- ment showing an outlay of £23,000 Mr. Hughes Also spends between seven and eight thflusand Pounds per annum in Wet London. In his receipts this year are the proceeds of the sale of a pearl necklet, which realised the handsome sum of £l3OO.</p>
        <p>TIPP4'S COCOA.—GRATEFUL AND COMF'RTTNG —" By a thorough knowledge of the natural law, wlveh govern the operations of diges ion and nutrition, and by a eareful tiPPflestion of the fine properties of well-eleetnd COCOA, Mr Rpns has provided for our breakfast and supper a deileatelY flavoured beverage wh ch may save is 1110.71 V heavy do-tor's hung It, is lw the judWous use OF su h art ic'es 'f diet ttrt a constitution may be gractual'y built on ontil stromt enough to resist every tendency to disease. We may esespe m.nv a fatal shaft by keeping ourselves ad, for:1111,d with vure blood and a properly nourished f tam F. ." Ci, it ,c'errice Gazette Made simply with boiling water or milk.—Sold only in pa. ckets by Gravers labelld—" J v ama 'pp 9 &amp;co • Ltd.. 4rommmathic Chemists. London."--Also makers of Epps'. IThseoatne or Cocoa-Nib Rxtract : A thin beverage of full flavour now withmany beneficial,y taking the place of tea. Its Retire principle being a gent'e nor e stimulant, supplies the needed energy without unduly eaciting the system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NEI,IIE,</p>
        <p>Z think last week I described a sealskin jacket. lig you. This material is to be quite the thing this winter, and I must tell you of a short cape made ti it. It is a most elegant garment, with a yoke, and a small cape on the other. It is lined with cream and gold brocade. This cape, which is a most stylish one, is likely to be extremely fashion- able during the forthcoming demisaison.</p>
        <p>Crdpon has by no means lost its popularity, and I saw the other day a very stylish dress of that material. The crtSpon was that known as Oplielia, and the cuffs were to match. The sleeves, like the draped sash and streamers, were in Pekin silk, striped cream and Ophelia. There was a pleated front and neck band, with Pompadour bows in silk muslin, and sailor collar, and vandyked ends in antique Venetian lace. The hat was in mixed straw, faced with lace, and lavishly trimmed with roses, foliage, and loops in chine ribbon. This style of dress also looks well in alpaca.</p>
        <p>This is a suggestion for renovating one of last year's winter dresses. It is a brown cloth with black almond boucle effect, and in its present condition has a black silk yoke. short basque bodice, and plain gigot sleeves. The skirt, too, is moderately full and gathered into the band. I should cover the yoke with a brown velvet collar, edged with a kilting of black silk, and the cuffs on the sleeves should be carried out in the same way. Both neck a waistband may be of black or brown silk, folded and cut on the bias. Do not forget this latter hint, as no fabric belt or band sets well cut on the straight, and this is a fact which seems to be forgotten by so many young amateurs. Then the pleats will require attention, as there are two worn now instead of three down the back. The gathering should be taken out and arranged differently, and a lining of horsehair would be a great improvement.</p>
        <p>Though shop windows are beginning to display autumnal, or rather, wintry headgear, we still cling to the rough straw, shady hat, to shelter us from a sun that has lost, so far, little of its power. Undulating brims are very fashionable, and will continue to be so, even on the smaller hats, which are being shown as the latest Parisian models. Here is a very becoming hat, in moss-green straw, and trimmed with chiffon rosettes and velvet bows. Just inside the brim, which is turned up a little, is a band of violet velvet, matching the ribbon bows. This is made with an inch-wide strip of velvet cut on the cross, folded and slip-stitched on to the wire, which it really covers. Right round the crown are pale pink pansy rosettes. Pansy and poppy rosettes are very fashionable just now, but they are not easy to make, for so much depends on the deftness of the fingers</p>
        <p>Then there is a very charming up-to-date hat in brown satin straw, intermixed with chenille, and arranged with a waved brim. it is trimmed with pink velvet roses and green poppies, which have crinkled petals. On one side of the crown is a stiff white aigrette, while draping the crown is some Pompadour ribbon tied in a large bow at the back. Felts are, in spite of the rivalry of straw, very popular, and of course they will become still more so as the autumn comes on. A neat grey summer felt has a highly-curled brim, ant is trimmed with grey rep bow. These hats are extremely comfort- able for travelling and riding, etc. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>A false alarm of fire was raised at the EVack pool rower Pavilion during the performance on Monday evening. A panic ensued among the crowded audience, and there was a rush for the doors. Happily the exits proved sufficiently commodious to allow the frightened persons to leave the hall without serious injury result- ing to anyone. The Tower Company have offered a re• ward for the detection of the person who gave the base- less alarm.</p>
