      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEFTEMBFR 28 1895. GLENALPINE'S OATH;</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEFTEMBFR 28 1895. GLENALPINE'S OATH;</p>
        <p>It is rumoured that Mrs. Langtry intends to abandon the stage, and live on tier farm in the United States. The Japanese method of lacquering is said to be at least two thousand years old. Pieces made ten cen- turies ago are still exhibited.</p>
        <p>Coffee is taxed £62 a ton in France, £56 in Italy, £4O in Austria, £25 in Portugal, £22 in Norway. £2O in Ger- many and Spain, £l5 in Russia and £l4 la Great Britain.</p>
        <p>People hiccough because of a muscular contraction of the diaphragtn. It is supposed to be sympathetic, and to arise from an effort of the diaphragm to assist the stomach to get rid of some undigested or disagreeable matter.</p>
        <p>The total exportation of tomatoes from the Canary Islands is now about one hundred and fifty thousand cases, or something like two tl.ousand seven hundred tons of fruit. It is grown, packed, shipped, and de- livered in London at an actual cost of twopence a pound. In Holland the peasant girl who is without a beau at fair times hires a young man for the occasion. As good dancers command a high price, two maidens sometimes club together to employ the same swain. In France a very good gas is said to be made from the fatty materials contained in the soapsuds after washing wools and yarns. The wash water of a woollen mill with 20,000 spindles will, it is claimed, annually yield enough oi this substance to produce 1,100,000 feet of gas. Charcoal is a powerful disinfectant because the carbon of which it is composed readily absorbs and combines with many different kinds of noxious gases, thus re- moving unpleasant odours and harmful substances from the air. An amusing printer's error occurred in a recent issue of the " Times." In announcing the marriage of a young City man to the daughter of a naval instructor at Sheerness, a few words of a telegram which apparently belonged to some notes from China or from Armenia were printed after the interesting intelligence. They read as follows :—" Trouble is expected."</p>
        <p>There is in Minnesota a girl's school of agriculture, said to be the only one lathe world, and the results it has achieved have been most gratifying. It has been established for some time, and the students receive in- structions in cooking, canning, sewing, dairying, fruit, and flower culture, household chemistry, and entomo- logy.</p>
        <p>Glass Eyes. A well-known nptician selis a thousand glass eyes A year, and he reckons that the number of elass eves used annually in the United Kingdom is about fifty thousand. The average life of a good Engl.sh glass eye is twelve months. English workmanship in this article of commerce is superior to all others. The British variety costs a guinea. Some people wear two 'lass eyes for the sake of appearances ; and it is very difficult indeed to tell a glass eye if it fits properly. Teardrops gradually destroy the enamel of the glass eve,and nothing has been discovered to resist the acid contained in tear. Many ladies with artificial eyes have secured kusbands ; and, indeed, play can be effectively made with a glass eye. Winking is even possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Bicycle Neck.</head>
        <p>The Bicycle Neck.</p>
        <p>Another danger for cyclisis has been unearthed ; but this time we fancy it is more fanciful than real. Hus hands, brothers, and lovers are said to be urging their [air relations to ride the wheel. And there is scarcely a husband, brother and lover who does not, if our inform ation be occurrate, now regret his temerity. " The husband," we are told by a correspondent, "has fouud Oat it has added to his nervous impairment in the ratio of about 50 per cent., and in addition thereto he has caught the husband neck. This strange malady ic caused by the constant craning of the neck, generally to the left, tfo see if the wife of one's bosom is safe Even after the husband has found that his wife ha, really become a skilled rider the habit remains. Hr can no more help turning his head at short intervals than he can help imagining that all sorts of dire disas• ters are befalling her."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Aristocratic Milkmem.</head>
        <p>Aristocratic Milkmem.</p>
        <p>The milk business is becoming arist.ocratio. I am ir receipt of a circular from the Earl of Egmont, who is anxious to supply myself and family with that nourish- ing and necessary liquid, writes Mr. Jerome in " To- Day." IF I require it he will also supply me with asses' milk. Indeed, his Grace seems to make a speciality of asses' milk, for I notice that the registered telegraphic address is " Asses' Milk, London." To encourage me. the Earl mentions a few of the customers already belong- ing to his walk. Amon., them I find two Royal High nesses, nine Marquesses, eighteen of his brother Earls, eleven Lords, thirty-nine Ladies, all the Rothschilds, a Field-Marshal er two, a couple of Admirals . . . a tew Generals, and a humble Knight. Really it seem, quite an easy way of getting into society. The Belgravia Dairy Company could give one of the most swagger " A, Homes" of the season. lam going to write to Barn Rothschild and bady Tichborne, asking them how they like their milk. If they speak well of his Grace I am going to deal with the Belgravia Dairy Company, and if I do not get My fair allowance of cream the Earl of Egmont will hear from me. Seriously, the Belgravia Dairy Company's circular is a fine stroke of business. P is snobbery—" Imptnial cement," as I once heard it , wittily called—that holds a people together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>EFFECT OF A LETTER.</head>
        <p>THE EFFECT OF A LETTER.</p>
        <p>Some letters bear in themselves evidence of such sound judgment, go( d sense, and absolute sincerity that a single word added to them would be a blemish. Happy the man who gets such letters ! They are the outcome of honest and not undiscriminating good sense The following is such a letter. It is dated from 6, Pickthall-terrace, Millwood, Todmorden, and runs thus</p>
        <p>" Gentlemen,—l am very pleased te be able to add my testimony to the very large accumulation that 70u must already have, with regard to the efficacy of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
        <p>" For more than 12 months, whilst residing in Leeds, was quite unable to work, my ailment, or rather ail- ments, being so many and complicated ; great depres- sion, extreme lassitude and weariness, loss cl appetite and nervousness being chief. I lost weight every week, and I almost wore away to skin and bone whilst under the treat's:lent of various doctors, and taking gallons of medicine which did me not the smallest good, but indeed seemed to greatly aggravate my complaints; and so much so, that I gave all up and made up my mind to allow things to take their course, and had been doing so for some time when a friend advised me to try your Pills. I was so irritable and ill that I did not follow this advice for many weeks, but my wife kept reminding lee of the matter, and at last brought me a box, which lam thankful te say I took, and began gradually to mend—very slowly, it is true, but surely, and after about half-a-dozen boxes I was quite a different person, and fairly healthy again. I continued the use of the Pills for a month or so, and I am to-day as healthy and hearty as I could wish, and quite up to normal weight. If at any time I feel a bit out of sorts, I immediately have recourse to Dr. Wil- liams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and am all right again in no</p>
        <p>"You may use this letter in any way you think fit. "Yours very sincerely, WM. E. BROWN." This is not an isolated letter received by this Company,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EVERY POST BRINGS THEM</head>
        <p>EVERY POST BRINGS THEM</p>
        <p>—and it is a well recognised fact that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are one of the medical marvels of the age. They cure rheumatism, neuralgia, locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, and prostration; diseases of the blood, such as scrofula, chronic erysipe- las, and restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health. They are a specific for all the troubles pecu- liar to the female sex, and in men cure all cases arisinf from worry, overwork, or excesses. Sold by all chemists, and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Solhorn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 134 91. Genuine only with full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>MORECAMBE DISASTER.</head>
        <p>THE MORECAMBE DISASTER.</p>
        <p>VERDICT OF THE JURY.</p>
        <p>The Coroner's inquiry into the death of the three people who lost their lives by the collapse of a landing stage at Morecambe pier on the 9th inst. WM brought to a close on Monday. A report prepared by Colonel Addi- son, an inspector of the Board of Trade, was read, in wh;ch he stated that corrosion of bolts was the direct cause of the accident. He condemned the construction of what are known as the eastern landing stages, and recommended their replacement on a better system. Colonel Addison insisted on the necessity of a periodical inspection of the pier. The Coroner (Mr. L. Bolden) commented in his summing up on the fact that the en- gineer who examined the pier last sounded no note of alarm as to the condition of the landing stages. The jury found that the accident was caused by the corrosion of a bolt, and expressed the opinion that the directors of the Pier Company had been guilty of great negligence.</p>
        <p>Last year five thousand pounds worth of rPmittances were posted in letters without any aadresq. Nearly thirty-one thousand letters were posted without address, and over thirty-two thousand stamps were found loose in the post.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>OR, THE MARK OF THE BLACK CROSS.</head>
        <p>OR, THE MARK OF THE BLACK CROSS.</p>
        <p>Y SYLVAIcUS COBB, J.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV. A THUNDERSTROKE!</p>
        <p>Sir Jasper and Arthur had reported at head- quarters, and were seated at a private table of the Kin°. William's Inn. "My dear boy," said the general, who had been for some moments thoughtfully sipping his Mar- sala, but setting down the glass as he spoke, " I really cannot tell whether the remarkable incidents of our adventure in the jungle have made Sir John or myself the happier man. As for yourself, your happiness must be beyond compute. "I am happy, Sir Jasper," replied the youth, with a soberness that was almost solemn, "hut the emoti nis not of the jubilant kind. I recog- nise the great good that has been opened to me, but at the same time I feel the weight of new and important responsibilities. If I may be allowed to judge of my own feelings, I am moved chiefly by an earnest, yearning desire to prove myself worthy of the love and confidence that have been given me. Perhaps, Sir Jasper, you would hardly expect to find that motive uppermost in my thoughts." "Why so, my boy ?" "Because I have been so wild and wayward in the times that are gone." "Bah !" cried the old general, with an emphatic thump of his glass upon the table. "Your deep, still water, lying darkly in broad pools and wind- ing nooks, where toads and lizards and serpents swarm, I never dare to drink. It is the sparkling, dashing stream, breaking over sharp rocks, anti leaping along with merry music, seeking something broader and more stable into which to pour itself, that yields the pure element." Arthur smiled at the general's quaint simile, and a grateful light was in his clear blue eyes. After a pause Sir Jasper said : "I held a conversation with Sir John upon a very important subject, Arthur." "Yes. But before I explain its substance I wish to ask you a question. You will pardon me, I know. You are, beyond all doubt, heir of Birch- mear, with its great wealth and peerage. When you were simply Arthur Rainsford, with acaptain's commission to mark your highest rank in life,Alary Wallace loved you, and I did not forbid her. How will it be now ?" b - . - -</p>
        <p>"How will it be now ?" repeated Arthur, slowly and uncertainly, as though he were not sure that he had heard aright. "Can Mary love me less because my prospects in life have brightened ?" "No,' said Sir Jasper, shaking his head,—" did not mean that." . .</p>
        <p>Arthur read the meaning in the old man' p ear- nest look. -</p>
        <p>" Sir, Jasper, do you wonder if I will still— But, no—you Cannot hold such a query in your mind." "And yet, my boy, it is natural that I should ask myself the question." The youth reached over and laid his open palm upon the back of the old soldier's hand.</p>
        <p>"Sir Jasper,", he said, with a quivering lip, and - with a rich, warm moisture gathering in his eyes, "before I dreamed that the love of Lord Birch- mear could return to me I was blessed with the love of your -sweet ward. It is something to be the heir of a baronial estate, and I own that I find pride and pleasure in the thought that I, if I live, shall be Baron of Birchmear. But, as God lives, I would cast all hopes of the peerage to the winds sooner than I would give up the pure love of Mary Wallace ! The one, of itself alone, is but an empty title, while the other is the very sum of all earthly joy." "Bravo, my boy! Spoken like a man !" And Sir Jasper grasped the youth's hand, and shook it vigourously. "And now," he proceeded, "I will tell you what Sir John and I have been talking about. It was about this very subject. Why, he loves my darling as though she were his own child; and from her lips, I opine, he gathered the fresh inspiration of love that led him through the jungle In quest of yon." And Sir John will smile upon our union 7" "He will insist upon it, my boy. So, now you have the way clear before you." "Bless you! my dear Sir Jasper, I think I am the happiest man alive." "It only remains," nodded the general, as he arose from the table, "to see if Mary will venture to bestow her love upon one who is likely to become a peer of the realm. It was a bold and dashing hero who won her heart." "A bold and dashing deed may have won her first regard," ventured Arthur; "but let rne hope that she found deeper and more enduring qualities to love." " Egad ! my boy, you are a philosopher! But come—you must go to the governor-general's, and close up your report, and I must go home. My darling may have heard of our return, and will be anxious. You will come up with Colonel Tabor this evening." "I will be there' Sir Jasper." "All right. I'll have a quiet corner reserved for you. Mary will want to hear the story of your new adventure." Arthur left Sir Jasper at the door of the inn, and repaired to the government offices, where he made his reports in detail. It was dark before his busi- ness was completed, and after this he joined Col. Tabor, and proceeded to the dwelling where he was to rejoin Sir Jasper. As they entered the hall the colonel said:</p>
        <p>"There are lights in the drawing-room—l think you will find Sir Jasper there. 1 must run up to my own apartment." Ordinarily Arthur would have knocked before entering the small drawing-room where the general was wont to receive his particular friends ; but on the present occasion his eagerness led him pre- cipitately on, and he pushed open the door and entered. The apartment was lighted by two large astral lamps set in brackets over the sandal-wood side board, and but a single occupant was there. It was Mary Wallace, sitting by a small ohm table, with her head resting upon her hands. She was dressed in t lack, and her dark brown tresses floated down against a cheek that looked like marble. She had not heard the opening of the door.</p>
        <p>Arthur spoke her name. She started up with a quick, frightened cry, and gazed upon the intruder. Arthur met her wild, weird gaze, and was terrified. Was this Mary Wallace? or was it some pain-laden spirit that wore her outer form of beauty without the soul and the life? She was pale and wan, and her dark eyes were dimmed as though with a wearing, deadening anguish. It Mary 1,, She started back, and put out her hands as if to keep him from her. "In God's name, Mary, what is this f" he asked In a hoarse whisper. He was like one in a dream. He was not sure that his senses were serving him truthfully. "Arthur." she said, struggling mightily, "this Is cruel. Why have you come ?" "My God I she is stricken with some fearful—" "Hush l" interposed the maiden, waving him off.</p>
        <p>She reflected a moment, and seemed to compre- hend the nature of the suspicion that had possessed him. . "Arthur." she said, restraining herself with the utmost difficulty, "do you not know—have you not heard—"</p>
        <p>"Herd what? 0! Mary—what is it? My own blessed love! My—" "Arthur !—Mr. Berwick l—you know not what you say. Let me go. My guardian will explain all."</p>
        <p>"But, Mary." And in his wild frenzy of fear and doubt he rushed forward and caught her by the hand.</p>
        <p>She sprang from him with a sharp cry, and again put up her hands to warn him away. "0, do not touch me !" she exclaimed. "The day is passed, and the night is come. Let me go, sir !"</p>
        <p>Arthur drew back, and gazed upon her. She stood there, crouching and trembling, as though she feared he would strike her. For a time his vision was blurred, and his head swam. Was he under the influence of some dreadful incubus, or was it all real?</p>
        <p>He drew still further away, and spoke softly and earnestly:</p>
        <p>"Mat:Y, in the name of Heaven, I conjure you to speak to me plainly. What haa happened? Do you love me no more ?' " 0 cruel, wicked !—" These words burst from her lips, and then she hesitated.</p>
        <p>"What have I done? What dreadful fate has fallen upon your love?" the youth asked, strugglng with his astonishment. "NQ more No more. sir t" returned Marv,</p>
        <p>"you do not know. If you had known, you woad not have come hither." "Not have come hither? Did you not wish to see me, Mary?" "Never !—never again 1" Thus speaking the maiden gathered strength, and standing erect, with a flash of pride and dig- nity breaking for the moment above her great agony, she added: "Henceforth we are as though we had never met. 0! would to God I had died ere you crossed my path ! Stand back! In mercy's name, touch me not ! Farewell! For the last time in life I speak your name—ARTHUR, farewell 1" And having thus spoken, she turned and left the apartment, closing the door behind her. Ar- thur stood and gazed upon the spot where she had disappeared—gazed until his utter bewilderment overpowered him—and then he sank down upon the seat which she had occupied, with his head bowed upon the ebon table. How long he remained thus he could not tell, but he was at length aroused by feeling a hand upon his shoulder. He looked up, and beheld Sir jasper. The old soldier's face bore deep traces of pain, and tear-tracks were upon his cheeks. "My poor boy, you are under a cloud." Arthur started up and grasped the old man by the arm. A moment he stood, as though trying to collect his scattered senses, and then he</p>
        <p>cried : "Sir Jasper, have I been dreaming, or is this horrible thing the shadow of a dreadful reality?" "Sit down—sit down, my boy—and let us talk calmly. Collect all your strength, and be patient." "But—Sir Jasper it real V "Sit down, Arthur, and listen." The young man resumed his seat, and the general drew up a chair for himself. "My dear boy, I have no particle of blame for you. Be assured, in the outset, that my friendship will not be withdrawn." "In Heaven's name, Sir Jasper, tell me what has happened! I shall go crazy under the pressure of this suspense." "Be patient, Arthur, and I will tell you." "Let it be in few words." "I will be as brief as possible. It is sad business —very, very sad." "Sir Jasper !" "Forgive me. I will not keep you in suspense. You wonder if Mary spoke seriously when she said she wished never to see you again. I will tell you the story, and you shall then judge for yourself. Mary, as you know, is an orphan—the child of my dead sister. She and her brother were the only children. Their father, an officer in. the army, died when they were quite young. Their mother died later. Her brother was five years older than herself, and had, through my means, gained an appointment in the army before the death of their mother. This brother was a wild, reckless, way- ward boy, but Mary loved him dearly. And on his part, I think his sister was the only thing on earth that commanded serious and heartfelt affec- tion. She was his idol, and he worshipped her. You know Mary's heart well enough to know how tenderly she must have loved such a brother. His very faults, glaring as they were, drew him very near to her through her earnest prayers to Heaven In his behalf. But their father's name was not Wallace. After their mother's death I adopted Mary as my own, and gave her my name. Her family name was—TALFORD." Arthur started and gasped for breath. He pressed his hand over his heart, as though to stop a wound where a dagger had been plunged in. His gaze was for the moment wild and vacant. He se.w not what was before him; but he saw another scene in another land—a scene of horror and of death! "Lionel Telford was Mary's brother!" pro- nounced Sir Jasper, in a whisper. The stricken youth bowed his head upon his bosom and groaned aloud. Had he held a loaded pistol in his hand, he might, at the moment, have ended by his own act the life which, in one brief hour, had become so utterly shattered and seem- ingly worthless. After a time Sir Jasper laid his hand upon the sufferer's arm, and spoke : "Arthur, I had nut dreamed of this. When you told me the story of your duel at Birchmear you gave me no name, and I asked for none. I know what Lionel was—l know that he was quarrel- some in his cups and of quick temper. I can see how easily the feud might have grown. I have myself fought two duels, though, thank God ! I did not kill my man in either case." Arthur looked up earnestly and imploringly, and took the old soldier by the hand. "Sir Jasper, I was forced into that horrible pit of evil. I cannot explain all now. lam willing to bear the blame of the folly that led to the meet- ing, but I had no ill-feeling in my heart against Lionel. I was led to believe that I should be held as a coward. I was challenged. I dared not re- fuse to meet the man whom they told me I had struck. I met him—l offered him my life in repa- ration while I fired into the air. Then they told me it was thrice cowardly thus to offer deliberate insult by accepting icy friend's fire and refusing to return it. You know the rest. I sought to avoid it still. I meant to have fired into the ground, but my muzzle was higher than I thought.' "I believe you, my boy," said Sir Jasper, feel- ingly; and I need not tell you what a blow this is to me. One of the proudest hopes of my life is shattered forever ! And yet 4 Arthur, God knows I have no blame to heap upon you." "And Mary," ventured the youth, in halting ac- cents, "she will—never—" "Arthur," said the general, as our hero came to a dead stop, "you cannot ask me of her. Your own heart should tell you all. Whatever may , have been the circumstances under which the dire- ful event was brought about, she can only feel that the blood of her brother is upon your hands. Ask yourself, my boy—how can she ever take that hand again without a shudder? Remember, that first in her love was the brother who had shared with her the gloom and the mourning of orphanage. She would be outraging the memory of the loved and the lost should he give her hand to the—" " Stop ! stop 1" groaned Arthur. "No more of that! I see—l understand. 0 if I could have known that it was her brother's blood my hand had spilled, I would not have outlived the dark day in the which we did battle with the pirates." "No, no, Arthur. There is a brave life ahead. Gird up your loins anew, and live it like a man." Again the youth bowed his head upon his hands and groaned in agony of spirit. "Poor Mary ! ' murmured Sir Jasper, thinking aloud, "I could almost wish she had never known It',' Arthur looked up and put out his hand. "Who told her of this!" " Lady Birchmear." The youth arose and staggered across the room. He came back and stood for a time with his hands pressed tightly upon his brow. "Sir Jasper," he at length said, huskily, and with painful effort, "I dare not trust myself to speak further now. I think I shall never be lord of Birchmear 1" "Arthur !" "Hush I I have no heart left for life. I will join my regiment. You will ask her—Mary—to think less harshly of me when I am away npon the fiery border, with my life exposed every day and every hour. You will try and made her under- stand that the deed was never in my heart—never I never !" "Arthur! My dear boy !" "Say to her—O! bid her listen willingly while you tell it—that with my last breath I will bless her To this poor heart no other love will ever come. She will believe you, and she will listen for the sake of the poor, outcast wretch who would have esteemed it the greatest prize of earth to have died for her !" Sir Jasper arose, and would have taken the youth by the hand, hut he shrank away. "No more now, Sir Jasper. My heart is burst- ing, and my brain is on fire. Let me seek the fresh air. Follow me not." " Arthur ! In God's name—" He read the old man's fear in his face, and quickly putting forth his hand to stop his speech, he said., with a strength of the old frankness in tone and manner: "My dear friend, fear not THAT! I am safe from myself. Go find Mary, and tell her that I am gone; that living I will love her with holy worship; that loving even unto death, I will see her no more! Go I" Thus speaking Arthur seized his cap and hurried from the house—hurried out into the street— hurried away from the hum of life—and finally wandered alone in a deserted nook of the espla- nade, beneath the darkening walls of the citadel. (To be continued).</p>
        <p>To MOTHERS.—Mrs. Vvii slow's Soothing Syrup has been sed over fifty yenrs by millions of mothers for theirchildren he le teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor ,fferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it produces ucal quiet sleep, by relieving the child from vain. and the tie cherub awakes 'a; bright as a button." Of all ,olnista,,la. lid. per hottle.—Adst.</p>
