      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FIRE AT A DOUGLAS BOA ItDING</head>
        <p>THE FIRE AT A DOUGLAS BOA ItDING</p>
        <p>HOUSE.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE VICTIMS..</p>
        <p>_ Further particulars are now to band of the fire at a Douglas boarding house, whereby Margaret Hinds, 21, of Warrington, and Margaret Higgins, 27, of Liverpool, were burnt to death. At the inquest Mrs. Archer deposed that Higgins belonged to Liverpool and Hinds to Warrington. On Thursday night last week they retired to bed, and witness, after seeing that everything , was safe, also went to bed. At five o'cloek in the morn- ing she was aroused by the ringing of the bell. Answer- ' lug it, she found a policeman at toe door, who said the attics were on fire. Rushing upstairs she found the ,1 attic in which Higgins and Hinds slept in fdimes. The door was baited from the inside, and it was impassible to force it—Police sergeant Fayle said that at five o'clock in the morning he was on his rood, and was passing Broadway in company with another constable. They noticed a smell of burning, but for a time could not locate it. Then they heard a woman's screams, and ' believing that a female was being assaulted they went to the back of the houses. Finding nothing they returned, and then noticed a blaze from the roof of Archer's house. Witness sent the other constable to call the fire brigade, and himself went into the house. With theassistance of W. Quirk, a member of the fire brigade, and of the visitors staying in the house, lie tried to force the door of the servant's attic, but the smoke and flame baffled them. The brigade arrived within half-an-hour with reel and hose, and soon got the fire, which had spread to the adjoining house, under. They then entered the room under the servants' attic, and found that the body of one of the women sleeping in the attic had fallen through the ceiling into the room. The other body was entangled in the half-burned rafters. Both bodies were shockingly burned beyond recognition. The feet of both were burned away, but the hair on the head ef one of the bodies was untouched. It was hrown in colour. The bodies were removed to the mortuary.— William Quirk, son-in-law of Mrs. Archer and a mem- ber of the fire brigade deposed that he resided in Vic- toria House. He spoke to being aroused by the alarm of fire. Fie thought the girls must have been reading in bed and gone to sleep, leaving the candle burning, and that the flame of the candle set the bedding on ere. He said everything possible was done by Sergeaet Frayle , and himself to rescue the girls, hut it was impossible ,1 owing to the intense heat and thick smoke, to force the door of the attic. 1 Several jurors expressed the opinion that they should have further evidence as to the fire brigade system and as to the extinguishing appliances and fire escapes. They would also like the evidence of Superintendent O'Hara cnief of the fire brigade.—The inquiry was ac- cordingly adjourned till Wednesday. The funeral of Margaret Higgins and Margaret Hinds, the victims, took place on Saturday aftarnoon. It was expected that the relatives of the girls would have crossed to the Island to arrange for the interment, but as they did not arrive, the bodies were buried at the cost of the Poor Relief Fund. The interment was at Kirk Braddan Cemetery, and was attended by a number of ' townspeople. The adjourned injuiry was held on Wednesday.—The Captain of the Fire Brigade admitted that there was no Central station where an alarm could be given, and that the members of the brigade had to be sum - maned by word of mouth. The fire-escape was kept in a locked Yard, and the manual engine was housed in another yard, a third of a mile away.—Mr. leennison, architect, said the room was never intented for habita- tion, or even as a lumber-room. His original plan showed no access from the staircase.—The Coroner, in summing up, did not think any blame attached to any- one. except perhaps those who allowed the poor girls to sleep in a room of the kind. He thought there had been no avoidable delay in bringing the esca peitc. to the house, locking to where it was kept and the manner of calling the brigade together.—The jury returned a ver- dict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>Thr Chinrao Government, according to a telegram from St Petersbure,haa decided to r.oreanige the navy. Both ships and material are to he ordered abroad.</p>
        <p>DON'T 1313 DIiCRIVRD.—Don't think the hair can he pre- re^eed without proper nourishment sit hair specialists nositively nx.ert that pure creAse is absolutely necessary for the preservKtion of the hair and ROW I.AND'!.; MACAS3 AR OIL is without doubt the best, it nourishes the hair, pre- vents it feting off or turning grey, eradi ate' scurf, end arrests baldness. Also In a golden colour for fair hair. Bottles 3s. 6d., Ts.. 10a. 6d. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OlL.—Preserves the hair. n.,wr.s.wo's MACASSAR OlL.—Prevents baldness. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OlL.—Prevents scurf. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OM—Beautifies ,the hair. Ask anywhere for ROWLAND'S MACASSAR Om of 20, Hatton Harden and avoid cheap, worthless imitations,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>MIMP PIIR •</head>
        <p>MIMP PIIR •</p>
        <p>that they did not see much of the plaCe. They pro- ceeded along the China seas to Shanghai. Here a great many E,lglist' people are in business, it being the great export place in China for tea. They visited the Chinese Part ot the city,and they in Lancashire could haverlittle idea of what the dirt aid filth was. He could tell them that the dirtiest place to be found in any slum in Lan- cashire was heaven to what it was in that Chinese city. The streets were very narrow, and the stenches were something frightful. His wife used Eau-de-Cologne the ' whole time, and he kept his pipe going. (Hear, hear.) The Chinese had no idea )1 cleanliness, and the marvel to him was that they lived at all. They were a filthy, dirty people, and in his opinion it would have b.e blessing if the Japanese had swept them from the face of the earth. From Shanghai they took steamer to Nagasaki</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>_EXAMI ciAt, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 28 1895.</head>
        <p>THE _EXAMI ciAt, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 28 1895.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in connection with St. Peter's Church, Warrington' was held in St. Peter's Schoolroom on Thursday and Friday last. The roam had beers very artistically arranged,and the whole appearance tell icted great credit upon the persons in charge of the arrange- inf•nts. The promoters were very desirous of raising £l5O, as a great amount of work is being. done in the parish. The parish is inhabited by practically no wealthy !persons, and consequently much help is needed from outsiders. The appeal was not made in vain, for on Thursday, the opening day, there was a large number present, at the time of the opening ceremony by Mrs. Lvon. Besides Mrs. Lyon, there were also present, the Revs. Canon Willis, B. Percival, J. A. Cooil, E. C. E. Carleton, J. Davies, and H. V. Pigot, Captain Buckton. and Messrs. P. Edgar, Joseph Taylor. and Kay. There were nine stalls which were arranged round and in the centre of the room. The following were the stall hol- ders :—Fancy stall. Mrs. Percival, Miss Green, Mrs. Edgar, and the Misses Broadbent; Mrs. Savage's stall, Mrs. Savage, Mrs. Gandy, Mrs. Baltnforth, and Miss Madeley ' • teachers' stall, Miss Roberts, Misses Butter- worth, Miss Williams, Miss Thompson, Mrs. Richardson, and Miss Knott ; plain sewing Mrs. Skelland ; flower and frui stall, Mrs. Parson, and t stall, Miss Place, Miss Calm, Miss Morrell, and the pupil teachers ; refreshment stall, Misses Brookes, Mrs. Cuppage, Miss Cuppago,Miss Agnes Cuppage, Misses Woods, Mrs. Proud, Mrs. T. Proud, and Miss Smith ; sweet stall, Miss Maggie Per- cival, Miss Arline Harrison, and Miss Marie Randall ; advertisement stall, Mrs. Kay, and Mrs. Taylor; book stall, Rev. J. A. Cooil. . . .</p>
        <p>Mrs. LYON, who was cordially received said that she was to ask them to be especially generous that day. Mr. and Mrs. Percival were doing good work in the parish, and were well provided for its spiritual necessities. It was a difficult problem to solve how to find the funds for the work, as there were no wealthy parishioners to assist them, she believed. They were therefore obliged to ap- peal to the persons outside the parish, and she thought that she coald say that in Warrington and the neighbour- hood, that appeal was never made in vain. She had great pleasure in declaring the sale of work open. Mr. P. EDGAR, in the absence of the churchwardens, proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Lyon for her presence there that day. The motion was seconded unanimously,and the serious business of the day then began.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance yesterday, when the sale of work was again opened, by Mrs. Bearnont. The VWAR in opening the proceedings, said that on Thursday they had received £46 4s. id. ,which was about £5 less than last year. He need hardly say that they would be glad if those present would try to make up for that, not because they were ungrateful for what they had received, but because their needs were the same as last year. Mrs. Beamout had been a very true friend to them during the year, as she had purchased a club room next door and so provided accommodation for nearly 260 children. (Applause.) The children were in conse- quence nottso cramped up in the school as they had been. He hfaped they would be successtul that day, as the captain of the Church Lads' Brigade told him that they would like a good grant that year as they wanted some rifles. . .</p>
        <p>Mrs. BEAMONT, who was warmly applauded, said that it scarcely seemed a year since she opened the sale before. Another year had rolled round and all the necessities were there again. She hoped they would help Mr. and Mrs. Percival to continue their work. She now declared the sale open. (Applause.) Mr. EDGAR proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Bea. mont. He said that Shakespeare asked " What's in a name ? " In his opinion there was a goo I deal in a name, especially in a name like Beamont. Applause. It was a name that was revered in Warrington and the neighbourhood, and Mrs. Beamont had spent a long and useful life. He hoped her life would be prolonged for many more years. (Applause.) The motion was carried with acclamation and the formal proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LATE DR. k'OX OF LYIIII.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. k'OX OF LYIIII.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Dr. John Ma kinson Fox, M.R.C.S. and L.S.A. (England), of The Grove, Lymin,were on Monday afternoon last week, interred in the grave-yard of St. Mary's Parish Church, Lymm. The deceased gentleman, whose age was 63 years, pos- sessed an extensive medical practive in and around Lvinm ; and for a period of 18 years, or more, had held the position of medical officer to the combined sanitary districts of Mid-Cheshire, of wh!ch Lymm formed a part. Prior to holding this appointment he was medical officer for Cockermouth. He was a fellow of the Medical and Meteorological Society and ex-president of the N. W. Association of Medical Officers of Health. Dr. F37C was also the author of several publications on medical and sanitary subjects including Catechism on Vaccina- tion," " Warrant of Scope of Hospitals for Infectious Diseases," " Excrement System in towns and villages," and " Milk and Public Health," etc. He was a Churchman, and had, since his residence in Lymm, regularly attended the St. Mary's Parish Church, of the congregation of which he was one of the most prominent members. In politics he was a Conservative, but never took any part in public meetings of a political nature. He was also an enthusiastic and accomplished musician, and had held for a number of years the post of honorary organist at the Parish Church. This he relinquished but a few years prior to his death. Daring the past few months he had been in failing health, and about seven or eight weeks ago was compelled to cease attending to his professional duties. A little more than a mouth ago he went to Blackpool with the purpose ef finding out whether the change of atmosphere would be beneficial to him, but after remaining there for ten days he returned to Lymm, having derived little or no benefit from the visit. He subsequently became gradually weaker, until at length pneumonia set in, and ternsinated his existence at about 3.30 on the afternoon of Thursday last week,at his own residence. At the funeral the chief mourners were Mrs. J. of. Fox , (widow), Miss Fox, Miss White, the Rev. J. M. Fox (rector of Wingham),Mr. Balance (brother of Mrs. Fox), Drs. Brabazon, Lunt, Vacher, and Reid (the preceding two gentlemen representing the Cheshire County Coun- , cil), Dr. Moreton and Dr. Jovnson. The procession also included Messrs. Gregson, Welford, Brown,Smith, Apple- ton, Gould and Williamson, repsesenting the Lynam Urban District Council ; Mr. McKenzie, Altrincham I Rural District Council ; Messrs. Cowley and Maddocks, Northwich Urban District Council ; Messrs. Speakman and Davenport, Nantwich Rural District Council ; Mr. Perbert Gentry, Nantwich Urban Council ; Mr. Wade, Biddulph Urban Council; Mr F. B. Cooke. and Mr. W. Boosey, Middlewich Urban Council ; the Rev. Charles Clay. Messrs. W. B. Masters, Kendrick. Pme, A. W. Callender, William and Henry Bailey, William Brazen- dale. J Rawson. H. Moss. H. Saville. H. Houghton, F. Druce, I. H. Evans, 11. Burgess, H. Warburton, H Marsh, William Flanbury, &amp;c. The officiating clergymen were the Revs. G. W. Guest, rector of Lymm, and J. T. Collins, curate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PEARSON AND KNOWLES COAL AND IRON COMPANY, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PEARSON AND KNOWLES COAL AND IRON COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The annual report el the Directors of this Company was issued on Tuesday, and is as follow :—" The profits for the year ending June 30 last amount to £18,607, and the balance brought forward from last year is £7.582, together £26,189. The Directors recommend that the preference divieend at the rate of 6 per cont, per annum be paid on the A shares up to December 31, 1894, but the present state of the accounts does not, in their opinion, warrant any further distribution. They also recommend that £5,000 be added to the depreciation fund, and that the balance, £8.563, be carried forward to the next account. In the present balance-sheet an amount of £44,390 hitherto appearing under the head- ing ,r machinery and plant in the books of the colliery department, but which more correctly belongs to stock, has been transferred to the latter account. The capital outlay at the new Cnppull colliery haebeen £4,699. The expenditure at the Ince collieries found to be necessary in order to bring them up to the modern standard with regard to mechanical appliances and general equipment has absorbed £4,319. Tne beneficial results of the vari- ous•• improvements which have been made are already apparent in reduced cost of getting and the more mar- ketable condition of the coal produced. An output of a million tons annually could now readily be attained if demand and prices warranted such a production. In consequence of the great falling off in the export trade, the Directors found it necessary to close the Company's puddling forges at Wigan in November last, and as yet the position and prospects do not justify the re-opening of these works, though it is hoped that the stoppage is only a temporary one. The year covered by the present re ort has been commercially one of the worst of modern times; almost every trade in the kingdom has suffered, and amongst those most affected by the general deprez- sion have been the Lancashire coal and iren trades. The demand has been altogether insufficient to keep either collieries or ironworks fully employed, and prices have been abnormally low. It is hoped that a better prospect lies before us, hut though there are signs of improve- ment in business all rosnd. very little real progress has as yet been made. The coal trade is so heavily weighted by over-production that recovery in. prices must be a slow and gradual movement, and can only follow an improvement in other trades on which .it is dependent. Foreign competition in the Iron te•ide oyes no evldenc) or abatement, and still renders the position doubtful and unsatisfactory."</p>
        <p>TRIED AND PROVED I Sufferers from Gr vel, Liimbuto, Piloe, Puns in the Back, Dropsy. Wind aud Water Complaint. Diseas -8 of Kidneys, Bladder Stone. Se'atica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a po-itive Cure in Holroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices Is. 4d. of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps. from HOLROYD'S Medical Hall, Checkheaten, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor and a box will be lent next post.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S WORKMEN AT WALTON LEA.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S WORK- MEN AT WALTON LEA.</p>
        <p>- ~-,5....,_,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Mn. J. CROSFIELD ON HIS TRIP ROUND THE WORLD.</head>
        <p>Mn. J. CROSFIELD ON HIS TRIP ROUND THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>The workmen employed at the Barak IQuay Soapery, Warrington, with their wives had a most enjoyable outing to Walton Lea, the residence of their employer, Mr. John Crosfield, J.P., on Saturday afternoon. The weather was most propitious, and no fewer than 500 persons responded to Mr. Crosfield's invitation. Omnibuses were provided by Mr. E Connor,Sankey- st., at Mr. Crosfield's expense for conveying the party from Bank Quay to Walton Lea, and on arri- val numerous attractions were provided in the grounds. The conservatories and greenhouses were thrown open for inspection, and the fine display of flowers, fruit,and shrubs, perhaps unequalled in the I district, reflected the utmost credit upon the head gardener,Mr.Kipps. The grounds, most artistically laid out, appeared at their best. Dahlias, asters, geraniums and other flowers were to be seen in full bloom, and the heavily-laden fruit trees were a tempting sight. In the windows of the hall there was an interesting display of various articles,chiefly of Japanese manufacture, which Mr. Crosfield brought back with him from his recent tour round the world. The articles included a beautiful fire screen, with artistically worked-out chrys- anthemums in silk, wickerware, numerous nick-nacks containing some fine examples of hand painting, a fine oicture,and other things. These were explained by Mr. Crosfield to groups of ready listeners. DAncing on the green was indulged in to the strains of the military band of the Bth and 40th regiments, conducted by Drum Major Riches. A number of visitors also engaged lin a game of bowls on the lawn. Shortly before four o'clock, Mr. T. 13 idles, of Sankey-street, photographed the whole company—over 500, whe were arranged near the hall. Tea was after- wards part4ken of in tents which had been erected for the occasion. Mr. John Willett, of Church-strent,catered I for the company, and the extensive arrangements which had been made were carried out without the slightest hitch. There was also a plentiful supply of mineral I waters which were supplied by Mr. Smith of Warring- ton. Quite an army of voluntary waitresses were engaged, and the way they performed their duties gave the utmost satisfaction.</p>
        <p>After tea Mr. -*lig called upon Mr. Crosfield to address the gathering. Mr. JOHN CROSFIELD, who met With a hearty recep- tion, said that in the first place he wished to congratu- late them on the beautiful day they had had for their entertainment. (Hear, hear.) That was the first time since his house was built in 1884 that the workmen and their wives from Bank Quay Soapery had been enter- tained in those gardens. He did not supposed there was anyone present, excepting "Jacky Long," who could remember the works as far back as he could, be- cause he went to them when he was a lad of fifteen, in the year 1847. At that time there was not a brick on the north side of the passage which ran through the works. Well they knew that now the main part of the works was built on the north side of the passage, and of the changes which had taken place since then, it would take a month to record. He could only say that he was delighted to see those present there that day—(hear, hear)—and that he hoped everyone had enjoyed the trip or excursion there that afternoon. He wished to take the opportunity of telling them a little of his trip</p>
        <p>ROUND THE W'RLD. -* He took it for granted that none prlsent had been round the world. His wife and himself left home on the 15th January, and they crossed the continent of Europe from Calais to Brindisi a 48 hours' journey bv rail. They then took steamer to Port Said in Ey,ypt, and proceeded along the Suez Canal to lamella. The Suez Canal he tniyht tell them was inferior to the Manchester Ship Canal. There were, fortunately, no locks on the Suez Canal,hut the hanks were very uninteresting,beiny simply of sand without any trees or anything to attract the attention. From Istnalia they took train to Cairo, and from there on one of the coast steamers, Rameses 11., they pro- ceeded</p>
        <p>VP THE NILE RIVER. The Nile he might tell them was over 3,000 miles long and they went along 600 miles of it. ft was of about the width of the River Mersey at Runcorn. In Egypt, he felt he was on historic ground. One of the places was Luxor, which was beyond the second cataract. There they witnessed the ruins, Karnak, the most won- derful ruins on the fare of the earth, and which saw the dawn of civilisation 6,000 years ago, There was also a temple there, the size of which he could not tell them, but it contained 132 columns, each column 80ft. in height and 36ft. in circumference. The columns were of stone, and were covered with hieroglyphics. It was the language of the people cut deeply into stone, and that writing to day was as perfect as it was 6,000 years ago. Two obelisks were outside this temple, and he supposed they were about oft. in height. Each obelisk was cut out of solid granite, polished, and the hierogly- phics were also as perfect as on the day on which they were cut. Whilst at Luxor they visited the grave of their friend, William Sharpe. The grave yard was about 30 yards by 20 cards, with mud walls, and the ground was the sand of the desert. There was not a flower.and there was not a blade of grass growing. A simple tabl-t was laid on Mr. Sharpe's grave, giving his name, stating that he was born in War- rington, and also the words " Beloved of all men." They afterwards proceeded to Cairo and visited the great pyramid, which was 4001 t. in height and 700 ft. square at the base. The steps up to the pyramid were from three to four feet in height, and his son, Arthur, had the foolishaess to climb up to the top. (Laughter.) He (Mr. Crosfield) had more sense—(laugh- ter)—than risk his bones and body in climbing such place as that. They also saw the sphinx, which the, would all be familiar with in their advertisements on the walls. This enormous figure was cut out of stone. and lay half buried in the sand. There were also tombs built of solid blocks of granite, some being 15ft. long, and 6ft. in height. Hew the people moved these enorm- ous blocks of stone was not known, but they must have had a knowledge of mechanics which their friend, Newall did not know to-day. (Laughter ) They again went down the Sun Canal into the Red Sea. This sea was 200 miles long, and they had a beautiful passage, as fortunately for them, they went at% time of the year.when the heat was not PICIPS4iOP. From the Red Sea, and through the straits of Bab-el-Mandeb,they sailed</p>
        <p>ACROSS THE INDIAN OCEAN. to Bombay. The water of the Indian Olean was as beautiful a blue as a sapphire stone, arid as calm as a ttcluck-pond." They reached Bombay safely. and he hoped he should never reach it Resin. (Laughter.) It had a hateful climate, and he believed not one sitting round the tables could live there for five years. He thought it would finish him off in twelve months. (Laughter.) It was a steaming heat, and was much like going into his hot houses where some of his plants were growine. There was nothing much to be seen except one drive, which was called the Qieert's-road, and then you had to go past the Hindoo Cemetery, wkere they burnt their dead,which emitted a beastly smell. Further on you came to Malabar, and on a ridge of a hill then?, were the Towers of Silence, where the Parsees left their dead to be devoured by vultures. The idea to waiting people was simply horrible. You could see these hideous vultures in hundreds floating about the place, it for the next victim, and he understood that In less than an hour from a body being placed in the temple,. there was not a particle of the kody remaining, except the bones. From here they went to Delhi where they were very interested in seeing the walls of the city, and the breach made into them by that brave and noble Captain Nicholson,who directed the cannon of the English army against this one spot and broke it through. Delhi was taken.but unfortunately the captain had not 2,14f1 200 yards when a stray bullet strhck him, and he died three days afterwards. They saw a place where the then king lived with his two sons, but when they found that Delhi was taken, they went by an underground passage and AA for their lives. But an English officer with 100 cavalry chased them and overtook them. He brought them hack, and as there was an attempt to rescue the king and his sons,the offi- cer deliberately shot the sons publicly, and the king was banished to Burmah. From :here they journeyed to Colombo in</p>
        <p>CEYLON. This was a tropical island full of rich vegetation. Beau- tiful crotons grew in the open air and formed hedges to the gardens, and from the cocoa nut tress in this island they got at their works, as they all knew, cocoa nut oil. Colombo had a horrible climate. It WO the hottest place on the earth, taking the whole year round. It was like being in a steaming bath. You lay down at night with out a sheet upon you, and in flee minutes ynnr pillow was wet with perspiration. They went inland by the railway to Neuralia,where the climate was charming and delightful. But while they had beautiful trees,beautiful flowers, and beautiful plants, they had a curse which they in England might thank God they were free from. They had poisonous snakes which grew to about five feet in length, and the bite of one of them would he the death of the victim in less than a quarter of an hour. In Kandy he made the acquaintance of an English sergeant, who on learning that he came from Warrington, told him that they had a Warrington man in his regiment named S-n-geant Ward. With this Sm.ge.int Ward ha had an hours' con- yam thP following day, about old Warrington. Flis wife was formerly a teacher in Bank Quay Sunday school, end her two sisters were in his (Mr. brnsfield's) srjr.'r4 sewing class at Bank Quay at the present time. It only showed how small the world was. From Ceylon they took steamer for</p>
        <p>HONG KONG calling at Singapore. At Singapore they were within three deerrees of the Equator, a distance of about 180 miles. For five weeks he never had a sheet over him, land he was too hot then. (Laughter.) When they ar- rived at Hong Bong, there was unfortunately a fog, so</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>IN JAPAN.</head>
        <p>IN JAPAN.</p>
        <p>He now came to the most criTe-ra.s.ting part of their trip. Japan was a country that we English people had very little idea of. It was a remarkable nation. We boasted and bragged about England and the English people, and , we had a right to do so for we had done a great deal, no doubt, to civilize the world, but he sould tell them can- didly that England might play second fiddle to Japan. In many respects we were a century behind them. For six weeks they travelled through Japan, and they never saw a woman strike a child ; they never saw a drunken man; they never saw a beggar, nor even an unhappy person. They had no poor houses, and they had a moral law to themselves—it was not the law of any Govern- ment, and consequently stronger than any law made by the Government. It was that every child was bound to maintain its parent. In Japan they were a short-lived race; a man was old there at 45, but you never saw him wanting, because the children of their own accord, voluntarily maintained their parents. It was a splendid trait in their character and he said, all honour to Japan. (Hear, hear.) They wmuld be surprised to bear that there they had no carriages and horses. They travelled in" rickshaws," little two-wheeled carriages about thrce feet from the ground. They were drawn by men who ran in the shafts instead of the horse. It struck him that most Englishmen would abject to that sort of work. But the men in Japan liked it, and would consider it a great deprivation—in fact it would be taking the bread out of their mouths, if you attempted to stop it. In Tokio with a million and a half of inhabitants, covering a space of ground as big as London itself, there were tens of thousands of rickshaws in the place. The men would trot at five miles an hour, and would travel fifty miles a day with a rickshaw. They never ccanplained ; you ever heard them utter an angry word, and when !in the middle of a hot day you asked them to have something to drink—most English men would be most glad to have it—they always refused. They drank their little cups of green tea, and were contented, happy, and pleasant. They went running along, laughing, and jok- ing and talking to each other as if they were having an excursion.Mr.Crosfield then proceeded to give a detailed account of a most enjoyable trip they had in company with an Englishman, from Yokohama round the great mountain Fusi. As it was the first time an English lady had been in that district, Mrs. Crosfield proved a great attraction, and Mr. Crosfield amusingly referred to the JaPanese inquiritiveness respecting her, and her dress. After referring to the most beautiful scenery he saw on this excursion, in alluding to the people, he said they were wonderfully clean in their houses and person. Everyone bathed ; most of them had a hot bath in the morning, and another in the evening. The water was that hot that it would simply scald you if you got into it. They had, however, no regard to what was outside the house. There you found mud and fifth, stenches and stinks which no sanitary officer in Warrington would tolerate. The wonder was that they did not get most fearful diseases. The excursion was one of the grandest he harrrver taken in his life and one he would never for- get. From Yokohama they took steamer for their jeurney ACROSS-MIC PACIFIC. Calling at Honolulu they saw beautiful crotons and other plants living in the open-air, some growing to the height sf five or Rix feet. From here they went on until they reached San Franacio. Proceeding further south they visited the Yosemite valley, the greatest valley on earth. It was 68 miles in length, and cliffs rising to height of 2.000 it. were on either side. There was a fine waterfall here, the volume of water being as great as all the waterfalls in Great Britain put together, and the scenery was something splendid. Referring to the railway journey to Chicago, he commented upon the in- feriority of the American railways,stating that he would not venttirA to e.ompare them with the London and North-Western Railway. The latter appeared to him like travelling in a drawing-room, whilst the American, was more like a hack kitchen. At Chicago), Ibis wife's diamonds were stolen during the night-time by the black Afrenalant, at an hotel. They were taken from his (Mr. Crosfield's) inside waistcoat pocket about five o'clock in the merning whilst he was fast asleep. (Laughter.) He must tell them that at San Francisco and New York the roads were simply abominable. The setts were placed on the hard ground without any proper foundatinn,and you were jogged along the streets until your bones ached. England was paradise when compared with America to travel in. His ennclusions had been that Japan was heaven, and America was the other place. (Laughter.) They went to see the Falls of Niagara,and they certainly were worth seeing. It was something fearful to look at the rapids where the man, Webb,f metiwith his death. They</p>
        <p>CROSSED mg ATLANTIC OCEAN. on the IFtrittanic. and there was fog the whole way. He mentioned two very narrow escapes from collisions dur- ing their passage from Queenstnwn, and coneludedilhv stating that when he landed safely at Liverpool he felt inclined to go down on his knees and thank God that he was on the sacred soil of Britain once more. (Loud WARDS,. ) At this stage of the proceedings an interesting PRESENTATION</p>
        <p>was made. Mr. George Gleave, one of the oldest work- men, stepped forward and asked Mr. Cresfield's acceptance of a silver tea service and tray from his workmen. He coneratulated him upon his anccessful journey round the world, and described him as being R good master to work for. In banding him the present he said it bore the inscription " Presented to John Crns- fi Id. Esq.. by his employees as a token of reaneet and estPPITI, and to commemorate his safe return from his voyage round the world." (Cheers) He then h,nded him the nresetat, which had been most handsomely worked, and sunnlied by Messrs. E. and A. Eustance of Sankev-stteet, Warrington. In responding. Mr. CROSFIELD said he was extremely obliged to Mr. Mears and the workmen for the present of that tea service. He did not anticipate reepivine any- thing ot the sort, and he accepted it with great pleasure. He did so more as a token of sympathy and good feeling than for its intrinsic value. HP had lived amongst the workmen now for 45 years and he had a very high opin- ion of Lsneaahire workmen. Ha had been round the world, and he had not seen any men equal tn Lqncnshire workrnen,without he made the exception of the Japanese. He must make that exeeptinn because they were an example of sobriety, honesty, and contentment, such as the world had never seen anywhere else. To him it was something marvellotss. He could not have believed it, had he net seen it. It was a nation of people perfectly happy, perfectly contented, perfectly sober, and without any poor. it was an old saying in Lancashire, end a true one, that" contentment is a continual feast," and there was another that " cleanliness is next to Godli- ness," and in Lancashire nothing" hanged" it. They, the soaDmakera, came before the parson, and they were Perhaps doing as much for religion P 8 the parson. (Rear, hear.) He again thanked them for the present. His wife told him thst they had not got a silver tray, and therefore it would be valued greatly. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Whan I gays the short condensed account to our workmen and their wives on Saturday afternoon at Walton Lea, I forgot to mention one or two things. At Luxor we crossed the Nile in boats, and .then rode on donkeys fire miler to the tombs of the Kings. Each donkey has its own name. and my animal was called Bismarck. He was a splendid donkey, and carried me like a featherweight. There are live tombs of the Kings, and each one is the size of a railway tunnel, and cut through the solid rock, with a slight gradient,4ooft. kr, the end of the tunnel there is a chamber,3oft. long, 20ft. wide, and 15 feet high. Inside one of these chambers we saw a hugh sarcophagus cut out of solid granite, The sides were quite one font thick, and the lid was 18 inches thick. The sarcophagus was 10ft. lnng.sft.wide. rnd sft. high, and I estimated that the weight of the lid must have been at least three tons. Inside this sar. , cophagus was found the mummy of Rameses In the museum at Cairo we saw this mummy. which was the tyrant Pharaoh, who tyrannized over the Israelites in Egypt. The features of his face were as perfect as those of a living man, and the expression of the mouth and the Chin indicated unmistakeably the character of a tyrant. I suppose that mummy id 6,000 years old. In Cairo we met a Mr. Kavanagh. a distant relation of the Mr. Kavanagh, a member of Parliament, who had neither arms nor legs. Mr. Kavanah was an offiner in the English Army, and 30 veers ago marched a regiment of soldiers 1,200 strong from Suez to Alexandria, when there was no Suez Canal and ns railway in Egypt. He told us that when they reached the Nile, the Colonel of the regiment forbade any man to bathe in the Nile,hut a sergeant named Patrick O'Reilly and another soldier went to bathe in the river. They were found out, and next morning were brought no before the Colonel and the other officers nf the regiment:. The Colonel ad- dressed him thus : " Patrick, for 20 years von have been splendid soldier. You fought for the Queen at Lucknnw and at Delhi, and at Cawnpore. Row dare you disobey the Queen's command when I forbade you to bathe in the Nile ?" Patrick replied : " Bedad, your honour, could net vo hack to my mother in old Ireland vr,thout telling her that I had bathed in the river where Moses was horn !" His reply set the court into laughter, and Patrick and his friend were allowed to go free.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>JOEIN CROSFIELD. Walton Lea, September 24th, 1895.</p>
        <p>WRINGING nuctnruis. irournlnger Warrington.</p>
      </div>
