      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>..•_ _ - .</p>
        <p>TERRIBLE TRAGEDY IN WAR RINGTON.</p>
        <p>A PUBLICAN SHOT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>LODGER ARRESTED ON SUSPICION.</head>
        <p>A LODGER ARRESTED ON SUSPICION.</p>
        <p>At a little after one o'clock, on Tuesday morn- ing, the police who were on duty in the neighbour- hood of Bridge-street, were informed by a man named Cartwright that Mr. William Green,the land- lord of the Shakespeare Inn,Dolman's-lane had shot himself. P.C. Cheney, and P.C.'s Williamson and Cocoran at once hastened to the house while Cartwright went for Dr.Cannell. When the officers arrived at the Shakespeare Inn, they found Green lying in the lobby, suffering from bullet wounds in the head, but he was not dead, nor unconscious, for when they were taking him into the room,he made a statement to the effect that Cartwright who lodged at his house " had done it." Dr. Cannell arriving soon afterwards, he ordered the wounded man to be taken to the Infirmary. At that institu- tion he adhered to his statement, but he died at 1.30 a.m. before a magistrate arrived there to take his dying depositions. Cartwright who is a man 23 years of age and was employed as a com- positor at the " Guardian ' Office, was at once arrested and brought to the police station where he was brought before the magistrates on Tuesday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>'TUESDAY'S POLICE PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>'TUESDAY'S POLICE PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning, the prisoner, Edward John Cartwright, was brought before Mr. R. Garnett, and Mr. J. B. Fleming, at the Warrington Borough Court, charged with being suspected of haying caused the death of William Green, the landlord of the Shakespeare Inn, Dolman's-lane, at a quarter past twelve the same moreing, by shooting him with a revolver. The prisoner pleaded not guilty. The Town Clerk (Mr. J.Lyon Whittle) who prosecuted on behalf of the police, said that the defenzant was charged on suspicion of having caused the death of the landlord of the Shakespeare Inn, by shooting him with a revolver. He was charged on the statement made by the deceased man himself. They had no evidence against him further than that. The police were called in a little after twelve o'clock to: the Shakespeare Inn, when they found the landlord lying in the lobby of the place suffering from bullet wounds from a revolver pro- duced, which contained at the time three discharged cartridges. He was shot through the mouth apparently in three places. The defendant himself was the one 1 who gave information to the police and brought them to the Shakespeare Inn. When the deceased man was seen by the constables he made the following statement, "lam dying. I have been murdered. It was Ted Cartwright who did it." Cartwright was a compositer at the" Guardian "Office. On being asked why he did it, the deceased man said " I don't know. He did it." The man was taken to the Infirmary and died at two o'clock that morning. It was upon that statement, which was a very serious one, that they had ch ,rged the defendant upon suspicion of having done it. Everything pointed to it being a case of suicide, but he asked for a remand pending the result of the coroner's inquiry. The deceased man, the defendant, and a fellow lodger had been out for a walk together in Stockton Heath the pre- vious night. The defendant had been lodging at the house for some months. P.S. Cheney was then called. He said that from a statement made by the prisoner and a fellow lodger,they bad been walking out from eight to ten o'clock. Prisoner had said that he was about to go to bed when he beard the shots. Witness was on duty about one oclock in the morning, when he met the prisoner in Butter- market-street. He told witness that he had better hurry up to the "Bang Up," as the Shakespeare Inn was called, as the" boss "bad shot himself. He asked for a doctor and witness directed him to Dr. Carmen's. Witness then proceeded to the house when he saw Green,the landlord, being taken in the kitchen by a constable and three or four others. Dr. Cannel! arrived soon after and witness asked the doctor's advice as to whether he must take the wounded man to the Infirmary. The doctor said that ,he had better be taken to the Infirmary. Immediately afterwards, the deceased man clasped his hands and said "I am dying. I have been murdered, and Ted Cart- wright has done it." Witness stooped down to him and and asked him why he had done it and he replied" I don't know, but he did it." One wound was on the lip, and there was a graze on the neck. Deceased was then taken to the Infirmary, and witness went in search of the magistrates's clerk and a magistrate, but before one could be got, the man was dead. The revolver produced was lying in the lobby four or five yards from the de- ceased man on the floor. It was a six chambered re- volver with three discharged and three undischarged cartridges.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk : I apply for a remand for a week.</p>
        <p>P.S. Cheney in repl.V- in a question said tht the prisoner walked down with him to the police station and showed no hesitation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Garnett : It was an:extraordinary statement for the landlord to make.</p>
        <p>P.S. Cheney : Ha also made the MIMA declaration in the Infirmary at the last moment, Dr. Bowden tells so.</p>
        <p>Mr Fleming: ft might be a case of murder. _ Mr. _</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk : It is. if the landlord's statement is correct. The statement is admissibly against the defen- dant.</p>
        <p>Mr. Garnett : A man in his dying moment would not make such a statement unless there was something iq it. The Chief Constable (to prisoner). Have you any re- latives in Warrington ? No.—A n pone who knew you before you came:to Warrington? No sir.—flow long have you been in Warrington P—Two years this September— Have you parents living ? A father. The Town Clerk who did not at first nhj.et to bailesaid that he understood that the two men who were probable sureties would now have nothing to do with it. The prisoner was eventually remanded until Friday to await the result of the coroner's inquiry, bail not being granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>WARRINGTON LEAG7ITE:OF THE CROSS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LEAG7ITE:OF THE CROSS.</p>
        <p>HOUSE TO _HOUSE VISITATION.</p>
        <p>The council of the League of the Cross, with the col- lectors went out into the various districts of the town on Sunday last, on their monthly visitation of the mem- bers of the League,with the happy result that a splendid meeting was held in the League Hell on Sunday night. The chair was occupied by the Very Rev. Father Sum. ner, 0.5.8., rector of the Church of St. Marv, Butter- market-mt.. and chaplain of the League. The President, Mr. Mullins, opened the meeting with a few well- chosen congratulatory remarks with reference to the energetic collectors and visitors on the success of their efforts as evidenced by the crowded meeting before them that night. Many old members had been induced to try once more the beneficial practice of total abstin- ence by the kind invitations of the visitors. He urged them to go forward and prosper in the good work of the League. Mr. Mullin. a private member, with a fine tenor voice, afterwards sang two beautiful songs, which were much appreciated by the audience. The meeting was next addressed by Mr. J. C. Dalton, vice-president, expressing a wish that the private mem- bers would not only give them a helping hand,or helping voice, by singing songs on Sunda; but that Fame of the old members who had been listening to temperance speeches for years would help by giving them addresses on Sunday evenings, on the all important question. Be said the League of the Cross was an essentially Catholic organisation. Its duty, its object was to lift them to the highest form of spiritual life, namely the life of a good practical Catholic. The fundamental rule of the League was monthly reception of the Holy Communion. Let them busy themselves in the interests of their neigh- bours, and where they knew of a Catholic who was liv- ing a life which was not consistent with Catholic princi- ples, they must consider it their duty to invite them to join the League of the Cross,which had no traditional,or other pride about it. There was not any smug respect- ability about the Lcague of the Cross. No matter how low a man had sunk in the social scale, he would always find a home and a welcome in the League Hall —The Chairman next addressed the meeting. He endorsed the words which had just been spoken by the vice-President. Total abstinence was not a religion ; with Catholics, it was but a help to religion, and unless a man had the intention, in taking the total abstinence pledge, of serving God with more fervour, and with greater fidelity than before he took this pledge his total abstinence was vain. The Chairman also gave a lengthy and interesting description a the pilgrimage to Lourdes. The meeting closed with the administration of the total abstinence pledge to a great number of men by the Rev. Chaplain. It is stated that nee of the men taking the pledge, has recently been partially cured of locomotor ataxy at Holywell.</p>
        <p>About seven o'clock on Monday night a man entered the shop of Mr. P. G. Powell, jeweller, Market-street, Manchester, and asked to he shown some watch keys. Mrs. Powell produced the Nero, hut the man then dis- covered that he had not brought his watch, and,promis- ing to come again, he left ?he shop. It was afterwards found that he had abstract, d a case containing a num- ber of diamond rings from one of the window cases. Mrs. Powell went out in pursuit of the thief, but failed tn find him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
