      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>"DID SHE ? "</head>
        <p>"DID SHE ? "</p>
        <p>" Yes, she went back home to him from her mother's that very night, for he j ist, sent, her a little paoket 0 tea and wrote on • Come back, Ruth. and I'll stick to this.' And that, bit o' Brooke, Bond's tea-wrapper has looked out of the corner of the old clock's lace ever since, for six een years, reminding 'ern every time they want to see What's o'clock. Yes, Ruth's husband did stick to his promise, and for sixteen years they've b en the h ppiest couple in the county. and every time they see a Broke, Bond packet, they just bless it as the good old peace- maker. Now I don't mind telling you as that young man was me : And Ruth, bless her good heart, has gone round with our two youngest kids to the Agent's tor the usual quarter-pound: This little bit of real life tells its own tale and you will pick up its moral when you buy your next packet o' BROOKII, BONDS'.</p>
        <p>Our Agents are everywhere</p>
        <p>and sell the sort Ruth likes best at Is.-81. a lb., but we have other sorts from Is. to 2s. Bd., and so we suit everybody's purse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>IROOKE, BOND &amp; CO., LTD.</head>
        <p>IROOKE, BOND &amp; CO., LTD.</p>
        <p>THE LANCASHIRE TEA WAREHOUSES 17. PICCADILLY, 29. MARK STREET }MANCHESTER</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF DR. BRAIDE.</p>
        <p>A presentation of a silver tea and coffee service, which had been subscribed for by the members and officials of the Warrington Board of Guardians, was made to Dr. Braide, at the office of Mr. Sutton, Bewsey Chambers,as a token of esteem and respect with which he was held, and wishing him much happiness in his married life. The service was splendidly got up, and was mounted on a stand of polished, oak. and bore the f(Alowim: inscrip- tion :—" Presented to Mr. George Braids by the Guar- dians and officials of the Warrineton Union, on the occa- sion of his marriage, September 26,1895." At the meet- ing there were present besides the recipient of the presentation—Mr. William Houghton (chairman of the Board), Mr. George Pilling (junior vice-chairman), Mrs. Clay, Rev. T. R. Matthews, and Messrs. A. Jolley, W. Hickman, *and A. Long (members of the Board), Mr. S. Sutton (deputy-clerk), Me3srs.T. J. Garnett and F. Auk- land (relieving officers),Mr. B. Shearer (inquiry officer),. Mr. F. Wright (school attendance officer), and Mr. Gib- sem (a former member of the Board). On the motion of Mr. SUTTON. seconded by Mr. WlLLtam HOUGHTON, Mr. George Filling was elec,ed to the chair, and the Chairman expressed his pleasure at the occasion on which they had met. He had great plea- sure in calling unon Mr. William Houghton to make the presentation to Dr. Braide.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON wished to express hie gratification at being akked to perform the pleasing duty of handing over the present to Dr. Braide. He supposed he was selected because he was Chairman of the Board. He would add his testimony to the esteem and regard with which their medical officer was held by the Board. (Hear,hear.) In doing so he could only express what he knew was the unanimous feeling of the subscribers to the present,and that was that he, and the estimable lady who was about to become his wife would live long toge- ther to enjoy them, and also that they would have some enq to hand them down to. The committee who had charge of the purchase had made a very good choice in providing something of a useful and substantial charac- ter. He asked Dr. Braide to accept the present with the best wishes of all the subscribers. (Applause.) Dr. BRAME, in reply, hoped they would excuse him if he did not say very much. The present had come on him as a surpri-e, although he had known something about it for the last few days. He could only say that he appreciated their present, not so much for its value, but, as Mr. Houghton had said, for the good feeling shown. (Applause.) Mr. MATTHEWS said that he was only a very junior member, but he was very much pleased to see such • manifestation of good feeling towards the medical officer. He had not had the pleasure of his acquaintance longs, but he hoped they would get to know each other better as time went on. He had great pleasure in joiuing in the good wishes to Dr.Braide and his future wife. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN joined in the general good wishes, and hoped that the union which was about to take place would ben? the right sort.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. JOLLEY. Mr. T. J. Garnett, Mr. Long, and Mr. Shearer, also spoke in felicitous terms of Dr. Braide. Mr. SmatouSurrox Said that it was his pleasing duty to propose n vote of thanks to Mr. George Pilling and Mr. T. J. Garnett, for the energy they had shown and exerted in collecting the subscriptions and in the buy- ing of that most valuable, and at the same time, most useful present. Personally he wished Dr. Braide and the future Mrs. Braids much happiness. Lie was glad that every Guardian and he believed every male official had subscribed to the present. (Applause.) He had not heard of a single refusal. If Dr. Braide was net happy now —he knew he would he the next day—(laughter)—he ought to be. (Applause.) Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON seconded the vote of thanks, and Mr. 1 illing and Mr. Garnett having replied,in which they bore testi nony of the readiness with which the pre- sent had been subscribed, the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Week ending 14th September, 1895. During the week, which was the 37th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths Warringtnn 3B 40 Latchford 6 2</p>
        <p>Total 44 42 The excess of births over death. was 2. The death rate from all causes was equal to 38 2 per 1,000, against 27 9 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 22.3 the corresponding week of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to 18.2 per 1,000, against 11.3 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 3 7 the corresponding week of 1894. The ages of mortality were- 0— 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 18 12 1 5 6 3 42</p>
        <p>42 The deaths included-10 from measles, 10 from diarrhea, 1 from acute rheumatism, 3 from cancer, 1 from pi..thisis. 3 from old age. 4 from brain disease, 2 from lung disease, 1 from heart disra.e. The following cases of infectious disease have hen reported during the week :—S.;arlet fever, 6 Cases. enteric 1 case.</p>
        <p>Total number of patients in hospital on Sept. 14-35. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 59.5 Fah.,. and therefore about the same aa the preceding week. The maximum temperature 82 F., was recorded on the 10th inst., and the rnimimum temperature 42F., on the Bth inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall meaeured 1.5 inch. The aeneral direc tin n was S.W., the reading of the Anemometer denoting, 1850 miles.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL. MPdical Officer of Health.. • WARRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL CANDIDATES.</p>
        <p>The class it of students in training colleges. exa- mined for certificstes July,lB9s.bave been iastzed Ncte: The nsmes in the first and second divisions are placed it' the order of merit. A letter e denotes equal merit :</p>
