      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY SEPTEMBER 28, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY SEPTEMBER 28, 1895.</p>
        <p>SAVE 25 PER CENT. FURNISH AT WORSLEY'S, •• THE PANTHEON. GAYTHORN, MANCHrSTER. ESTABLISHED 54 YEARS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SHOWROOMS COVERING NEARLY ONE MILLION</head>
        <p>SHOWROOMS COVERING NEARLY ONE MILLION</p>
        <p>SQUARB FIST.</p>
        <p>THE MOST COLOSSAL FURNISHING ESTABLISHMENT</p>
        <p>IN Taii-NORTU. SPECIALITR: HIGH CLASS SHOOND•RAND FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>puliWiriniE-cir JAMES LAMB, HENRY OGDEN and SONS, GOODALLS, GILLOWS of Lancaster, GARNETTS of Warrington, CUTTER of Liverpool, SHOOLBRED of London, STARKEY &amp; MELD of Altrincham, CHAMBERLAIN of Birmingham, and other High-cisss Houses. TERMS CASH OR DEFERRED PAYMENTS. All goods are advertiSecl at cutting prices, being in most cases 50 percent. below ordinary city prices for cash. If by deferred payments, no differ, nee is made in the invoice price. but a aligheper centage is charged according to the length of credit desired. As compared with the hire system this saves from 10) to 2.0 percent.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY CHAT.—ShOOND-HAND FURNITURE. 11 Benjamin Franklin said a penny saved is a enny made. This is an obvious truism, and ought to be borne in mind by all, rich and poor. But at "The Pantheon" it is not only a question of saving pennies, but of pounds. House Furnishing is one of the important epochs in every family, well deserving careful thought. If you can economise with- out detriment to qu ility and stability, then by all means do so, and you cannot practice this theory better than by paying a visit to '' The Pantheon," which will cost nothing, but profit you much. One of the most interesting sights in toe Fur- nishing World is "The Pantheiin," a busy hive of industry, containing without tear of contradiction, th ee to four times the stock of any two of the largest furnishing houses in Man- chester. The bulk of this stock is High-class Second-hand Furniture by high-class makers, at quarter the price of the poor miserable, veneered orange box furniture from the East End of London, now so largely retailed by drapers. Beware ot purchasing London turniture ; it lacks stability and finish. A Good Second-land Article is worth a hundred of spurious imitations of furniture made in East London. Worsley's can supply you with all you require in furnishing, by such makers as Lambs. Gillows, or Garnet, at prices simply pul- verising competition. For instance, as a simple bargain you can obtain a Fine Sideboard by Povestons which cost tB5, for £l6 ; what chance has the competition of new furniture against bargains like these? If you go to an auction room and buy you take the risk yourself as to whether your pur- chases are sound and durable, but at Worsley's you have no risk, Worsley's absolutely taking all risk, guarantee every article for ten years. and will take any article or articles back not giving satisfaction. It will cost you nothing to call at " The Pantheon,"and see for yourselves ; you can inspect without feeling compelled to purchase; they will be delighted to show you round whether you buy or not. Under these , circumstances you will be guilty of criminal, wilful neglig. *me of your own interests if you neglect visiting Worsiey's before deciding where tic Furnish.</p>
        <p>ONLY ONH W OISSLEF'S IN THE WORLD. GREAT BARGAINS IN COMPLETE HOUSES OF FURNIIURE.</p>
        <p>THE GIGIATRST BARGAINS IN TRH WORLD. --</p>
        <p>COMPLETE ROUSES OF FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>Lot 1. Splendid House of urniture, complete, the most substantial lot in this city, and containing more for the money. The Dining-room has a heavy Dining-room Suite, complete, in hard grain, Levant leather, the frames beauti- fully carved in has relief; has comfortable couch and luxuri- ously upholstered easy chairs, a heavy dining table to n etch. extends to dine 10, with incised early English legs on patent castors; a handsome Persian bordered carpet and rug to match, solid brass bar fender,flre brasses and dogs complete; cornice, with rings, ends and brackets ; Notting am lace curtains ; and last , wiaitth is thrown in gratis and is in addi- tion to what any competitor could do, simply for an adver- tisement, a handsome American walnut sideboard, in the Renaissance style, to match the dining-room suite. The Bedroom Suite is solid Cyprus wood and is a suite which can be highly recommended, guaranteed for 10 years, being charged in some establishments as much as we ask for the whole of this house of furniture; a handsome solid brass and (wart elled bedstead, with complete flttiogs of mattress and bedding; there are the usual accessories for bedroom—carpets, curtains, fender, chamber ware, Ac. Also Kitchen Furni- ture, consisting of the usual chairs, heavy white top table, fender, ashnan, and top bar, floorcloth for kitchen, landing, and hall. I will pack and deliver the lot for 211 guineas, 10 per cent, extra if by deferred payments. Call and inspect this lot, it pulverises all competition ; if you approve buy, if you don't there is no harm done.—WORSLET 'S.</p>
        <p>Lot 2. A Splendid House of Furniture for £4O, solid oak and walnut, heavy massive furniture throughout, in capital condition, worth £BO, dining-room, two bedrooms, kitchen. ball stairs, and landing ; a great bargain. Lot 3. A workingman's House of Furniture for £l5; a splendid bargain. There is better stronger, and more furni- ture in this lot than many competitors offer for £3O. Lot 4. The best House of Furniture for £BO ever offered. Same quantity as Lot 2, but higher class, except that we are prepared, as an advertisement, to present a pianoforte with this lot or its value. This offer holds good for 14 days only. Lot 5. And last, but not least, we have a magnificent House of Furniture, deposited by the executors of a deceased gentleman. of Alderiey, with us for immediate disposal. Price for the lot, £200.-, Contains superb furniture for draw- ing, dlning. sitting,- 'and three bedrooms; kitchen, hall, stairs, landing ; besides sundries ; honestly worth £SCO. Full and complele particulars on application.</p>
        <p>GREAT BARGAINS IN DINING-ROOM FURNITURE LOT 113. A remarkably handsolne carved oak Dining- room Suite, in morocco, hair stuffed, bold, massive, an haadsorne ; couch, two emus, six chairs, six-feet sideboard, eight-feet dining table, fine bold overmantel, coal cal.inet, the lot complete for £45; worth £2OO. —WORSLEY'S. LOT 212. A capital solid American Walnut Dining-room Suite, in best English Morocco. hair stuffed throughout, the framework is h. avy, magniticently carved in ban relief, consists of broad cornfortab:e couch, pair of luxuriously up- holstered easy chairs, a set of six high-padded back standard single chairs, all upholstered in a rich wine-coloured Turkey grain Morocco, beautifully banded and studded, cost £7O; price £l5 155., guaranteed for 10 years. The sideboard is of mob e proportions, of modern design, well equipped with every mo fern convenience, early English design, fitted with broad deeply-cut bevelled edge mirrors of great lustre and brilliancy, cost £65, for 16 guineas. A massive handsome Dlnlng Table, £3 10s. , cost £l2 10s., and Overmantel, which matches the sideboard, for £5 10s„ worth £25. All the above in splendid conditi m.—WORSLIIrS.</p>
        <p>Lot 251. A heavy handsomely-carved Dining Room Suite In Oriental saddle bags, a full suite of nine pieces, kgether with magnificent couch and easy chairs ; honestly worth £45, for 14 guineas.—WoltSLßY'S. Lot 261. Excellent Spanish Mahogany and Morocco Dining Room Suite, nine pieces complete; cost LF:O. for 8 guineas.— WORSIAY.S.</p>
        <p>Over 200 Diaing-room Suites on view, from £2 to £500; over 200 Sideboards, from 355. to 100. guineas ; over 300 Dining Tables, from 15s. to £2O ; over 400 Overmantels, ii slispes, styles, and sizes, from Ss. 6d. to £3O.—WORSLEY'B. inspection invited.</p>
        <p>..</p>
