        <p>MUSICAL CHAT.—The approach of the winter season is now causing flutters of excitement amongst young and old as to arrangements for musical enjoyment. the selection of a good musical instalment, in 99 cases out of a hundred, is a Pianoforte, and no more important purchase could be made. To possess an Interior instrument Is to harbour a constant pest to yourself and a nuisance to everyone else ; to buy a good piano( arta is to make a good bid for personal comfort and enjo) ment, and a constant source of pleasure to all your friends. A word of advice on the selection of a Pianoforte may be acceptable; if so, then unless you can go to a high price, don't eurchase a new instrument, especially a cheap German. Going to o high price means purchasing a first-class piano by it first-class maker such as Brard, Steinway, Bro wood. Colla-d, Bechetein, Ibach, 31insmead, and Kirkman. Worsley's, of Gaythorn, can remedy both the high price and the miseral Is, clamp new rubbish,by submitting for salelifigh- class Srcond-hand Pianofortes, by the above makers, at even.. less cost than many inferior cheap Germans. livery Piano forte is warranted, and exchanged if not approved. Inspec. lion invited; please yourself whether you buy or not. Es. tablished 1841. Worsley's save 5 ) per cent in Pianos. • Lot 131. Magnificent Rud Ibach. powerful; cost £BO, for £29. Lot 135. Splendid Broadwood, nearly new; cost £75, for 19 guineas. Lot 164. Really fine Collard and Collard Piano; cost ti.CO, for £25. Cheep. Lot 181. A Grand Cottage Piano, by 13rinsmead ; cost £95, for £lB. Lot 192. Magnificent Bbonised Piano, by Jauko ; cost £250, for £3O. Lot 220. A Handsome Rosewood Cottage, by Chappel ; for £l2, cost £5O. Lot 223. The Handsomest arul Best Pianoforte in Man- chester for £2B, being worth from £7O to £80; it is by Brant, the noted maker, almost new, in splendid condition, full compass, 7 octaves, iron frame, with latest improvements. Guaranteed. Only requires inspection to effect a sal