        <p>THE curates of the Church of Eng- The Revolt et the Curates land should bo if not a highly paid . _ _</p>
        <p>at least a well pad body of men. IlleY have been accurately described as the 'Working clergy," and when it is remembered that the emoluments of the bishops reach into thonsands and that the bulk of the upper clergy Itre in receipt of substantial stipends it seems only fair and just that the men who do the most work Should be adequately remunerated. It is true, of 'course, that the Church of England has been de- 40d as our last bulwark against Christianity, but that definition was not framed by those who have authority in the Church. They will no doubt maintain that the Church of England is organised 61' the promotion of Christianity by precept and !Itainple. They will, therefore, be distressed to 411 d that it is the openly expressed opinion of many cnrates that the huge income of the Church is not fSirly distributed—that, in short, those who the most work get the least pay. .strongly have some curates been moved by their grievances that they have actually t4ttftpted to form a Curates Union—a sort of .rade union for the protection of their own Interests. The attempt that was made in London on Tuesday to inaugurate the Union was hot successful chiefly owing to the fact that there Wo la present at the meeting an organised band of :°llenta. But enough was said and done to c'w that there is a considerable number of i_tlrates in the Church of England who are smart- t_ug under what they consider to be very unfair rea ttaeub, and the movement that has been corn- lenced „ will certainly not be stamped out by 7"at was nothing More nor less than the rowdy- lafl of a temporary majority.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. 11. Riddette opened an interesting dis- 'ept"i(nsi at the last meeting of the Warrington Th°4ll of Guardians when he introduced :" subject of boarding out the children I:iktead of keeping them in the Padgate 018- Ile spoke strongly in favour of the board- °Ut system, characterising the present system bad,' and he made a very strong case. There d 4 1:13 doubt that the practice of putting the chil- -./1 in private homes to be brought up as foster rldren in the same manner as other children is i!fitr the best one which could be adopted where practicable. Rut some members of the Board ere doubtful whether the system could be worked ...the Warrington Union. Another difficulty which 112,71 Pointed out by the Clerk is that only orphans deserted children could be boarded out. The °,,t4er children,whose parents are in the Workhouse, Who are in the schools owing to their parents' th ,unee could not be treated in that way, for e ei 110 a. ratite reason that their parents might be in a timitiOn any time to claim them again. At the same e,s—e, as Mr. Riddette showed,these difficulties have tilTitrently been overcome in some other Unions,aiod ThePrePosal is certainly worthy of consideration. opt Board of Guardians declined to fe;tra in favour of the boarding out, not from any 4:1111? of opposition to the principle, but because were not fully acquainted with the subject.</p>
        <p>--- I.B°lne delegates, representing both employers and icpVe rkmen, of the British Iron Trades Association reCently been visiting some of the principal `onworks in Germany and Belgium and the re- Porte eY have brought back are exciting wide- 41,fread attention. And no wonder, for the reports tou.nter co all the pre-conceived notions upon toaietho 1c 8 lelrbtje,Ct of foreign competition in the iron trade. When the foreign manufactdrers gained tory advantage over their English competi- th,:t to has generally been taken for granted e t Englishmen were placed at a disadvan- toll° hY reason of the higher wages they had th 134Y* 131lt, according to these latest reports, 11:" i 8 not the case. The English ironworker will bLtanrPrised to learn that the German is actually ter Paid than he is. A Darlingt-on man who has Working for some years in a Germsn iron- work 4 8 made the following emphatic statement. ; undoubtedly our men are better off than in Eng- ° 1"111:;, We Pay, generally speaking, higher wages. :ave Some few men who get higher wages 111:' anY men in our works, but over the whole of '4 --11 we nay higher wages than you pay. That 1 ali absolute fact." The figures that are given are to bear out this statement. It is even alleged gat the Germans not only get more money, but .41,81) Work shorter hours, have much better and s'eaPer houses to live in with large gardens tit!. -aChed, and are provided with pensions in sick- and old age. These are certainly very remark- assertions and no doubt more will be heard of • GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>BOARD MEETING.—The monthly mg of the tieti Opt h. :21 Board was held on Tuesdayeetin evening len:, there were present the Rev. E. S. Jack- Gent Mr. Joseph Wright, Mr. Henry ei Bate, JuMnr. •Henry J (tie,P Clarke (head master), and Mr. W. D. Jeans, '4)• Mr. 11 Rate (vice-chairman) presided.' The %the reported that there was a balance of £ll 3s. sd. 44, hands of the treasurer. £4O would shortly be re- 'll p._ecl itum the overseers, and there were other sources 134-irePti so that they would have sufficient plopeyto Clerk tiot, Irt Month's bills. The C said he bad received a InNet•n°ll.l Mr. Phelps intimatinlgthatthe examination la- meAdli...-4 Would take place on the 10th October at 9.30 a . m. tet,e;,...aster's report: Mr. Clarke presented the following 31st, on books 386, present 340, aver. iNts 3. u ; money received 14s. September 7th, on `l4•4ri ', Present 339, average 314 9; money received 8 4'etag'. Pternber 14th, on books 384, present 334; °4 thei,ns 4 • money received 123. Bd. September 21st, ,reteiN:ki°,}cs 353, present 330; average 269 2 . money _ 133 1 "etkia:" 'O4. . Total money received, R 2 The at- t4l4 ower last wee 44 wa I k owing to the in crease in gips a a 8 the younger chil- t naea I especially "long through 4 , Th s es, scarlet nIPC* •rkere was one family absent tiirt.hi-at-te"beAClerk made an explanation with reference uhe "ahee not living so good gustuoatlh.e povertyl t was, \t'd hi4l Tltit had explained to himowaiso .3(retube4ketet°l2,t Sankey Bridges. There were somescohmile- hiete,hl haa jort)otri no legal p could be taken. *they ``)re. attend° I that %en!) `would sb school.--he Headmaster reporter ufficient clothes to wear,and could not, theyt'l, beenn9(3rtly have to re-arrange ge the staff of the iwould b_o under the requHireemanents of the new code utelinlin:ruvn.dietrstaffled at the beginwnZ a the d have to be taken into con i yienaern.tioned the matters- HOIEING I/ -3.310 T NIGgEN'S ON WORK Li</p>
        <p>SOCIETY. hi --- 'Soti„,9I3EN RICE FROM SWINDON. _ Wn. . Oil ,'"Y held its first annual open race frofromScol ieB) on Saturday August 3151, 73 birds m. ,4 r),., ° birds were liberated by Mr. George Runt, ee-rii at 11e . a.m. a strong N W wind blowing. AP4I4 6 p race 20 nsued, several birds being wired in k.N.I.w ery fe_ rh w minutes of each other. The winner, ilAiat., -urton of Mill- ' lane Warrington, may be con- -141s;i41111)Oh his success as he is a comparative nov- -111968 41; Be Success hon.secretary iie Ist. prize is a silver cup subscribed for .• Dlb4l;se 4nd friends, together with 40 per cent, of sq,tth io'n ; the second 20 per cent. • 3rd 15 per iSilit . six Pert the sth 7t per coa ziTworil...,: oirn 6t,h'7l per cent. also receive a club's diploma each. :1;14*-ItiohR'cl'at8.n.. 9,50 B Widnall 885 T Miller 783 I WeishY 877Healey 733 R Clare 873 5 4 4,x,littil ... 42 . j wNv e'by 726 W'dcoes 726 7811'4' "••:. 'B9. B Wid•na" 8724 "do 708 912 F4,1131x,,,,°ad 88525 900 J Marl °° 812 hl44m-:•r, ly,:, ere disqualified or wurton . seen:: birrd)natdC°3lll. 'o4thua mtCh • J.ripi'hated .1.( Timperley's loft of Stockton i pßluyringegssw,iEth. t''' -Icier nominated I'. L.sters' loft of Stockton</p>
