      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A FORMER RUNCORN CONTRACTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Weston, who for a number of years was at Runcorn, has been successful in securing an important contract on Cae Thames for the construction of a large wharf which will enclose a considerable area of land,the property of Messrs. Brassey Bidder, and Wyther, and known as the North Woolwich Land Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO A CHILD.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A CHILD.</p>
        <p>A child, four years of age, named Mary Eva Done, daughter of Mrs. Margaret Done, of 21, Comper-street, Runcorn, met with a serious accident on Wednesday morning. It appears that she was playing in an old boat lying on the banks of the Ship Canal, near the ferry whim she fell out, and on being picked up it was found that she had been iNjured. She was taken home. and Dr. McLennan was called in, and on examination, he found that her thigh had been broken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RESCUE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RESCUE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Nally Brown, aged four years, daughter of Mr. Peter Brown, of the Rutter Vaults, Brunswick-st., Runcorn, had a narrow escape from drowning in the Bridgewater Canal, on Friday. The little one went to meet her father who is engaged at the bridge being constructed at Halton Crane. Getting too near the side,she fell in. Her father heard the splash, and immediately jumped into the water. Be was succQssful in rescuing the child from the water, who was little the worse tor the immersion.</p>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF AN OLD RUNCORN RESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. Joseph Hewitt, of Low- lands-road, Runcorn,whose death at the aee of 76 years, oocurred on Wednesday last week, were interred in the burial ground attached to the Parish Church on Satur- day afternoon. The deceased was born at Warrington, but when a youth he came to Runcorn,where he resided until his death. For a period of 30 years he was en- gaged at the Runcorn Soapery, and afterwards he was employed at the works of Messrs. M tthieson's,at Widnes, where he remained until about twelve months prior to his death, when ill-health compelled him to relinquish his duties. He was a member of a Warrington branch of Oddfellows. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of this Council was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Wed- nesday morning. Mr. J. Pritchad d was voted to the chair. There were also present Rev. Dr. Gastaldi,Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., E. Imison, J.P.: G. Hewitt, D. Bisbrown, M. Sweetman, G. H. Kirkham, J. Jones, J. Wilson and Mr. Lake (law clerk), Mr. Marshall (secre- tary), and Mr. M. Barker (surveyor). The business was purely formal, being for the purpose of thawing cheques on the treasurer for interest on water mortgages due on the 30th inst.—The Secretary explained that it was for the half-year's interest on mortgages taken up when the water works were purchased, amounting to £1,083 Vs. 61, and it was necessary to pass the cheques now, as the next Council meeting would be held too late to do so.—Mr. Taylor moved the passing of the recom- mendation that the cheques be drawn on the treasurer. —Mr. D. Bisbrown seconded, and it was agreed to.</p>
        <p>El ARVEST TUANXSGIVING SERVICES AT ST. PAUL'S, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The first harvest thanskgiving services in connection with St. Paul's Weskyan Church, Runcorn, took place on Sunday, and were attended by large congregations. The Rev. C. de Putron Gliddon was the preacher at the morning service, and the Rev. W. George occupied the pulpit in the evening. The decorations were on a lavish scale and were much admired. The choir under the con- ductorship of Mr. Morris, sang special hymns and anthems. Mr. Grice was the organist.—On Monday evening a service of praise was held, and was well at- tended. The Rev. T. Eiraclcenbury delivered a delight- ful address on his holiday in Ireland. Several anthems were sung by the choir, and a duet "0, lovely peace," was contributed by Mi. sea Jones and Birkett. Mr. Mus- grove presided at the organ, and played several volun- taries. The service was also takes part in by the Rev. W. George, and the Rev. C. de P. Gliddon.</p>
        <p>BRUNS WIC/C MUTUAL IMPROVEMSNT CLASS.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of thesession in connection with this class was held on Wednesday evening when a fruit banquet was held. The proceedings were held in the large hall, Brunswick-street, and the room had been most tastefully decorated for the occasion by Mr. George Hayes. Mr. T. Sirnister presided, and delivered a brief address in which he expressed a hope that the ensuing session would be as successful as previous ones. Short addresses were also given by the Revs. T. BrackenburY, W. George, and C. De P. Gliddon. The musical portion of the programme consisted of songs by Captain Le Couteur, Mr. Morris, and Mr. Birkett. Duetts were pleasing rendered by Miss Taylor and Miss Simkin, and a glee party consisting of Miss Jones, Miss Birkett. Mr. John Birkett, and Mr. Morris contributed a couple of glees in an efficient manner. The accompanist during the evening were Miss Wright and Miss Taylor. Fruit and light refreshments were handed round at intervals. An enjoyable evening's entertainments concluded with a number of parlour games.—As will be seen from an ad- vertisement in another column, a lecture, under the aus- pices of the class, will be delivered by the Rev. C. De P. Gliddon on Wednesday evening next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SONS OF TEMPERANCS.—RIINCORN Basxes.</head>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCS.—RIINCORN Basxes.</p>
        <p>- Mr. John Jones presided at the quarterly meeting of the above named seciety on Saturday. Mr. Thomas Joseph Holt, one of the sick stewards, gave his report,in , which he referred to the amounts paid to sick members being £43 55., against £44 16s. jet the corresponding quarter last year, or during the last six months £lO2 11g. ,thus showing the Incalculable geod these sick bene- fit societies are doing to their members. A letter was read by the secretary, received from Mrs. F. J. Norman, on behalf of the nurse tund. It was decided to send a subscription of one guinea, a number of members ex- pressing their appreciation of the work done by the nurse. After the routine business had been transacted a valuable paper was read by Mr. William Cooke on "Sound." The paper was greatly appreciated. The following officers and members took part in the discus- sion :—Messrs. D. Smith, Edward Key, Thomas J. Holt, S. M. Temperley, Thomas Cooke, and Daniel Bisbrown. A cordial vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Cooke.</p>
        <p>RI7NCORN BOARD SCHOOL.--APPOINTMENT OF TEACIIRRS.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall presided at a special meeting of the School Board on Friday evening. There were also pre- sent :—Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, Mr. E. Imison, Rey. Dr. Gastaldi, and Mr. J. Moss (clerk). The appointment of assistant teachers for the Greenway- road and Brunswick-strret schools was the business of the meeting. A number of candidates were in attend- ance, and after their credentials had been examined the following appointments were made : Miss Naylor. of Moseley ; and Miss Bertha Hill, of Runcorn, to the Greenway-road Bus's' School. at salaries of £5O per annum each ; also Minl4 Clara Edwards,of Runcorn.to the Brunswick-street Boys' School, at a salary of £6O per annum. The chairman remarked that the appointment of female teachers to the boys' school was a new depar- ture, but the Board was only adopting the method which was in vogue in the best schools throughout the country, was the sanction of the Education Department. Boys I came from the infants schools at an early age, and it I with considered wise to continue female influence ever them for a year or two in the boys' schools. He quite agreed with the course that had been adopted, and felt sure that the parents of the children would do the same. This concluded the business. BENEFIT CONCERT AT RUNCORN. The Public Hall at Runcorn was crowded to excess on Wednesday evening when a grand concert, the proceeds of which were for the benefit of Mr. James Harrison, of Runcorn, was given by the 2nd Earl of Chester's Volun- teer Band, assisted by local artistes. The chair was occupied by Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., C C., and at the com- mencement of the proceedings he said he was very pleased to accept the invitation to be Chairman,not only because he felti his duties would be light, but chiefly I because he knew the object for which the concert was being got up was for a most worthy cause. The pro- ceeds were for the help of a young man who unfortu- nately met with an accident some four years ago, and who had been unable to follow his employment. His parents were but working people, and were not able to do very much for him. A number of friends were trying to help him and it was most worthy on their part to do so. They all knew the old saying "That many can help one,but one cannot help many." It was most gratifying to him to see such a large attendance with the first object of helping such a cause. The Pro- gramme opened with an overture " La Rouchd'Or "by the Volunteer band under the conductorship of Sergeant Outhwaite, and during the evening they interspersed two other very agreeable items, the selections from Beline's operas being especially well played. Miss Heatherington, Mr. C. Leathwond, Mr. Edward Wor- rall, Mr. Gowanlock, and Mr. W. Skillicorn were the seloists,and all sang in ' such !Toad style as to secure hearty receptions. " Home Deane, Hems," by Miss , Beatherington was in every way a success, and comply- I ing with a recall she sang " Come lasses and lads." She was equally successful in her next song " Island rof Dreams." In this, Miss Flaatheringtnn was again encored and responded with " Home sweet Home." in such a a manner as to still further please the audience. Mr. Leathwood's first contribution, was " The Wreck," but he was more successful at a later stage when he sang in company with his wife, in the duet "The Singing Lesson." In response to vociferous minimise, the latter portion cif the duet was repeated. Mr. Worrall's two songs were "The Star of Bethlehem," and " When other Lips" and he sang both with musicianly taste and feeling. " Alice, were art thou ? " and " Pilgrims of Love" were well suited to Mr. Skilltcorn's style, and the same remark applies to" The Lighthouse," sung lay Mr. Gowanlock. In the duet " Call to Arms," Messrs. Parry and Shingler, were deservedly encored, and sung in response, "Allis Well." Mr. David Billington did his hest to infuse humour into the entertainment, by humorous songs and antics. The duties of accompanist were efficiently fulfilled by Mr. C. C. Le Coutenr. At the close, a let er was read from Mr. EIMISOR by Mr. Yates, who had acted as secretary, in which he desired to thank all those who had sn willingly and generously given their assistance in promoting the success of the concert. He especially wished to thank Mr. Byfield for inaugurating the concert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURI)AY, SEPTEMBER 28,1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURI)AY, SEPTEMBER 28,1895.</p>
        <p>A NUISANCE AT TOP Locks.</p>
        <p>During the Zak a memorial nu—m-Wor-u-sly signed by residents at Top Locks has been forwarded to the Run- corn District Council, praying for the abatement of a nuisance caused by the night-soil boat of the Council on the Bridgewater Canal at Top Locks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHETRCH.</head>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHETRCH.</p>
        <p>In this -Church on Sunday next, the harvest thanks- giving services will be held. The church has been nicely decorated for the occasion, and there will be Special musical attractions. The preacher will be the Rev. J. Simpson (pastor) and he has chosen for his even- ing subject, "The Basket of Fruit." FORTHCOMING VISIT OF MR. W.R. JUDE. _ . . _</p>
        <p>This well-known musician will visit Runcorn on Tues- day evening next when he will give a musical evening, entitled "'Fhb Music of Humanity," in the Public Hall. The proceeds are to be devoted to the St. John's Presby- terian Church Building Fund, and from the programme which has been circulated, there is every prospect of an interesting and instructive evenings entertainment. Further particulars will be found in our ad ertising columns. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>FORMATION OF SHIP CANAL PILOTS ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>FORMATION OF SHIP CANAL PILOTS ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of pilots and others interested, was held in Manchester on Monday, for the purpose of taking preli• minary steps for the formation of a pilots association for the Manchester Ship Canal. A committee consisting of the following was appointed : Mr. Marshall Stevens (ex officio chairman), the five superintendents on the Canal (including Captain Beesley of the Runcorn section), four Liverpool pilots. Captain H. Hill, of Surrey-street, Runcorn, represents the Runcorn pilots. This committee will hold examinations and issue licenses during the next month.</p>
        <p>HARVEST THANKSGIVING SERVICES AT ST. MICHAEL'S. The annual harvest thanksgiving services in connec- tion with St. Michael's Church, Runcorn, commenced on Thursday evening with a festal evensong. There was a lurge congregation. The preacher for the occasion was the Rev. W. 0. Hughes, M.A.. rector cf Tarporley, who delivered an appropriate sermon. The sacred edifice had been most artistically decorated, and lending itself, as it does, to such treatment, it presented a most beautiful appearance.</p>
        <p>THE TECHICA r.</p>
        <p>Since the opening of this Institute on Monday, the number of students who have joined tke various classes has exceeded the anticipations of the committee. For the dressmaking class, conducted by Mrs. Dobson, and the cookery class by Miss Head, there was such a large number of entries for the classes,that it was found neces- sary to form two separate classes for each subject. Classes have been formed in almost all the subjects con- templated,and there is every promise of a successful session. Intending students should give their names in at once to the secretary (Mr. Marshall).</p>
        <p>GIFT OF A MODEL TO THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. Mr. Joseph J. Speakman, of Scanley Villas, has pre- sented to the Technical Instruction Committee a finely executed model of the as. City of Paris. It has been placed in one of the rooms of the Institute, and a plate is to be affixed bearing the name of the donor. A letter of thanks has been torwarded Mr. Speakman for his present. BOWLING TOURNAMENT.</p>
        <p>At the Blackpool Bowling Tcurament held on Saturday at the Talbot Hotel Green, T. Woods of Runcorn beat T. Hogarth of Blackpool, and W. Wright of Runcorn was beaten by J. Winstanley, of Blackpool. The first round was concluded on Tuesday. and among the successful competitors was Mr. T. Dean, of Runcorn, who beat J. Ashworth of Blackpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN CONTINUATION SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CONTINUATION SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>In Greenway-road Board Schools on Monday evening the winter session for the continuation classes, which have been held for some years in Runcorn, was com- menced. The prizes awarded for good work during the previous session were distributed. Tha chair was occu- pied by Dr.McDougall,and there was a good attendance. The following were the recipients of prizes and certifi- cates :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MALE DEPARTMENT.</head>
        <p>MALE DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>First Class Prizes for Regular Attendance and Success. —John'A. Gatcliffe, George Lee, John Houghton,Christo- pher Snowball, Charles Collins, Thomas Prince. Jabez Scrutten,Alfred Pritchard,John Thomas,Richard Collins, Arthur Brazendale, Leonard Hughes, Thomas Curzon, Albert McNorton, William Carruthers, Thomas Car- ruthers.</p>
        <p>Second Class Prizes for Regular Attendance and Suc- cess.-William Molyneux, William Jackson,James Hough- ton, Thomas Hadley, Arthur Prince, Arthur Joynson. Robert Hussey, Edward Key, Walter Jones, John A. Hughes, Thomas Moores, Alfred Goadier, Joseph Hewitt, Joseph Inglesfield, Samuel Temperley, and Joseph Savage. Certificates of Merit awarded by the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Institutes :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FIRST CLASS.</head>
        <p>FIRST CLASS.</p>
        <p>Arithmetic.—Fred Ward, Alfred Pritchard, Thomas Prince, Thomas Paul, George Lee, James Kirkham, John A. Hughes, Henry Hill, Joseph Hewitt, John A. Gatliffe, Richard Collins, Charles Collins, John T. Burton, Sidney Buckley. Second Class :—John Thomas, John James Taylor, C. Snowball,William J. Molyneuz,Wallace McQuinn Arnold McNorton, Leonard Hughes, Thomas Hedley, Gowen W. Fryer, Ernest Brereton, Arnold Bra zendale. GIRLS' EVENING SCHN)L.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL Paires.—Cookerv, Fanny Hill, Elizlbeth Blythe; Arithmetic, Ada Davies; Needlework, Lillie Sberratt. _</p>
        <p>IST. CLASS PRIZES.—EIsie Hewitt, Florence Parr, Nellie Dyer, Eliza Sweetman, M. Britland, Clara Pilgrim, M. Withington, Jessie Johnson, Ada Elitchmough, Emma Farrell, N. Buckingham, G. Brown, G. Barnes. 2gro CLASS PRIZIM—Kate Done, Edith Wright, Maggie Roberts, Nellie Cornea, E. Cornea, F. Hilton, Edith Kenyon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CERTIFICATES.</head>
        <p>CERTIFICATES.</p>
        <p>IST. CLASS.—Nellie Bucicingham,Arla Davies, G. mien. Mith McLean, M. Stafford, Lillie Stubbs, Edith Wright. -</p>
        <p>2ND CrASS. —Gertrude Barnes,M. Britis nd, Elsie Hewitt. Ada Hitchmough, Janet Hughes, Annie Lambert,Emma Stubba,Gertrude Seragg,_and Eliza Shaw.</p>
        <p>After the Chairman had concluded the distribution, he said that about four years ago he had the honour of dis- tributing the prizes to the pupils who had succeeded in gaining them during the previous winter session. It gave him great pleasure to come there again after an interval of so many years to witness such an excellent attendance of what he hoped would be a good evening school during the forthcoming winter, and a strong evi- dence of the interest they took in those schools. He was also glad to see on that occasion a number of young girls, because it was becoming a source of considerable trouble to the Board that these evening schools for girls had not been so well attended as could be wished. The result of the establishment of those schools had been highly satisfactory, not only to the teachers, but also to the School Board who encouraged their establishment. He added that the parents of the children had had very few of the advantages which were offered to those present that night. Fprty or fifty years ago people could hardly read or write, but twenty years hence he hoped there would not be a single individual who could not read or write. He hoped they would equip themselves with knowledge that would help them to fight the battle of life, not only in England but wherever their lot might be cast. He wished to call attention to the reports which had been presented of the last session. In the boys department, he found there had been five sessions, and all had been of a satisfactory kind. The total num- ber of pupils bad been 516, being an inersase of 103 for each session. The average attendance for e ?eh evening since the commencement had been upwards of 60. The maximum grant had been earned each year, the total amount for the five sessions beim., £258 Hs. The re- port for the last session said " This is a distinctly ex- cellent evening school ; the work throughout reflects the greatest credit upon Mr. Moulton and his staff." That was the testimony of an expert. With regard to the girls school, that had only been established two years. The number of pupils had been 98, and the grant earned £22 149. The report for the last session said " This school is doing good steady work; all the subjects are well taught,and the needlework deserves special praise." The full grant was earned in this school. He would like to remark that the expense to the public of the evening schools since their establishment had only been £ll 16. That WAS a very small sum indeed. But with regard to that, he must tell them that an excess over the limit had been earned, but had not been re- ceived by the Board. If the excess money had not been deducted the schools would have been paving schools. But the sum which the public had been called upon to pay had not been above a 30th part of a penny in the £ which was a very small matter when compared with the great advantages. He noticed that two boys who had received prizes had not missed one attendance, namely, John A. Gatliffe and George Lee, and two others, John Houghton and Christopher Snowball had missed once only. (applause.) Mr. Moulton proposed a vote of thanks to the Chair- man and to the School Board for granting the prizes. As head teacher to the boys' evening school he could as- sure the Board that the granting of prizes had been a very great help in encouraging the students to attend re- gularly, and to attend to their work. The' certificates which had been gained were evidence of the diligence the pupils had brought to their work. Mr. MOSS seconded the vote of thanks. He was glad to see such a large attendance, as he had had some fears that the attractions of the Technical Institute would have taken most of them away. That evening continu- ation school was to prepare students for the Technical Se.hool, and he hoped they would go there when they had finished learning at. the evening _classes.</p>
        <p>... Mr. Davies supported the proposition, and spoke of the great interest taken in erineatian by the Chairman. —The resolution was curled unanimously and the pro- ceedings terminated.</p>
        <p>John Heptinstall, vice-president of the Crewe Co- operative Society, was on Wednesday fined £5, or one month's imprisonment, for stealing postage stamps hem the society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>REVISING THE LISTS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>REVISING THE LISTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. A. V. Colt-Williams, revising barrister, held a Court for the revision of the lists of the Parliamentary County and Parochial electors in the Runcorn district of the Nortbwich division, at Runcorn Police Court on Fri- day. Mr. William Handley, assisted by Mr. Clement Handley, represented the Liberals,and Mr. C. J. Hughes with Mr. A. Lightburn, were present on behalf ef the Conservatives. The overseers for the Runcorn and sur- rounding townships were also present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>COUNTY OFFICIALS AD THEIR VOTES.</head>
        <p>COUNTY OFFICIALS AD THEIR VOTES.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives claimed a vote on behalf of Ed ward Charles Hicks. inspector of police, residing on premises connected with the Runcorn Police Station.—Mr. Hand- ley referred to the case as being a very important one as it might affect others at Police Stations in the County. He informed the Barrister that the Police Court and build- ins attached formerly were use 4 by the Runcorn Im- provement Commissioners for the Town Hall, and the portion now occupied by the Inspector were then used as offices. The rates—poor and district—were then paid. When it was purchased by the county an appeal against the payment of any rate was made on the ground that it was County property and therefore free from rating. The subject went before the Assessment Committee with the result that the premiies were taken off the rate book, and since then they did not appear in the valuation lists so that there was nothing to show that there was a police station or residence for the Inspector. The matter was raised a few years ago when Superintendent Large resided in Runcorn, and his vote was allowed without going into the legal question the claim being thought to be reasonable. There had not since been an opportunity of dealing with the matter. In the Franchise Act of 1834 the duty of the overseers was shown clearly—section 9, sub-section 9,—as follows :—" That in any part of the United kingdom where a man inhabits a dwelling-house in respect of which no person is rated by reason of such dwelling-house belonging to or being occupied on behalf of the Crown, or by reason of any other greund of exemption, such person shall not be disentitled to be registered as a voter and to vote by reason only that no one is rated in respect of such cl veiling-house, and that no rates are paid in resneer ro the same, and it shall be the duty of the per- ooll.s Lilealiltlp: 011' th. rato hnok or vilustion roll to enter any such dwelling-house as last aforesaid in the rate book or valuation roll, together with the name of the inhabitant occupier thereof."— The Barrister said County buildings were exempt from rating. The residences on each side of the Court house were used for county purposes because the Superinten- dent and the Inspector lived there, in accordance with the duties they performed.—Mr. Handley said the offi- cials paid rent. They each paid 4s. he believed in the shape of rent.—The Barrister :—Are they not obliged to live there ?—Mr. Handley : Yes,but that is service fran- chise.—The Barrister : As part of the salary P—Mr. Handley It is entered separately, but it is part and parcel of their salary.—Mr. Hughes said it was for con- venience that they lived there. It was that they might better discharge their duties.—The Barrister : It is the rule when a place is attached to a Court house that the Superintendent is obliged to live on the premises and take charge of the builling.—Mr.Handley said it was an important question because throughout the County they were erecting Police stations.—The Barrister said the names should be on the list as stated by the Clause quoted, and he knew County buildings were exempt from rating but he could not say at that moment under what act.—Mr. Handley said the section stated that in that case the premises must still appear on the valuation list. Those in question did not. There was nothing to show such property, and these would rule hundreds of cases throughout Cheshire.—The Barrister stated that it was the duty of the persons making out the rate book to insert them.—Mr. Handley : The Assessment Commit- tee struck them out with instructions that they should not appear again.—Mr. Sutton (assistant overseer) said the premises were stated in the rate book and the entry was struck out by the overseers.—The Barrister : The committee were wrong in doing so ; they should have left them on.—Mr. Handley : If they had left them on the book the Inspector's name would have appeared in the off column. It would only be a matter as to the divisions.—Mr. Hughes: The occupiers ought not to 'suffer because the premises were not liable to be rated. It was through no fault of their own that they were not rated .—The Barrister stated that it appeared to him that the occupiers of the two houses attached to the court were entitled to be on for separate buildings. They were in bonaftde occupation, and they had no right to be dis- franchised. If they were legally exempted from rating as he believed they were, they ought not to lose their vote because the overseer han acted wrongly, thinking the Assessment Committee knew better than he.—Mr. Hughes : The action of the Assessment Committee ought not to disfranchise them. They pay rent —Mr. Handley: It is not a question against Inspector flicks, but on legal grounds. They ought to be on the valuation lists with- out anything opposite as the charge.—Mr. Hughes stated that by the section quoted, Superintendent Athertm had been placed on the register, but Inspector Hicks had not.—The Barrister: Is there any reason why one should Ibe on and the other not ? The houses are on each side of the Police Court, and if one is on, then the other ought.—Mr. Handley said he had raised the question because it would, no doubt, crop up throughout Cbeshire.—The Barrister stated that the present case was not on all fours with the houses occupied by the police in different townships. In the one in question the premises were the property of the County, but in other cases the police had to reside in dwelling-houses which were rated. In his opinion the inspector's name should be placed on the list.—Mr. Handley concurred. For, notwithstanding what the Assessment Committee might have done, it was the duty of the overseers to insert the premises and names of occupiers in the rate book under a special column.—The Barrister said he was quite pre- pared to state that such was a proper interpretation of the section.—Mr. Flandley said it was simply a legal point which might arise any time. The same spelled to Northwich.—The Barrister said his first duty was to find out whether the claimant had a qualification, and he thought the rate part of the business ought not to weigh with him. He would allow the vote. If there was any necessity to state a case the matter should receive further consideration.—Both agents were of the opinion that such a necessity would not arise. This was the only case of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FOOTBALL THE NORTHERN UNION. MEETING IN MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL THE NORTHERN UNION. MEETING IN MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>An important committee meeting of the Northern Rugby Union was held on Thursday evening at the Spread Eagle Hotel, in Manchester. Mr. W. H. Waller (Brighouse) 'presided, and the other members present were Messre. A. Fattorini, J. Clifford,S. Sewell. J. Nichol J. E. Warren, E. Gresty, J. Quirk, J. H. Smith, and J. Platt,hon. sec. The principal business transacted was the completion of the rules relating to professionalism and insurance, but the result of the discussion was not permitted to transpire. The decision of the Committee on the two subjects will, however, be made known at a general meeting of the clubs forming the Union to be held at Hnddersfield on the 17th of October. The rules for the government of the county contests will also be submitted for approval at that meeting. After an ex- planation given by Mr. Sewell, with reference to H. A. Close joining the Leeds Club, the following resolution was passed :—" This Committee exonerates the Leeds Club from blame in playing Close against Widnes and Batley previons to him having obtained his transfer."— Mr. Nichol (Halifax) gave notice that he will at the general meeting draw attention to the fact that many clubs, members of the English Rugby Union, are acting In a very reckless manner with respect to professionalism having the idea that if they become suspended they will be able to join this Northern Union. Be further gives notice that he will propose a resolution at the same meet- ing to the effect that any club suspended by the English Rugby Union for professionalism shall not be admitted to the Northern Union. Mr. Hutchinson (Wakefield Trinity) was appointed referee in the Lancashire v. Cheshire raatch at Stockport on October 21.</p>
        <p>A PASSENGER STEAMER ON THE ROCKS. RUNCORN PASSENGERS IN PERIL. The steamer Midnight Sun, which left London on the 9th September or a three weeks' cruise round the United Kingdom, struck on a rock opposite Luing island, 15 miles from Oban, to which place she was bound last Friday. There were about 90 passengers on board, and among them were Mr. Joseph Pritchard, jeweller, Run- corn ; and Mr. Thomas Hadfield, of the chemical works, Weston, near Runcorn. The steamer arrived safely off the coast of Antrim at an early hour on Friday morn- ing, but there was such a heavy swell that Captain Nivison determined that it was impossible to land, and started at once for Oban with a Scottish pilot. At one o'clock the Midnight Sun struck ' with great force oh a hidden rock. She was running at a good speed, and as a strong tide had set in two or three grinding thuds followed the first striking and the , ship took a list to starboard. The engines were imme- diately stopped, and the boats were ordered to be ' lowered. Passengers ran to their cabins, and all who were not too much panic-stricken secured lifebelts. After some difficulty the boats were got into the water, and it was now that matters assumed their worse aspect. All the boats on the port side seemed to be lowered at the same time, and the consequence was a frightful scene. One boat lowered suddenly came with a thud with her bows across the stern of another filled with passengers. One boat was then pushed off, and another was carried empty away by the current. The passengers and crew pulled for the island of Luing, more than a mile distant. What baggage could be got together in the hurry was brought ashore,and the unfor- tunate passengers, many of whom were dripping wet whilst others were bleeding from trifling cuts, made the hest of their condition. Here the passengers remained for more then five hours. When the tide was at the full the Midnight Sun fl3ated and swung off the rock. The crew reboarded her, and she was steered into Oban. Meanwhile a passing steam tug was engaged to bring the passengers on to Oban, and that place was reached shortly before eight o'clock in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SAD DEATH OF A RUNCORN GIRL</head>
        <p>SAD DEATH OF A RUNCORN GIRL</p>
        <p>LIFE ON A CANAL BOAT. On Thursday last week, at noon, a sad drowning fat- ality occurred on the Bridgewater Canal at Appleton, near London Bridge. Two narrow boats, known as the John and William, from Rucorn, were proceedingalong the canal, the first one being steered by Job Jones and the second by his ten-year-old daughter Lily. When the boats had reached that portion of the waterway lying between Red-lane Bridge and London Bridge the child, who was sitting upnri the top of the cabin, near to the chimney, fell into the water. The occurrence was wit. nessed by her father, who got ashore as soon as he could and endeavoured to save his daughter by means of co boat-hook, being afraid to enter very far into the w as he was unable to swim. He did not succeed, how- ever, and the child sank for the third time before assist- ance arrived. The body was recovered from the water, and Dr. Willett, who had been summoned, certified that life was extinct.</p>
        <p>The inquest on the body was held on Friday morning at the London Bridge Inn,Appleton, before Mr. Worsley, coroner, when evidence agreeing with the above particu- lars was given by the father. Questioned as to what measures he adopted to rescue the deceased, the father informed the Coroner that he procured a shaft, but that proved too short to reach the child. He then got another and a longer shaft, and entered the water up to his waist, and at length she was pulled out of the water. He was enable to swim. The girl, through struggling, had worked herself a good distance from the bank. The length of time she was in the water would be about ten or fifteen minutes. No one else was about at the time of the occurrence, except witness land the boy who was driving the horse on the bank, but when the body was being pulled out of the water there was a large num- ber of folks watching. His daughter was not accustomed to steering the boat, and she was only trusted to do so at meal times. Witness here admitted that on Thursday she was the only being nit the boat,and was in sole charge of it. Witness had made this arrangement owing to a boy,whose duty it had been to steer the boat,having left his employ at Runcorn. Witness thought that his daugh- ter might be trusted to steer until they arrived at Man- ' chester. In witness's opinion the girl had fallen into the water through the chimney of the boat giving way,and against which she was leaning. The chimney hung over the side of the boat, being held in that position by a chain.—Mr. Coppock asked witness ii, on leaving Run- corn, he did not think that the boat was undermanned, to which witness replied that it had been his opinion " then that his daughter could,until Manchester was reached, take the place of the boy. Mr. Cuppock considered that the steering of a boat from Runcorn to Manchester would be a severe tax upon a girl.—Anotherjuror asked witness if it was not pos- sible for him to have reached his daughter before she sank for the third time.—Witness replied that it had been very difficult for him to get off the boat and then run to the spot where the girl had fallen in. It had also been very difficult for him to go so far in the water as he did, seeing that he could not swim.—The juror : Many a father would have gone further for his girl. William Bangle,nephew of the last witness,with whom he lived at Runcorn, spoke as to the measures adopted to save to girl. His uncle had sone into the water until it retched his waist, but not being then able to touch the girl he came out and used a longer shaft. By the time the body was reached life was extinct. Witness said he could not swim, neither could his uncle. He Win not aware of anybody having left his uncle's employ at Run- cotn,he was not aware that a boy had been employed to steer the boat ; the deceased had steered the boat ever since the last witness had had charge of it, which was only a week agu. His uncle's wife also managed two boats with the assistance of a daughter and a son, so that four boats were being worked at once by his uncle and aunt jointly. The Coroner observed that the man seemed very thrifty, and was evidently trying to work the four boats as economically as possible. He recommended them to return a verdict of "Accidental death," which the jury did accordingly, one juryman remarking that the father ought to be censured for having evaded the truth by telling them that the girl was in charge of the boat in consequence of a boy having left his employ. The sug- gestion was not, however, acted upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORMISPONMINT.</p>
        <p>The number of students who have joined classes for continued and technical education, during the present week, in Runcorn, has been quite en- couraging. Both the Evening Continuation School and the Technical School were opened on Monday, and at the former the number of scholars shows a decided increase upon last year, whilst at the Tech- nical school, the number of students has exceeded the anticipations of the promoters. The report of the Continuation School for last session is one to be proud of and reflacts the greatest credit upon the head teachers and their assistants. Boys and girls who are just leaving school could not do better than continue their education at these night schools, which, so to speak, pave the way for technical instruction.</p>
        <p>The cost to the public of Runcorn for maintain- ing these Continuation Schools is very small indeed. In fact, since their commencement the sum total drawn from the rates has been only .£ll 16s 61. This, as was pointed out by Dr. McDougall on Mon- day evening, is not evens thirtieth part of a penny in the pound—a very small matter, when the ad- vantages are taken into consideration. Even this amount would not have been required from the rates, had the amount of grant earned been paid in full, but as the Education Department cannot go beyond a certain limit, the surplus money was withheld from the Board. This seems an injustice, and does not appear to lend itself to encouraging the teachers to press forword sith zeal in their work.</p>
        <p>A peculiar and rather important case came before the magistrates at Frodsham Petty Sessions on Wednesday. It arose through the Runcorn Rural Council being desirous to supply the house of Mr. Edwards with water from the public mains at Frodsham. Mr. Edwards, it seems, had been in possession of a private water supply for years,which he contended was good and adequate, and he strongly objected to paying for water which it was proposed should be provided him by the public body. He refused to have his house connected with the main, but as the Council were of the opinion that the supply to the house was not satis- factory, the magistrates were asked to make an order, permitting their official to enter Mr. Edward's promises in order to obtain particu- lars for the specifications to be drawn up for the performance of the work. The magistrates granted the order, and as the present one was regarded as a test case, the decision will affect a number of other residents in the locality who are in a somewhat similar position. It is no doubt to the interests of thegeneral public that the water supply should be in the hands of one body instead of many single individuals supplying their own wants. But when it is so,a great responsibility rests with the authority to keep an adequate supply. Not many months ago this was brought home to the members of the Runcorn Rural Council when the residents of Wes- ton were loud in their complaints at the intermit- tency of the public water supply.</p>
        <p>A new departure has been taken by the Runcorn School Board in the provision of teachers for the first and second s'andard for boys. These classes were formerly taught by males in the Runcorn schools, hut three females have now been appointed to the classes, and if the experi- ment is successful i will, no doubt, he carried still further. In support of this action it is stated that it is only following in the wake of first-class board schools throughout the country, and another consideration for adopting the method is, that it is wise to continue female influence over the boys, who now come from the infants' schools at an early age. The result of this naw method will ba awaited with interest.</p>
        <p>It is gratifying to note that the Ship Canal Com- pany are faith fully endeavouring to carry out their agreement with the Runcorn Urban Council for the provision of increased facilities at Runcorn. The work of supplying these is now proceeding apace, and in the course of a few months, Runcorn will be Possessed of lay-by accommodation to the extent of 1,200 feet. This should prove of decided advan- tage to the town in making it a more efficient distributing centre for goods coming up the Ship Canal,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ITALTON. •</head>
        <p>ITALTON. •</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A HALEWOOD MAN.—AbOllt seven O'clock on Saturday night, as Thomas 13a.snett, of Halewood, was walking along Halton villa?" near to the Post Office, when he accidentally fell and fra3tured the splint bone of his left leg. A man,who was passino, helped him to the side of the road. and Mr. Albert Rowe, an Ambulance man, coming up shortly afterwards, an, plied temporary splints. Burnett was conveyed to the A Bangor cyclist named William Williams was killed house of his sister-in-law, whither he was bound when near Port Dinorwic on Wednesday,owing to a horse tak- the accident took place. Dr. Carruthers set the limb, and the injured man is :making good progress 'towards ing fright and driving the shaft of a market car into his recovery. stomach.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND INCAPABLE.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown police station on Monday morning, before Mr. C.B.F. Borron, Thomas Britch of Psrk-road, St. Helens, was charged, on the infor- mation of P.C. Tivney, with being drunk and incap- able in Legh-street, Earlestown, on Sunday night, and was fined 2s 6d. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>1.0.G.T. EARLESTOWN EBENEZER LODGE.</head>
        <p>1.0.G.T. EARLESTOWN EBENEZER LODGE.</p>
        <p>This Lodge held its utual weekly session, there being a very fair attendance. Bro. W. Hatton pre- sided. The programme consisted of readings in the Lancashire dialect, readings being given by Bros. W. J. Taylor, .J. Taylor, H. Brown and ochers, which were well given and received. A very pleasant evening was closed about 945 COTTAGE FIRE AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>On Monday the roof of the thatched cottage occupied by Mrs. Wilson, at Pevvfall, Ilaydock, was seen by that lady to be on fire. Information was conveyed to Messrs. Evans and Co.'s works, and a number of workmen with the steam five engine at once proceeded to the place, but before the engine could be got to work the fire was extinguished by buckets of water. The damage is estimated at £lO. It is not known how the fire originated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BOWLING HANDICAPS.</head>
        <p>BOWLING HANDICAPS.</p>
        <p>A bowling handicap took place on the Viaduct Institute (Earlestown), No. 1 green, on Saturday afternoon. The preen was in fair condition, and there were 42 entries. 't be first prize, value .£l, ' was secured by J. P. Barr, second 10s. John Williams third 7s. J. Marsh, fourth 33. 61 John Bolton. A handicap also took place on the green of the Via- duct Institute bowling club for prizes given by Mr. Astley Bing. The green was in good condition,and the day being fine, the bowling was above the arc r- age. he first prize, a set of fire irons, was won by Mr. E. Cronin, 2nd, half-a-dozen knives and forks, James Watson, 3rd, set of jugs, R. Green. The prizes were at the close distributed by Mr. Cromp- ton, vice-chairman. SHOCLASTIC.</p>
        <p>The Chester College Certificate Examination List shows that in the second year's examination 24 firsts and 24 seconds have been obtained, and that the first year students have obtained thirty firsts and twenty-five seconds. Mr. James Speakman, of Park-road, Newton-le-Willows, has again obtained a first class and has been placed second on the Col- lege List. As this is the second time that Mr. Speakman has occupied that position, and as the man who headed the list last year now stands , eighth, it will be seen that to Mr. Speakman is awarded the honourable position of having obtained the highest average of marks during his two years term in College. EARLESTOWN BAPTIST CHURCH. Special harvest thanksgiving services were held in the above church on Sunday. The edifice was very prettily decorated with fruit, fl)wers, vege- tables and cereals, which at the close of the services were sold by auction to those present. The ser- vices on Sunday morning and evening were con- ducted by the Rev. F. E. Miller, and in the afternoon a children's service was conducted by Mr. A. Thompson, 8.A., of Liverpool.—On Monday even- ing a tea and social meeting was held, when there were abcut 120 present to tea. The subsequent pro- ceedings consisted of musical selections by the choir and addresses by the Rev. F. E. Miller and others. The collections in aid of the church debt amounted to £lO. GOOD TIIMPLARS.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the of Earlestown lodge of (*.Kid Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall. Earlestown on Monday evening. Bro. James Hatton, C.T., presided, and there was a large attendance of members. Nine candidates were ad- mitted to membership,and several others were pro- posed. Bro. Barker, secretary, gave the financial 1 report of the tea meeting recently held, and it was adopted. It was then resolved to declare the meet- ing open, aud a large number of the public were admitted. Bro. G. E. Warburtion was responsible for the programme, and took charge of the pro- ceedings. Songs were given by Sisters A.Newman, and Mrs. Willis, a duet by Sisters M.and E.Jackson, recitations by Bros. J. Hopkins and Wilson, Sisters S. Stringer, and R. Cordingley, and two humorous songs by Bro. J. Huddlesron. An earnest address was given by the Chairman. VULCAN DAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The annual tea party to the scholars attending the Vulcan infant school took place on Friday afternoon. The children to the number of over 100, assembled at the Vulcan foundry field after dinner,and after indulging in games they adjourned to the school room where they partook of tea. Miss Laithwaite and her assistants bad charge of the children. Several ladies also attended, including Mrs. Collingwood, Mrs. Carson, Mrs. Norman and friends, Miss Mansell, Misses Carson, Miss Edwards and Miss Eddington. After tea the children again proceeded to the field and resumed their games until dusk. Sweets and nuts were distributed dur- ing the afternoon. VIADUCT INSTITUTE TECEIN/CAL INSTRUCTION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CLASSES.</head>
        <p>CLASSES.</p>
        <p>It will be seen from-ii;;Z;ertisement in another column, that students ale still required in the domestic Sciences, in order to ensure the foundation of closes in these subjects at Earlestown. The same thing prevails at the Newton end of the township, ' in fact they are much more backward than Earles- town. This is a fact which is greatly to be re- gretted, as it would be a pity if such classes were allowed to collapse for lack of students, which will certainly be the case, if the number required was not made up before the 4th Ocrober. LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>A meeting of Liberals was held at the Central Committee rooms, Cross-lane, Earlestown,on Thurs- day evening for the purpose of discussing the advisability of forming a Liberal Club. There was a large attendance and the meeting was presided over by Air. W. R. Renshaw, registration agent. The matter was fully discussed and it was decided to form a club, on the premises then occupied. SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening the usual meeting of the Cadets of Temperance was held In the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown. Sister Ethel Pearson, W.A., was in the chair. Several items of interest to the sec- tion was brought forword. At 730 the quarterly meeting of the Britains Need Divi-ion No. 290 was held in the same room. Bro. J. Pickett presided. After the opening ceremony the election of five re- presentatives to the next grand division to be held at Widnes, October 19th, resulted as follows : Bros. J. Pickett, J. French, T. Foden, T. Judson, C. Broster. The usual quarterly bills were ordered to be paid. A grant was made to a brother from the benevolent fund. It was resolved that the levy for the benevolent be called in in March and Septem- 'ler there not having been any levy for this fund during the last 15 years which is 3cl.per member for the half year. The division is in a very prosperous position both in numbers and finance. The meeting closed at 9.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>WARGRAVE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The special thanksgiving services tor the ingathering of the harvest which were commenced last Thursday, were continued on Sunday in the above church. The morning service was conducted by the Rev. W. J. Adams, vicar of St. Augustine's, Liverpool, who preached an excellent discourse. The choir sang the anthem, " Man goeth forth to his work and to his labour until the even- ing." In the afternoon, a sacred musical service was given by the organist, Mr. E. Leyland, and the choir, there being a very large audience. The programme was excellently rendered, and apparently fully appreciated by those present. The,evening service was conducted by the Rev. J. Carson, the sermon being preached by the Rev. P. J. Byrne, curate of St. Anne's, Warrington, who gave an instructive discourse from the text " Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will net aepart from it." The anthem given by the choir was " Praise the Lord,o Jerusalem." The collections which ware in aid of the Wargrave and Vulcan schools, amounted to £2l is. 10d.</p>
        <p>A SUSPICIOUS ACTION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>This (Friday) morning at Runcorn Polies Court, David Burton, 103, Surrey-sireet, chemical labourer, was charged with being on the enclosed premises of Mr. Samuel Taylor, J.P., C.C., Rydal Mount, Halton-road,at 9.30 p.m., the previous night, with intent to commit a felony.—P.C. Wylie spoke to being on duty in the neigh- bourhood of Mr. Taylor's house about the time stated and on looking round the house in consequenee of com- plaints having been received of the thett of fowls, he saw the prisoner looking through the kitchen window. On seeing witness Burton crouched under some bushes. Witness went up to him, and on charging him with being there with intent to commit a felony, the prisoner said he did not know what caused him to be there. On being further charged at the Police station, ha stated that rie saw a light in the kitchen, and went there to see what was going on.—Mr. Taylor, son of Mr. samuel Taylor deposed to having missed "ins fowls from their place within the last fortnight.—Superintendent Ather- ton stated that the prisoner had resided in Runcorn for about fourteen years, and no charge had been previously brought against him.—ile was discharged with a caution: The magistrates on the bench were Mr. James flandley (chairman), and Mr. E. Imisen.</p>
