      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>STREET THE/T.—tot:a CHASE BY DETECTIVE MONAGHAN. David Clay, 32, Warrington-road,Widnes,was charged at the Warrington Borough Court ..on Monday, with stealing frem Mi9S Blackhurst 124.9 d. in cash on Satur- day afternoon. [he Chief Censtable said that about ten o'clock on Saturday mornine, Detective Monaghan was in the neiehbourhood ef Market-gate and saw the prisoner looking very closely into Mr. Milling's shop. He was there about ten minutes and watched everyone who went in and out of the shop. Monaghan thought he was worth looking after. Presently he went into Butter- market-st , arel watched the people in Bowcock's shop for some time, his movements been very suspicious. He afterwards went to the end of Scotland-road, and fol- lowed closely a respectable woman with a basket on her arm. The woman crossed to Academy-st.„ and prisoner got so near to her that she turned round and he then went away. He then went to Mersey-st., and stopped in front of two " putty" shops. Monaghan secreted himself and watched him for an hour and he handled many articles outside the shops. At length the bells in the works ran!, and the men came out This caused the prisoner to go away rather quickly, along Rose and Crown-street to Bridge-street. Monaghan followed, and saw him follow Miss Blackherst, who had come out of Mr. Toffs restaurant. Monaghan saw him follow her over Warrington Bridee. along St. James'-street and linutsferd-road. Miss Blackhurt was at the time carry- ing two Behr paper parcels, a satchel!, and a purse con- taining 124. 93. lit cash. When near Black Bear Bridge, Monaghan saw the prisoner suddenly get hold of Miss Blackhurst's right hand, and then take the purse out of her left. Be then ran away in the direction of Monaghan, and the latter got hold of him and a severe struggle took place. During the struggle the prisoner threw the purse some distance away, hut a woman picked it up and gave it to Monaghan.—P.C.Monks was just going past, and Monaghan sent him after Miss Blackhurst who had gone on without raising the alarm. She had fnegetten her purse and thought that as she still had her satchel' and parcels, nothing had been stolen. Her finger, however, was severely hurt. On being brought back by P.C. Monks, Miss Blackhurst at once.identifir:d the purse as her property. The prisoner was then fireught to the police station and when charged admitted the theft. Monaghan was enabled to follow him so far unperceived by judiciously making use of people, especially a broad carter. (Laughter.)— Detective Monaghan gave evidence corroborative of the Chief Conatable's statement. —The prisoner said that he was hungry and had nowhere to sleep. Hunger tempted him to do it —The Mayor said that the prisoner had a' very bad record. He bad five years in a reformatory School, and had been committed for 14 days time after time. He would receive the maximum penalty of three months' hard labour. A 'MIDWIFE STINHONED. Emma Wright, midwife. 12, Eldon-street, was charged at the Boreugh Court on Monday, with making a false declaration on Sentember 911, to the effect that a child was still hors. The Town Clerk who nroseeuted said that the preeeedings were taken under Section 40 of the Births and Deaths Registration Ant of 1874. Thedefend- ant attended a woman named Brannon at 3, James-st., and the child was born and was baptised by Father Duggan later on in the same day. On September 9th, the defendant signed a declaration that the child was stillborn, and it Was buried in unconsecrated ground, and the burial fees were not paid. It was probably for this reason that the declaration had been made. The defendant was liable to a fine of £lO or seven years penal servitude for this offence.—The husband of the defend- ant, who was in court, said that his wife was 72 years of age and had a stroke two rears ago.—ln conse- quence of this pea the Bench inflicted a penalty of si. and costs.</p>
        <p>ABSTRACTIgG THE TOWN'S WATER. Thomas and Walter Cross, builders, Longford-street, were charged at the Borough Court on Monday, with unlawfully taking a quantity of water belonging to the Corporation on August 30th, without having paid the water rate. The Town Clerk, who prosecuted, said that the defendants were building some houses in Ferstemtreet and althourdi he had not paid the water rate, he had used the town's water.—Mr. Walter Cross said that he had paid the rate tor a portion of the houses, but there was no main in the part where the houses in Question were. There wire only six houses unpaid for.—Mr. Thompson, clerk at the water r ffice. said that Mr. Cross had been taking the water for two months.—The defend. ants were fined kl. and rests. THEFTS FROM SHOP DOORS. At the Warrington Borough Court on Wednesday, before Mr. Ilhn irclonlh (chairman), Mr. John GeddeS, and Mr. J. H Fleming, Patrick Riley and Thomas Woods were charged with stealing at two o'clock the previous day, a man's overcoat valued 305., the property of Thomas Woods, tailor, Friars Green.. Woods was also charged with stealing from Isaac Houghton, Mersey-0. on Monday, a man's macintosh. valued 104.—The Chief Constable steed that the two defendants were farm labourers with no settled abode. On Tuesdac, Mr. Percy Coates saw the men at Friars Gate carrying some roller tow4s. and a little afterwards he saw them again, and this time Woods had an over- coat. They went up Bridge-st., and Mr. Coates in- formed P.C. Newsy wha followed the prisoners up Bridge-st. and down Buttermarket' -st. where they turned into Dr Peacock's stables. P.C. Nevzey followed them and asked them what account they had to give of the coat. Woods said thlt he had bought it eight months ago in the " Paddy " market, Liverpool. Riley tried to get away hut the constab!e got hold of him and brought him and Woods to the police station. On in- : quiries being made, it was discovered that the coat be- longed to Mr. Woods, Friars Green. With regard to the second charge, the prisoner Woods went into Mrs. Les- lie's "putty" shop with another man and offered a macintosh for tale. Mrs. Leslie noticed that there was a private mark upon it which showed that it had come from Mr. Isaac Houghton, pawnbroker. S'le sent to Mr. Houghton, but before she could obtain any definite in- formation the men loft leaving the macintosh behind. It was discovered that the article did h,long to Mr. Houghton. Mr. Hunt ,aid that the prisoner Woods appeared to think that ir WAR safer to have a second man with him, and re-hans Riley would he hest served by giving him the benefit of a doubt as to his guilt.—P.o Newsy having given evidence, the magistrates decided to send Woods to gaol for on- month, and discharged Riley. They also complimented Mrs. Les'i and Mr. Percy Coates for the Dart their had taken in the eiTtir. Two yotrruirom Tntaves. Peter awl Ktte Hankinson. brother and sister, were chargel at the Borough Court on Wednesday, with steal- -stlg.in Horeemarket-street from the shop of Joseph Turner, on Saturday, eight ernes of matches. Kate was also charged with stealing on Tuesday, a quantity of firewood, the property of Robert Heaton, and alto some firewood, the property or-lames Rowlineon. The Chief Constable said that the two children were the son and daughter of Peter Hankinsnn. 6 Back Crosslerstreet. On Saturday at about five o'clock the complainant Raw a box containing nine gross of matches in the Hop Pole yard, where he kept them, but a little after nine he went again and found the box containing the matches roken into and eight gross of them missing. The police were informed, and it was discovered that the two de- fendants bad been setting the boxes at a penny a dozen to boys to sell again. The boy was not found until Tuesday afternoon when Detective Monaghan captured him and he then confessed to sleeping out in a closet in : Bold-street since Saturday. The girl was met by Mona- ghan late on the same day and was carrying a q3antity et wood. Monaghan asked her what 'Gamut she</p>
        <p>had to give of the wood, and she said that she ht bought it of Mr.Heaton. On being taken to Mr. Heaton's yard, however, she owned to having stolen the wood. It WKS also ascertained that she had stolen some wood from Mr. Rowlinson, Buttermarket-street.—The fattier of the children said that they were both good children,although the girl did sleep out occasionally. The boy had never slept out before the present occasion.—Tne bench de- cided to adjourn the case against Peter for a fornight in order to find a home for him, and Kate was sent to prison for 14 days, afterwards to be sent to a reforma- tory for five years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
