      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MINO It CASES.</head>
        <p>MINO It CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Wright and Mr. S. Wright. A Ronavrev.— Thomas Hindle, a boy about 14, was charged with having absconded from the St. Joseph's Industrial School.—Sergeant Cheney said he saw the de- fendant reading the papers outside the Police Station,and noticing that he was a stranger, witness questioned the lad who said he had run away from the above industrial school and stolen some money. He had been to Black- pool and had been knocking about ever since.— Word having been received that the school authorities would call for him the magistrates handed him over to the police again. NOT ACCOUNTABLE FOR HIS ACTIONS.—Thomas McCann, a strongly built man, who appeared with his head band- aged, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Parker-st. at 10.30 on Thursday night; he was al,o charged with assaulting P.C. John Cooper while in the execution of his duty.—P.C. Cooper said that on the night in question he saw the prisoner in the street with blood running down his face. He asked him who had been hitting him and he said four gipsies had been foul of him. Witness advised him to go home, whereupon prisoner without provocation struck him in the face.— In court prisoner said he remembered nothing about the , matter, and the Bench believing that his injury, which was somewhat serious, rendered him unaccountable for his actions, discharged him. THE CURIOUS WIFE DESERTION Can.—Arthur Ow Stockport, was again brought up and charged with de- serting his wife Elizabeth. Mr. Browne represented complainant and Mr. Davies,the defendant.—Mr. Davies said his client refused to live with his wife, though they had bad no disagreement. He had offered 54.,a week to her. In common with Mr. Browne he did not think the defendant answerable for his actions, and he also con- sidered him unfit for any woman to live with.—The Bench made an order for him to pay 7s. 61. a week. QUARRELL BETWEEN WOMEN.—Mrs. D,,rnpsfw, 3, Mil I- yard, was bound over to keep the peace for six months for having assaulted and beaten Mary Robinson, 44, , Pennell-street, on the 14th September.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Xte,sqrq. J. Burgess (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>S. Chandley: THErr ov A CROW-Ban.—William Hammond was charged with stealing from a siding of the Cheshire Lines Railway on the previous morning one crow-bar value Ls. the property of the Committee. Priaoner pl.aded guilty —The Head Constable stated that the prisoner was a farm labourer, 62 years of age, and a native of Preston. On the morning in question Detective Sergeant Monaghan was standing at the end of Sootland•road, when he saw the prisoner with the bar. Noticing that one end of it was wet, as though newly pulled out of the ground, Monaghan questioned the prisoner, who, not giving a satisfactory account, was taktm into custody.— The Bench sent Hammond to gaol for seven days' with</p>
        <p>hard labour. ABSENTING HIMSELF FROM WORlC.—Willians Jones was charged on warrant. he not having appeared to the summons charging him with havina left his work at Messrs. Eddowes' mineral water manufactory, on the 2nd inst., thereby putting his employers to a loss of ss. —Defendant, who stated that he left woTk for a holi- day, was ordered to return and to pay ss. and the costs.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —Before Um Ma.yor (Councillor James Fair- clotigh), Alderman Harrison, Mr. T. Birehall, and Mr.</p>
        <p>_ J. W. Hayward. BEGGING —Thomas Durkin, Perch,lls-told, was sent to gaol for seven days' hard labour for begging in Golden Grove-st., on Saturday. DRITNICENNESE.—Tho following personNwere dealt with for this offence :—Thomas Johnson, Scotland-road, fined 5.. and costs ; John Wilfrid De!ooze, 82 Lythgoe's-lane, ss. and costs: George Richards, 35 M-rßey-at.. 53. in- cluding coats; Catherine Bate, 8 Morris'-yard. 53. and costs ; Samuel Sutcliffe. 4 Copper's-brow. 23. 611. ; Bridget Mannino, 20 Erwood-at., dismissed ; William Nicholson, 7 Wakefield .st., 24. 6d. • TEMPORARY AtITHORITY.—George Vernon was granted temporary authority to sell beer on the premises of 14 and 16 Powv's-st., in lieu of James Potter. DAMAGING TILES IN THE PAWL—John James Soaker- vine. 9 academy-st., was fined is. for damaging a tile in Bank Park on S-prember 15th. P.S. Rowbottom said that the defendant was jumping on the asphalt near the bandstand, and broke one of the tiles. The defendant said that it was quite an accident. BREACH OF THE PEACE.—Thomas Pannell, and William Cunningham, were bound over to keep the peace for six months for fighting in Butterrnarket-street, on Septem- ber 15th. CHIMNEY ON FIRE.—John Hartley, 326 Knutsford-road, was fined is. for allowing the chimney of his dwelling- house to be on fire. TOESDLY.—liefore Mr. Garnett (chairman) and Mr. J. H.</p>
        <p>Flemin.</p>
        <p>A WIFE ASSAULT.—James Green. 28 Haydock-street, was charged with assaulting and heating his wife on September 10. Mrs. Green said that her husband was going to Oledge his shoes and she tried to stop him. He then ran her into her sister's house and kicked her all over, black and blue.—The sister said that the complain- ant was black and blue all over. The husband denied kicking her and a female in court having examined irlr, and having found that ther were marks upon her body, the Bench fined defendant 104. and cost. SCHOOL ATITSDANCR CASES.—The following cases for irregular fitrendance at school were dealt with, Mr. W. Jones prnaecuting :—John Powell, 21 Oliver-street, war- rant granted owing to non-a pnearance of the defendant, James lii,chall.23 Albeit Terrace, fined 24 61. ; William ; Burton, 62 Pierpoint-street„ 54. ; Charles Cambell, 13 Bakehouse-vard, 5. ; Simnel Clifton, 2 Er-wood-omq,, adjourned ; .Tohn Coyne 4 Slcer-equare. 24. 61. John et Flannaan, 28 Hopwood-street., 2. 61. ; Patrick (hover, 4 Mill-vard, 2s. 61.; Jame-. King, 47 Flkmiltnn-street, 24. 61., EA ward Lamy, 4 Rail street, 24. 61.; Richard Newton, 3 Roger-street; adjourned ; Isaac Phillips, 3 Navigation-street, 54,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUNCORN PE rTY SESSIONS MONDAY.—Before Mr. P,ilm SnPakmln (ollairman), and</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PE rTY SESSIONS MONDAY.—Before Mr. P,ilm SnPakmln (ollairman), and</p>
        <p>• Mr. F. J.Narman. AN AMICABLE SETTLEMENT. —William Antrobits. night wate.ht.,,n, of 28. Rotr,rr-itr, was summoned for assaulting Elizabeth Anti-obits. his wife, on the 13. n ins. Defendant pleaded guilty. Mr. Burton, solicitor. represented the complainant. end stated that the parties had been married for about eighteen months. The com- plainant, had received considerable provocation a, the handa of her huahand. On the 13th inst., the defendant came home and made snme complaint about his dinner not being ready. That was ahout three o'clock in the afternoon, and without any provoneth.n he struck her in the mouth, and made it bleed. She thm went into the Yard, and he commenced smashing the fursiture. This did not belong to him He efterwardS got hold of the complainant's face and struck her in the era. , Throne' his violence she fainted in the yard.—Cormborative evidence was given by the comnlainant. who stated that since the occurrence vhe had lived at her mother's house Her husband earned 304. a week.—The defendant steed that it was his wife's parents that were to blame. fie had been illtreated tic them ever since he had been Married.—The Chairman said it was a case where younr married people should try to bear and forbear with one another. The case would be adjourned to the end of the court,and in the meantime he would ask Mr. Burton to see if an arrangement could he come to.—At the close of the court. Mr. Burton said the case, with the consent of the magistrates, would be withdrawn. They were very much obliged for the suggestion of the Chairman. The complainant and defendant had promised to live amic- ably together ag,ain.—The Chairman said he was sure the Beach was very glad indeed that the meter had been settled.encl to Mrs.Antrohus he would saw, ttN•ver be in a temper when scar husband is in one." cis 42ND APPEAEANCE.—A charge of being drink and dieorderly waa preferred against John Burke, labourer, of Water-St.. Runcorn, on the 14th inst.. in Church-a'. The case was proved hr P.C. Booth. Inspector Flirok; said it was the defendant's 42nd appearance, to which , he retorted that he had never been tip for anything hot drinking a pint of hcor.—Ths Chairman : When are you I going to a teetotaler ?—D,fenitant : I will sign the nled,te in a week o. two.—The Chairman : lam afraid coo a a a procraatinator.—A flee of ss. and cots was imposed. ANITEIER OLD OFFENDRR —Ellen Gandy, St. lihmat., was released from orison on Saturday, and on Mon.lav she was again before the reagistr ttee nn a char' of be- ing, drunk and disorderlr in Church-st., the nrcviout night. It was stared that there were 42 conviction. against the prisoner. She asked the magistrates to nVP,s look the offence this time, and she would ,einrm.—Fined 54. and costs. CHARGE OF ASSAULT —P4er Wrizht, Runcorn, wag summoned fir assAulting his wife .11 the 20th June. The defendant did not sop' ar,and the com- plainant stated that he had told her that he did not ears for magistrates, I, weers, or pohcemen. She inform. the Bench that a 8 30 on the night in question she re- turned home a fter being out washing. 'I he defendant and draTso,cl rho chni• from under her cam. dry:v.l,l, 3;ra.</p>
        <p>WOMEN AND AND WOMEN ONLY are mnst comrelent to fully apprmiate the purity, sweetness, and delirano of CuTIOURA SOAP, and to dimover new uses fcr it dsily In the preparation of curative washes, solutions, &amp;1., for annoying irritations, ohafinF.rs,and excoriation of the skin and mucous membrane, or ton free or offensive perspiration, it has proved most grateful. Like all others of the CIITICIIHAL preparations, CIITTCURA SOAP apOP*l9 to the refined and cultivated evervwhere,and is 1 beyond all comparison the most effective akin purifyin.f and beautifying snap,as well as the purest and sweetest or toilets both had aoreer7.</p>
