      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 28, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 28, 1895.</p>
        <p>'and she fell tt, the Boor. On getting up he told her he would finish her, and again struck her knockina head through the window. He then weut away.— warrant was issued.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MEM/X.—Before Messrs. J. C. Parr (in the chair), H.</p>
        <p>Nield, and J. Mclntyre. Jung Lurre.—The assistant overseers presented lists of persons "duly qualified to serve on juries." No objection was raised.—Several persons were also " excused " from payment of poor rates on account of poverty. DRUNICENNESS.—The following persons were fined for drunkenness :—Dominick Knockton. 103. and costs; Dennis Burns, 53. ; Anthony Daley, 103. ; and John gins, sa. ' AN UNsusrsiNED CHARGE.—George Pete, 26, Bank-st., Warrington, was summoned with respect to an offence alleged to have been committed on the 15th September. On that date he was searched by Constable Robinson. .• who had good cause to suspect that he had come from certain land at Moore where he had been in search of game," nets for the purpose having been found in his possession.—Mr.Browne appeared for the defe.ndant,and pleaded not guilty.-R thiuson said he was in Green-lane, Moss Side, on the day in question, when he saw the de- fendant coming from land in the possession of Mrs. Davidson. Ho had something bulky in his possession. and witness searched him and found a ferret and a couple of nets on him.—Mr. Browne said that before the Bench could convict they must find that the defendant was lawfully searched, and then that he was coming from land, not going to it. The words used did not meet the case of a poacher suspected of going to land in search of game. He (Mr. Browne) aornitted that the says probancli lay upen him to prove that his client had no intention in going in piiriuit of game. The de- fendant was a respeet able young rnan,and nothing w is previously known against him. He was not a poacher, hut went in for ratting,and had done so for some years. I On the morning in questiqn he went out ratting, when he met a ntiglibdur named Woods and his erandson. They had a conversation, and the result was that they all determined to go to Moore together, and they were together when the constable saw them. Thee had been the whole of the tim. on public roads.—The Chairman, at this stage, said the Bench were quite satisfied that there was no case against the defendant,who was accerd- ingly discharged—The defendant was then charged with allowing his dog to be at large on the day in question.— Mr. Browne again appeared for the defendant, and ad- mitted the offence. but said the defendant took off the muzzle in a private place for the relief of the animal.— A fiflP of 2a 61. was inflicted. Dons AT LANGS.—Joseph Bnatock, labourer, Appleton. and Leonard Groves, Acton Bridge, were fined ss. and 2s. 6.1. respectively for allowing their dogs to be at PLAYING AT CARDS.—Fred Goodier, William Woods, Samuel Dobson, George Houghton and John Jepson, all of Stockton Heath, were summoned for having on the Bth September, at Appleton, played at cards.—Constable Brew stated the facts of the case. The offence, he alleged, was committed at Hill Cliffs at 3.15 p.m. on the day in question, which was Sunday. The defendants were sitting no the side of the road. He found the pack of cards and the money produced on the ground.—An attempt was made to prove an alibi with regard to Goodier, and it was successful, that defendant being discharged ; the other defendants were each fined in the costs. 3s. 61. AN OLD WEDDING CUSTOM —ROPING THE ROAD—John Goodliffe and Frederick Kirkland, of Stockton Heath. were summoned for bayiecr on the 22nd Aueust, At Appleton, nbitrileted the free Passage of E'lestnere-road by placing a rope across it.—They pleaded guilty.—P.C. Brew stated that on the day in question a wedding had been celebrated at Stockton Heath Church, when the defendants placed a rope across the road and stopped the cab containing the bride and bridegroom.' Several com- plaints about such conduct had been made to the police of the direrict.—Seperintendent Atherton said it was an old custom to rope a road on the occasion of a wedding, but the police had been advised to take legal proceedings against offenders in this respect.—One of the defendants said he stooped the cab to wish the happy connle, whom he knew, much happiness.—This being the first offence of the kind, the defendants were dismissed on payment</p>
        <p>of the enqq. Exrcasmar or Tram—Mr. C. W. Phillip. of the Dareshury, applied for an extension of time on the 24th prox , on the occasion of the annual dinner b Sr Gilbert •Greenall's tenants. An hour's extension was granted. •</p>
        <p>Ized for foul play, but Dunbavin failed to turn it to ad- vantage. The Hornets, by a strong rush, forced their vay to the home line, where a pack was formed. Here Mason got possession, but his intended pass to his three- quarter was intercepted by J. Faulkner; who dodged in :med style, and passing at the right moment to S. Abrams, the latter put on top speed and scored under tire posts. Dunbavia failed to negotiate the .asy place. From the kick-out central play ,ollowed, and Rigg saved finely on two occasions when disaster looked inevitable. The play was mostly occupied by the forwards. J. Faulkner started a fine passing roovement,which was taken up by the whole of the home threevarters, and West got over, but a pass forward negatived the point. Runeorn kept up the ! , pressure, and from a strong rush by W. Faulkner, Myers ; got over, but again the referee decided against the home tea tn,and a pack was farmed on the fins. This time J. ; , Faulkner made no mistake,and got over in an easy posi- tion, Dun havin kicking a goal. Restarting, the Hornets had a turn of passing, but little headway was made, Warder replying with a fine run. Myers got a pass and dodged grandly over, but no goal resulted. 'the kick out again saw the Lancastrians penned in their own quarters, and Riggs saved cleverly time after time. Only tor hi ,s fine defence the score would have been much heavier, so marked was the superiority of the home team. Just before half time the visitors, by a strong I forward rush, got to the borne 25 for the second time ; during this portion of the match. The score at the in- terval read—Runcorn one goal two tries to nil. W. Faulkner restarted, to which Rigg replied, and the first riaeks were formed in the home half. J. Faulkner got the ball away, and Rigg was taken ball in hand. Facing by the home team enlivened matters, and West had hard lines after some tricky play. Warder kept up the pressure,and passing correctly to Dunbavin he raced over at the corner,but the same player failed to augment the point. Right away from tbe kick out, from a pack in tire centre of the vround,A.brams got hold and passed to .1. Faulkner, who cleared his men and repassed to Abrams, who when challenged repassed again to his companion half back, who in turn handed over, and Abrams completed the •prettiest piece of combination ever witnessed on the Runcorn ground,by scoring under the posts. Dunbavin this time placed with success. From a line out at centre, C. Pendlebury secured, and dodging man after man scored between the posts. This was a fine piece of work. Dunbavin again added the malir point. Some exciting passages took place c wing to the Cestriana endeavoura t Wailer score, whilst the Hornets were equally persistent to keep their lines in- tact. However, after another fine exposition of the handling code, Dunbavin sccred a try, but no goal accrued. The aual result was—Runcorn three goals, four tries to nill.</p>
        <p>WtatRINGTON Sr. MARY'S V. ALTRINCHAM '6 A."—These teams met on the /round of the latter on Saturday, a vubbornly-contested game resulting in a draw, neither side scoring. W. Rou%hley and Siddal played a good game for the Saints, vv bile the half-backs displayed the Lest football for the home team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>FOOT B L</head>
        <p>FOOT B L</p>
        <p>I HO. G. BUSSEY_&amp; CO., are the leading mak.rs of appli- ances for Football, Hockey, O.ai, Lacrosse and all Sporteadd Games,also for Ciymnasi um and Physieal xerel ses ldustosted Cataloi,ins m Or be obtained from Local Dealers or the SPoras NTANuFicToildr, London 5.%</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>The first away engagement of the Warrington team was with Bradford on Saturday, and the team had to face a fifty-four mile rialway ride and then pad to strip and take the field without any " steetching " of their timba. This, and such a powerful lot as Bradford, were something to face,but right manfully did they set them- elves ttethe task, and though suca a heavy score was pled up against them, it does notrepresent the game tall, as Bradford were by no means 23 points better, than Warrington: The Braatord ground is a, very fine one, and is capable of aceommadating 22,000 people,but ea this number is only reaOted on rare occasions, the aver- use crowd being about,A,ooo, as was the case on Satur- day. Mr. earter, of Halifax, acted as referee, and the following were the players under his charge :—Warring- ton . Boscow, full back ; Barbeie•O'Brien, Burton, and Broady, three-qaarters ; Bate and Fodea, half-back; ; 1 Dakin, Buckley, Berry, Burrows, Saunders, Swift,Balley I and Callaelian, forwards. Bradford : Bitted, full back; Crompton, Cooper, Poppjewell and P elard, three-quar- ,ters Booth and Pawson, ; William Sugden, Walter Sueden.Firth, Ramsden, Batas, Illation, Robert- son and Wilson, forwards. Barber won the toss and set his neponenta to face the sun, and Wiliiam Sugden kicked off. Bailey made his mark, and frog' his kiek, Both ecured and ['lased to Crompton who threw to Popplewell. Crompton again got the ball and sent it over the line, and Firth racing an grounded in the first minute. The try, however, was- offaide,end the ball was brouelit hackeeillaehan now got away, and was supplemented by Barber, who gained considerable ground. Buckley and Dakin by a dribble got to half way. Firth broke away with a rush, and Booth's atrempt to find touch was frustrated by Baseow who sent his opponents back to their own quarters. Bradford again returned to tho attack and Bite spoiled a good run by a had pmts. Nmtral ground was the scene of hostilities for some minutes, well BuekleY got to the born. 25 by a dribble, and paresirg between Bate, B and Broady, saw the latter eaykled just in time. flip Bradford men bestirred themselves at this point, and led by Ramsden and Wilson reached centre, and Pollard picking up the ball rushed to the line, where BlßellW grassed him ha the nick of time. A m unent later, Wm. Sugden rushed over and opened the scoring after a quar- ter of an hour's play. Cooper failed to aizein.mt. Burton dropped out, and after" the usual exchanges, C 'taper, Crampton and Pollard heSedled the ball suc-essively,and the latter was again tackled on -the line, and a minor was conceded immediately after. After the drop out, Warrington looked likeen.siness hut were frustrated by Booth and Pawaon. Pollard again had a run down the field,and fcr a third time was tackled on the line. Bteard then had a drop at goal but onlv a minor reaulted.Cooper now had a sprint hut his c arme was stayed . by Barber under the goal posts where a eerimmage ensiled. Cromp- ton intercepted a pass from Bate, and dropped a pretty goal. After the res'art Brnadv was proFnirent with a run and lone kick,but Brad lord again pressed for a time. Warrington, he waver, returned to the attack and passine between Barber Barron and Broady took play to the home 25, A short visit was. paid to centre, hut Foden getting possession, ran thron2h the thick of his opponents near to the line and then passed to Burrows who almost scored. Broady now had a long drop at goal hut only a minor resul ed, and half-time arrived with Warrington pressing hotly, and the score of Bradford one dropped goal and one try to nit. . Burton re-started, and getting the better of Firth's i return, play was for a time in Bradford lines. William Sugden, however, by a sudden rush got past- Bee,cow 1 and would have scored but for Br-wily and Burton. Ponplewell got away and passed all opposition when Broady caught him and brought him to mother earth. Crompten again had an attempt to scare but Broady ran I him into touch. O'Brien now ran down the field and tratuferred play into the Bradford half. and Bailey. Buckley, Berry, Saunders and Dakin were particularly prominent by dangerous dribbles. After a somewhat prolonged attack, the ball was got over bat only a minor resulted. From the drop out, Conner went off at_. a _ _</p>
        <p>. . great pace' but found Broadv ton quick for him,and the latter with Barber pulled him to toe gnund. But for</p>
        <p>Broady a try would certainly have been scored. A moment later Cooper twisted his ankle and took no further part in the came. Wilson going three-quarter. Just after the restait Pollard rushed over and scored under the goal posts, but Cronan'on failed to improve. Sarin afterwards Robertson got over the line behind the goal posts, and Crompton landed a goal. Bate end Burton put their side on the offensive after the kick off, but Bradford came away again with a rush which Barber pluckily stopped. Robertson and Wilson spoiled a dribble by over-running the ball,but Crompton soon afterwards scored the best try of the afternoon, for gathering up the bail near the cent-re, he darted through the Warrington teem and got over at the corner, Boscow tackling him just as he ground, d. lie took the kick but failed. After I,several scrimmag,,i in Warrington quarters, William Sugden scored his second try which Crompton put the finishing tooth on. The remainder of the game was mork even, but when the whistle blow Bradford had won by three goals (23 points) to Warrington nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINOTON AV. ,BRADFORD A.—The above teams met on theler-poet Ground en Saturday afternoon, before* large number of spectators. The weather was fine and the field in good condition. On commencing the game Lawleas carried the hall up the field, but was stopped near, the visitors' goal line. A number of scrim- 1111.4 P, ensued in which the-hall was gradually worked to the centre of the field. SOMP good passing by the Warringtnn men was witnessed and the hall again went to the visitors' end. More scrimmages took place,and the Warrimenn men allowed themselves to be the sonerior team. They k.pt the ball near the visitors' goal line for same time. Evans eventually got possession. and made A dash, bet he was floored when crossing the lino. Bradford forced the ball down the field and in a scrim- mage at thecentre,Lawleas got the hall and bmke away hut did not run far before he was collared. Several rushes were ma:le by the; Bradford men,but they did not make couch headway, the hall being seot hack to the centre, where scrimmages were of frequent occurrence. Lawless uetting the hall and tieing pressed, kicked up the field. where it was caught by Burns; who made a dash down the field and then kicked, but the hall fell in touch. Both sides began to do some good passing, and for a short time the hall passed from one end of the field to the other. Carer got the ball and paved to Lawless, who was floored a few feet from the line, and the ball rolled between the visitors' goal posts. After a short 'scrimmage, Carey obtained posses,ian, and scored a try, which was not improved. During the passing, Lawless received the hall and made a splendid dash up the field With it and then passed to Edelsten, who, however, was upset near the Bradford line. Play was suspended for a short time, owing to one of the visitors' being winded. On resuming Lawless threw the ball to Carey, but it fell 1 short and the visitors obtained possession and ran it down to the Warringtnn quarters, where it remained until half time was called, a few minutes afterwards. On resuming, Lawless got the ball and made a grand run down the field passing all his opponents and cleposited the ball between the posts. Elelsten took the kick and scored a goal. The visitors now began to play better. Lawless again passed. to Carey, who. kicked down the field, the ba:l -dropping near the visitors full- back (Burns). He ran half way up the field sot,aht tilhtt las itoihe notn,biel nl. a dangerous tnd it to the he w( centre, ein corner: wt oh nhueencar. Lt the The ewtl visitors ieosins e a galine, in nasled to Carey, hut the latter missed.- Lawless, how- recovering the hall and taking it down the field. Houghton picked up avid grounded the ball behind the onB, s. The kick at goal faileci. Lawless handed to Price, who threw to Houghton, hot before he had gone far he Was tm&lt;hed in tnneh near the line, Edelsten re- cei Yin.z the halt. passed it to Evans, who was stopped on the line. L'lwless cot a-vay from a fine out, but spoiled t a good chance by a throw forward. Cross let the visi- tors in. hit the Warrington for wards speedily recovered the lest gratin&amp; Braftford pressed and took the ball up the field, Petting close to the home line before their pro- gress was arres, ed. Warrington were put on the defen- sive by loose play amongst the visitors, but there was aot much danger., aS time Was Called immadiately after, leaving the mere at Warrington one goal 2 tries to nil The following players took Dart :—Warrington : Full back. Cross • threetpiartera, Evans. Edelsten. Lawless and Carey; half baeka, Price and Mason ; forwards, P. Hill. Barrett, Thornton. Savage, Fairhurst, Wilcox, Andrews. and Houghton. Bradford : Full back, Burns ; threevartera, Riwnaley, Waddington, Tye and Jnnea ; half hacks. Grandaos arid Smith; forwards, Follicles. F-arnlvy, Riley. bikes, Sugden, Robinson, Holt and Briggs.</p>
