      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FRODSHAM PETTY .SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM PETTY .SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY..--BefOre Messrs. Charles Reynolds and Thomas Rigby. THE MUZZLING or Dons.—Annie Hutchinson, Main-st., Frndsham, wa* fined for allowing her dog to beet large. —The Chairman said there was an idea that the Rabies order had been withdrawn, and he asked the superinten- dent if there was any truth in the statement.—Superin- tendent Johnson said no definite instructions had yet been received. SCEOOL ATTENDANCE CASES —For not causing their children to attend so 'OOl regularly the following persons from Newtown were fined : Alice Gayter, Thomas Moores, and Thomas lannion. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—George Nicholls, Willington, sear Tarporley. and James Fletcher, Helsby, were sum- moned for being drunk and disorderly on September lfich at lielstiv.—Fined ss. and costs each. IMPORTANT WATER SUPPLY CASE—Mr. A. Brdwne, solicitor, Warrington, made an application on behalf of the Runcorn Rural District Council for an order of entry anon the premises belonging to Mr. James Edwards, Main-qt., Frodsham. for the purpose of taking measure- ments, with a view to having the public water supply connected therewith. In sonnort of his applica- tion Mr. Rravvee wanted section 365 and the Public Health Act, 1875, under which notice had been given. Mr.Ect wards declined to have the water provided by the township, hut the whole nbjact of the legislature in framing the At had been to make every ratepayer. whether willing or unwilling, hear part of the cost of providing public water wcrica. The correspondence which bad passed between Mr. Farrington, inspector of Nuisances for the Council, aid Mr. Edwards was read by Mr. Browne. Mr. Farrington was instructed by the A uthority to take a sample from Mr. Edwards' well. This was frrwarded to the enelyist, and up in his certi- ficate instructions were given hy the Authreity to serve Mr. Edwards with the proper notice. Before doing so M. Farrington wrrte enclosing him a copy of the result of the a,nairi•. ni recomm-nding him to have his house concerted with the rmblic water sunnlv. Mr. El wards replied that they did not. make use of the water frons the well fur domestic purposes, as they otherwise had a good supply. He did not intend to follow out the recommendation. The Council, however, did not think be haila prnrlPr sopply, and a notice was served noon Mr. E I wards that the house was without a suffin.ent sunplv,and that water could he supplied from the publics mains, at s cost not exceeding the scale of charges pro- perly laid down. He. was asked to do the work within A specified lime, anfl warned him that if he failed to do sn the District Council would exscute the work and charge him with the Cost. As the respondent took no rntien of this, Mr F mington again wrote to him point- ing nut that he had not complied with the notice. Under the circumoannes the Council could have caused the rates to be charged to him as though the water had hen supplied. Through the inaction of Mr. Elwards, Mr. Farrington was ordered to get the work done, but this could not he carried out withont the I trinz of tenclemand for these tha quantities of material required was to be stipulated. Mr. Farrington visited the 110t1S9 for the purnnse of obtaining the particulars. hut. Mr. Edwards declined to allow him in do .so.—Mr. Earring- mn.eve evidence aa to asking Mr. Edwards' permission tc enter his premises for the purpose of taking ineesnre- ments in order to opt tenders for the Work to be done, hut was refused. lie now applied for an Order to enter them—By Mr. E lerarcis : When he visited the house he was told there *roe good smartly from a cistern and that they had a large filter P-• Fie had also a supply from the fountain ? Mr. Kiwards Was proceeding to go through s Inne list of persona who did mat use the town's simply, when he was sronned by the Chairman (Mr. Reynolds) who said Oct those did not affect the case before them. They understood that his point was that he had a good supply on the premises,end it would be better if he pro- ceeded to rp•ncte ft.—Mr. Edwards said before doing en he should like to raise the legality of the, establishment of the public water supply in the tnwn.—The Chairman said that had nethingte do with the question at issue.— Mr. E laver is said he objected to:the Bench being ad- vised tar the Clerk 'Mr. Ashton) who was also Clerk to the District Council. and was the head and front in that matter.—Th. Chairman stated that if he was diasatisfied I with their rtilin7 he had his remedy.—Mr. Edwards. ,proceeding, said Mr. Farrington had admPted that several houses in Frodaham were eupplied with rain water by cisterns, and he (Mr. Edwards) had aoah a minpiv.The dimensions of his largest cistern was 2,500 gallons which was equal to according to the scale of the Local Government ;Boasd of a 60 day's sup- ply. In ad 'ition to the cistern he had the fountain which was within 250 feet of his house, and which had . been the prit.eipal supply for years for the township of Frodsham. TI. thought this was within reasonable dig- tance frnm his hong.. The present vr,s a test ease ; there were other parties interested who were in a similar pasition„ and they would consider it a Ereat injustice if th aeneh consented to the tyrannieal and , arbitrary application. His home bad open supplied in the present manner all his lifetime. He ennside,ed there . had been animus displayed towards him by the members of the Council—The Chairman: Are you ening to prove it p—Mr. Edwards : No. Continuing he said it termed • hard that when their forefathers had gone to such ; exly.,oo in providing a simply that this should be cam. • p!e•eic thrnwn a war, and the prearsat owners furred to ennent to the pur,h,so of something which was not _ .</p>
        <p>, really tiouned —Eridenee NCAA then 2 iven by John Tacl,r. employed tw Mr. E iwardP, thet pone ef the well water went inside the tiousc.—The Chairman, after A short consultation. said they had eeeided to make the order They considered that Mr. Edwards had arglicrt the case nut very pleasantly, but they wished he had not referred to the animus of the Council, because they were public servants, sad had sufficient to contend with, withont such statements being made.—Mr. Browne applied for costs, but the Bench declined to allow them. Edwal : The public funds again. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
