      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY (WA-R-li7N-OTT-ON)- Y. -.EARLESTONVN OLYM- PICS.— rhis match was played on the ground of the latter on Saturday. The homesters wou the toss, and decided to play uphill. The game was well contested, each voal being visited in turn, but nothing was scored up to half time. The ball being re-started,the homesters pressed h ird, and were not long before they got two gals to their credit. Final : Earlestown Olympics, four gods; Holy 'Frinity, nil. WAHNINOTONST. MARY'S A.F.C. V. SUTTON COMRINA- TION. —Cit,•3t interest was taken in this match it bring the first league game for both teams. The Saints lost ttif.. toss. awl had to face a blinding sun and the incline. ,twithstanding this they at once assumed the agree- sive, E. Mather opening the scoring for the Saints with n-at screw shot at close quarters. Even play followed. 'Sutton eventually equalised, and half-time arrived with ! the score two each. Sutton restarted and the Saints I having the advantage of the incline quickly got down, the forwards playing a splendid combined game. Richardson completely beat the home custodian with a long swift shot. After this the game became very fast the ball travelling from goal to goal Sutton playing a very &amp;termined game. The pace began to tell on the Saints, arid Sutton managed to put on four goals to St. Mary's one. the game ended Sutton 6, Sc. Mary's 4.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL CASUALS V. WARRINGTON.—This friendly encounter took place on Saturday last on the Anfield ground. Warrington kicked off and having the best of the opening movements were quickly on the aggressive, Wilson passing nicely to Gorton whose shot just missed. Even play followed until the Casuals right were again in evidence but Webster was " all there" with the final effort but was bldly fouled when going well. Selby carried play towards the Casuals' goal. Marshall placed the ball well in front of goal but "the defence being good ‘Yilson was hampered in his attempt and kicked over." Selby and Mather now worked the ball down and forced a corner, but nothing resulted, the whistle blowing for half time with Warrington still attacking. Liverpool I had the best of matters at the restart, Limtnond and Connor upietting successive rushes by the home for- wards. Not to be denied, however, they came again, J. Mather this time doing the needful. Give and take play followed until the Liverpool right obtaining possession Webster was beaten with a good shot. Play was now in , Warrington territory, the defence being severely taxed, the ball eventually going through off one of the War- rington players. Warrington now meant business and urged on by this second reverse quickly took play in front nf the Liverpool goal, a corner being forced. ' This being well placed enabled Jewkes to put the ball turough with his cranium. Warringtoa still kept up the pressure. Webster had to • throw away a hard shot hut to the surprise of players and spectators alike, the referee gave a goal against Warrington alleging that I the goal keeper had taken the ball under the bar in the act of throwing. This unfortunate affair seemed to quire upset the visiting team with the revolt that Liver- I pool quickly notched another goal. Warrington now pol:Pel themselves together Nlarshall kicking well up en- abled Selby to score. Play was very even for the re- , mainder of the gams the whistle blowing with the score L'verpoul 4, Warrington 2. BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESFIIRE COUNTY ASSOCIATION. SENIOR COMPETITION DIVISION.</p>
        <p>SOUTH WARRINGTON V. PENDLETON.—S-Wth ‘Varrington and Pendleton met in the semi-final sonkr a,s)ciation competition at Cheetham on Saturday, and a vigorous battle ensued, in the course of which Warrington succeeded in putting in ten wins- against two by Pen Ileton. The latter team played an uphill wirrlA rhruu,!hout, and four of their players failed to get beyond single•fizure totals. On the other side F Berry, gold medallist, put in the heaviest majority for Ram, and their total return was within eleven shots of the full record number. Score :</p>
        <p>SOUTH WARRINGTON. F Smith II D Wilcock I.' Foyers 2l R Mul.rton W Jelly - 2 l 2l W Webb 2l Fa'rbrother k Thlwarels H ;Town A Kirkpatrick S Bennion 9 Llttlewold B ltfpbinson J Firth A Disney</p>
        <p>PENDLETON.</p>
        <p>J Whitehead l3 P Webb</p>
        <p>Total ...... ...154 Total 241 Maj,iritv. 87 for South Warrington. WARRINGTON NORTH (CEMETERY) v. COBDEN LIBERAL CLUB. KIiOUGHTON.—These teams met in the semi final round in the above competition on Saturday. on the green attached to the Angel Lintel, Stretford. The green was in excellent condition, and the bowling WA4 ex- tremiely good all round. The Cobdel men fancied their chance of lowerinB the colours of the North team, as they have this s,sason dismissed Douzlas in the Cup Com- petition and flarporhey fcr the Association Second Divi- sion nrizs. The Warringtonians, however, meant busi- nes., and by ex,!ellent bowling won the first six matches off the reel, and finally ran out winners with 66 points in their favour, thus becoming entitled to compete in the final round, which takes place on Saturday. Below are the individual slores. Mr. A. Twiss ably performed the duties of referee :</p>
        <p>IVIRRINGTON (NORTH). COBBEN (BROUGHTON). 5 Allison 2l A Hodson (4 Bath .inn. :r 13 Brlmelow (captain) ... 21 J Cnapman l9 J. Ilewltt T Ellison A J Jones lt Farrington W °leave . 2O C Artnitroniz vy wn A 2l F 3hackleton (captain) ... 16 IT Williams R Taylor ITB Atom</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RECOn nrca-oF CHEESE AT</head>
        <p>RECOn nrca-oF CHEESE AT</p>
        <p>WHITCIIII RCA.</p>
        <p>NO IMPROVEMENT IN PRICES.</p>
        <p>At the monthly cheese fair, held at Whitchurch on Wednesday, there was a record pitch, fully 100 tons, an increase or 30 tons compared with the same fair List year. Taken as a whole, the quality was gold. Huyers were unusually numerous. several never having visited the town heiore, and the demand was better than for the past two months, without, however, any increase in roicP. Last month the highest quotable price was 47s 61.. a hitherto unheard-of occurrence, but on Wed- nesday the higheat figure was 475. and that for one lot only, a farmer from near Market Dray/on being the " man. A few lots of the finest Cheshire made from 435. to 455., but the bulk of the hest cheese was sold at from 38s. to 41s. medium 31s. to 375. and lower grades from 21s.</p>
        <p>SEWING Kansas g 8 Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome w +lna cover.—l and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18. Sankey-it.. Warrington.—Adtd.</p>
      </div>
