      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>PETER WALKER &amp; SON, LTD., C.C. IST XI. V. Los- TOM GRALAAL—PIayed on P.W. &amp; Son's ground on Saturday. Result : PETFIR WALKER &amp; SONS. LOSTOCK GRALAM. W AAhton b sr nstran4 ... 12 Parkes b Ashton 0 E Al en run out 0 Thllinglon run out J Caine b Thllington W Brookes b Billington ... 7 Pools c Boiton b Todd 1 C Unsworth b Parkes J Bolton a Morris b Parkes 0 Pendlebury c Ashton b T Todd b Par res l9 Todd 9 J Tilling b Poole l4 Worsley c Brookes b 1M Glennon bPo de 0 Ashton , A Ayling not out 1 lloward b Todd 1 Johnson c Pendlbury b Gibbon b Ashton Poole 0 Cowburn not out H Parkes run out 0 Extras 2 Extras 2</p>
        <p>Total 6l</p>
        <p>Total 2O</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>This club wound up their season on Saturday by a match between a team selected by Mr. C. W. Emmett and 15 captained by Mr.J.Ogden. The match was moat enjnyable, and ended in a draw, the score being as un- der :</p>
        <p>MR. OGDEN'S TEAM. MR. EMMETT'S TEAM. T Marsh c Huddleston b Miller b Harrison . 11 Aid rson J Marsh b Emmett 0 C Emmett not out 33 0 Hipwood c tiuddieston 4 Dennis c Hipwood b b Emmett 2 Williams J Barr It Emmett 1 W Huddleston not out W Hatton not out J. ShufflebottombEtnmett 2 W Harrison „ 0 J Davies „ 10 J Ogden c Emmett b Miler J Huddieston c Dennis b 1 Emmett 5 J Gibbons It Alderson 3 W Williams „ W Gilmour „ 3 Seddon c Huddleston b Alderson 6 J Anderton b Alderson i Extras l3 Extras 3</p>
        <p>_ For 3 wkts ...... 84</p>
        <p>For 15 wkt5...132</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the game the players adjourned to the Viaduct Hotel, where they and several friends sat down to an excellent knife and fork tea. There were , about 41 present who did full justice to the good things provided. After the cloth had been removed the subse- sequent proceedings were presided over by Mr. C. W. Emmett, Councillor Gilmour filling the vice-chair. The usual loyal toasts were duly honoured, the other toasts being '; The Club," " The Captains," " Non-Players," and "The Visitors." During the evening songs were given by Messrs. Huddleston, Williams, Gibbons,Delves, Shuffiabottom, John Seddon, G.W.Dennis,James Seddon, S. Swan, and F. Alderson, recitation by .f. Miller, and selections on shepherd's pipe by J. Shuffiebottom. A very pleasant and sociable evening was spent, which was suitably terminated by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
