      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FROM THE BENCH.</head>
        <p>FROM THE BENCH.</p>
        <p>Some years ago a judge was on circuit who was noted for the sarcasm which he dispensed during his adminis- trations of justice. On one occasion a young man was tried for stealing a pocket-book. The next case was for murder. The evidence in the larceny case was slight, but in the other seemed to the judge conclusive. To his amaz-mmt and wrath, however, the jury convicted the young man and acquitted the murderer. In passing sentence upon the convicted thief, after the discharge of the other prisoner, the judge : " Young men, you have not been in this country long?" " No, your honour," replied the prisoner. "I thought not," said ' the judge ; " you don't know these people ; you may kill them, but don't touch their pocket-books." On another occasion, when the evidence seemed to point cmclusively to the prisoner's guilt, but when the judge, from long experience, distrusted the jurymen's wisdom, the counsel for the defendant said : "It is better that ninety-nine guilty persons should escape than that one innocent man should suffer." In his charge to the jury. the judge admitted the soundness of this proposition, but he added, impressive and severely : " Gentlemen, I want you to bear in mind that ninety-nine have al- ready escaped."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MR. GLADSTONE, M.P., AND COLONEL</head>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE, M.P., AND COLONEL</p>
        <p>NORTH.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. J. Gladstone, M.P., speaking on Saturday at a garden party at Ciledhow Hali, the residence of Sir J. Kitson, M.P., said that at the general election he had had a hard fight. He was quite proud of that fight. I Before that fight he was quite unknown to the people of I this country. Since that fight he had found that by ' virtue of having had such a distinguished oppsment— , (laughter)—he had received a little reflected glory upon himself. (Laughter.) Colonel North received this con- spicuous tribute during the election, that his speeches were not only reported—(laughter)—verbatim by the Leeds papers, as was to be expected, but they were re- ported verbatim over the length and breadth of the laud, and there was not a single man who'could say the same—(laughter)—whether it were Sir William Harcourt or Mr. Artnur Balfour himself. (Laughter.) Be had been spending a few weeks in the far north of Scotland, and he had found the keenest interest, even in that re- mote quarter, in his contest in West Leeds. When he was speaking to gillies, stalkers, keepers, and shepherds, they at once began to talk about that contest. " Eli, ma-an, he was a queer one to fight against "—(loud laughter)—they would say, and he had to glee them a full account of his contest. He was not sure whether he ought not to express to his friends in West Leeds his profound sympathy with them in the dis- appointment they must have experienced when they heard that he and not Colonel North had been returned. When he considered what West Leeds had lost by the defeat of Colonel North he felt most apologetic. Be was in the most difficult position, hardly knowing bow in the future he should be able to justify the choice of the constituency. 'lO begin with, they all knew, on the authority of an enterprising tradesman in West Leeds, that the whole of the electors lost on the result of that election a fine cut of beef from the whole ox which was to be roasted, and every elector lost, he believed, in addition, a bottle of wine which was to be given when Colonel North was returned. And if they could swallow that disappointment, a great deal yet remained. They would not get the" orders "—("Oh," and laughter)— and how much loss that meant to West Leeds he left it to them to determine. But it was an extraordinary election. He did not think they could find an instance ot a more glaring attempt to corrupt a constituency. Colonel North did not say, "I will see that justice is done ii the giving of Government orders as between West Leeds and other constituencies of the country. which have an equal right to be considered." (Hear, hear.) What he said was, " When you return me I will give you orders ; I will see that you get Government orders." He did not say what orders. There was no limit to this boundless field opened out to them. Every manufacturer, every tradesman, every man of business in West Leeds had this golden prospect opened out to him—boundless, unlimited, vast beyond human compre- hension. But it did not do. (Cheers.) " Farewell, little blooming bud" was placarded about the walls, but the electors had some'hing to say, and it was no " fare- well." (Laughter and cheers ) They triumphed over their opponents, and he was doubly proud of his consti- tuency because it was not an ordinary fight. Some people thought that Colonel North's candidature was a pieze of toolery,but they knew the solid methods,under- iving all the tomfoolery, which were at work throughout the division. They triumphed over it all, thanks to common sense and solid Liberalism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>HAS "JACK THE RIPPER" BEEN FOUND?</head>
        <p>HAS "JACK THE RIPPER" BEEN FOUND?</p>
        <p>DR. FORBES WINSLOW'S OPINION.</p>
        <p>That well-known specialist on madness, Dr. Forbes Winslow, is at present in New York, where he has gone to attend a Medico-legal Congress, and will read a paper on " Suicide considered as a Mental Epidemic." 'fliis is, says " Science Siftings," the first time, we be- lieve, that there has been a Medico-legal Congress any- where, and its utility is very manifest. Dr. Forbes Winslow will preside over the department of insanity and mental medicine. He will be particularly useful to the Congress, for he has qualified both in medicine and law. On his way out to the States a reporter took the opportunity of interviewing the doctor, and he made a most interesting statement about "Jack the Ripper," who by his crimes terrorised London a few years ago. The wretched creature, it appears, is incarcerated in a county lunatic asylum in England, and those horror- mongers who have been speculating as to his fate may now rest satisfied. Dr. Winslow says this fact is known to the authorities, but they have hushed up the case. The speciality of Dr. Winslow has been insanity, and particularly cases of murder, where the perpetrator has been insane.</p>
        <p>"As an expert witness," says the Doctor, " I have testified in an enormous number of cases and won them all save one. In that case a man killed his wife and then cut his own throat. Be missed killing himself by the merest fraction. Had he succeeded, the verdict would have been murder and suicide while temporarily insane. He was, however, nursed back to life and tried. I testi- fied that the man was insane, but the jury thought *therwiss. lie was found guilty of murder and banged.</p>
        <p>„ "Insanity is very largely on the increase in England. There are now 94,000 insane persons in England, and the number is increasing at the rate of 2,000 a year. To what do I attribute the cause? Drink, undoubtedly. It IS a sad fact, but nevertheless true, that the drinking habit is on the increase in England, and particularly among women. They do not do their drinking at the table. "Mrs. Major So-and-so does not drink anything —oh, dear, no; but she has probably got a bottle con- cealed under the mattress in her bedroom. This class of dypsomaniacs is the most dangerous and the most cun- ning. The evil is largely attributable to the fact that under our system alcohol can be purchased from the grocer, and the consumption among women of cherry brandy, far instance, obtained through the medium stated, is enormous. Our inebriate law is a dead letter, and I find it necessary to send my patients to asylums in Belgiusa..</p>
        <p>"As to suicide, I am rather loath to discuss it, since that is the subject upon which I have prepared a paper to read at the forthcoming congress. In that paper I shall treat ot what diseases induce a disordered brain to commit suicide. My main areal- meat will be that a suicide is insane and irres- pon4ble. In my opinion, all suicides are insane. At the same time, there is no doubt that many suicides oc- cur as the result of imitation. If a man jumps off the Monument in London on Monday, two will repeat the act on Tuesday. ''l take the view that suicide is a complaint which is not te be regard as premeditated. It is an impulsive act, committed on the spur of the moment. Suicidal acts comes in waves, spasmodic generally. The statistics of suicide compiled recently in England show a recent enormously high percentage. I consider that this is due to the epid imic of influenza that swept ever England. All suicides are insane, and no responsibility, moral or legal, attaches to their taking off." Dr. Winslow related some of his experiences with in- sane patients, and the many times his life had been threatened. In this manner the conversation got around to " Jack the Ripper," of whom the doctor said : " Jack the Ripper" was a medical student, of good family. He was a young man,of slight build,with light hair andiblue eyes. He studied very hard, and his mind, being natur- ally weak,ga've way. He became a religious enthusiast and attended etrly service every morning at St. Paul's. His religious fervour resulted in homicidal mania to- wards the women of the street, and impelled him to murder them. He lodged with a man whom I knew,and suspicion was first directed towards him by reason of the fact that he returned to his lodgings at unseasonable hours; that he had innumerable coats and hats stained with blood. I have in my possession now a pair of Canadian moccasins stained with blood that the Rip- ' per 'wore while on his murderous expeditions. I noti- fied the Scotland Yard authorities, but at that time they refused to co-operate with me. Subsequently the young man was placed in confinement and removed to a luna- tic asyluel,where be is to-day. Since his incarceration there has been no repetition of the horrible murders that he perpetrated. These facts are all known to the English authorities, and it is conceded that the man now in the asylum is 'Jack the Ripper.' It was deemed desirable however to hush the matter up. The details were too hr.rrible to be made the subject of a public trial, and there is no doubt of the men's hopeless in- sanity. And that tempts me to say that there is no such thing as a hirmless lunatic. Ail lunatics are dangerous, and should be kept in confinement. The man who be- lieves that there is such a thing as a harmless lunatic is labouring under a delusion bimself." •</p>
      </div>
