      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>ROOST FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Warringtornans suffered a severe defeat at Brad- ford on Saturday, but the score is out of all proportion to the play. Warrington were not overpowered by any means by the Bradfordians, though the latter were the superior team. When Bradford come to Warrington on January 18th, it is very improbable that they will return with anything like the victory of last Saturday, even if they win at all.</p>
        <p>Bradford were the better team in the forward line. It was here where Warrington were beaten. The Bradford forwards are like so many Fair Turners or Tosh Thorni- leys, and therefore they can push to some purpose. In the tight packs Warrington were overcome, but in the loose, Warrington did not show badly. Buckley was about the best forward and he worked like a Trojan. Callaghan and Burrows also shone out. The rest also worked with a will, but had to many odds against them.</p>
        <p>At half back" Smack" Bate was very prominent, but Foden was scarcely sharp enough. One run of his, how- ever,from half way line almost to the line was a very fine effort. The three-quarter line had plenty of defending to do, and Barber and Burton were very prominent in this department. Broady deserves great credit for the manner he tackled his opponents. He was the best runner on the field on Saturday, and by his speed he saved at least three certain tries, the way he overhauled Cooper on one occasion being a treat to see. O'Brien would do much better if he would pass a little more. He can defend all right, but this want of passing spoils his play. Boscow was well tested on Saturday, and came out of the ordeal well. The game all through was a very fast one, and fought with great friendliness on both sides and was in marked contrast to the Hull match. Brad- ford are said to be &amp;ars noirs of Hull, but if they always play with the same spirit as they played on Saturday, they will be the favourites of Warrington.</p>
        <p>The other matches in the Northern League were very interesting. Although Bradford beat Warrington by 23 points to nil, this was not the heaviest defeat, for Run- corn took 27 points to nil out of Rochdale Hornets. Tyl- desley rather surprised Oldham by defeating tie latter at Oldham by 11 points to 3, and Oldham are not mak- ing the show they expected, although on Monday they journeyed to Huddersfield and gained their first victory by 16-6. Bradford had every reason to be satisfied with themselves this week end, for on Monday they put on 10 points to nil against Stockport, and thus had a total of 33 points to nil.</p>
        <p>The splendid victory which the Runcorn first team gained over their opponents on Saturday, gives en- couragement to hope that they will give a satisfactory account of themselves at Manningham on Saturday. Of course the Yorkshire club have a far better reputation than the Rochdale Hornets team, who were the oppon- ents of the Runcornians last week, but the following team to represent Runcorn should be strong enough for them to finish on the right side. Full back, J. Butter- worth; threequarter backs, T. Warder, G. Robinson, El. Myers, O. Dunbavin ; half backs, S. Abrams and J. Faulkner; forwaids, W. Faulkner, J. Langley, H. Far- mer, F. Gayter, H. Williams, S. Walker, T. Moores, and S. Johnson.</p>
        <p>Widnes did not expect to win at Halifax, but they made a very good show, only being beaten by a penalty goal. Wigan, however, could hardly expect Wakefield Trinity to run up a score of 13 points against them. They had previously swept the boards, and had not a single point scored against them. Their average is now considerably spoiled,and now instead of being among the top sawyess they are only the tenth.</p>
        <p>A glance at the table shows that Bradford are going ahead and are now two points clear of the rest. Of course they have played one more game than the majority of clubs, but then a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush. Thy win ablre at home and away,and at the end of the season they will not be far off the top.</p>
        <p>Warrington are very low down at present, but they ought not to stop there. They have had to meet some powerful clubs so far, and could hardly expect to beat Bradford, though they ought to have beaten Hull.</p>
        <p>The following is the Northern club league table up to the present time : Pts. Pts. Pts. Pld. Won. Lost. Dr . For. Ag'st. Bradford Halifax Leeds St. Helens ... Beghouse Ran 3 ... 2 ... 1 ... ... 18 ... 3 ... 4 ' Runcorn 3 2 1 . 42 9 4 Leigh Tyldesley Hunslet Wigan 3 2 1......21 ... 13 ... 4 Batley 4 1 . 2......18 .17 3 Beght'n Ran 3 ... 1 ... 2 ... ... 18 ... 23 ... 2 Oldham Widn.s Wakefield T 3 ~. 1 ... 2 ... ... 13 .. 30 ... 2 Manningham 3 ... 1 ... 2 ... ... 5 ... 20 ... 2 Warrington Huddersfield 3 ... 1 ... 2 ... ... 18 ... 27 ... 2 Rochdale H. Li versedge 3 0 3 0 11 0 Stockpot t</p>
        <p>The following are the present positions of the Lan- cashire and Cheshire section :</p>
        <p>Pts. Pt.. Pts PM. Wn. Lost. Drn. For. Aest. St. Helens 2 • • 0 0 .13 ... 3 ... Runcorn Wigan 1 . 0 0 9 0 Tyldesley Leigh Stockport Oldham 1 Widnes</p>
