      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>STRONGEST FORT.</head>
        <p>THE STRONGEST FORT.</p>
        <p>In the World is only equal to its weakest part : the strength of the strongest chain to its weakest link, and the capabilities of the healthiest man must only he ad- judged by the weakest vital organ. The circulation may be good ; the brain active; the muscles wiry and supple, and the lungs sound ; but let disease attack the kidneys or liver and see how quickly the appetite becomes poor, the step less elastic and firm, the face pallid, aches and pains that were strangers appear frequently, and grad- ually the man or woman, who gave promise for long years of health, becomes helpless. Do not be deceived by appearances, nor neglect the warnings of nature— the seemingly insignificant symptoms—but fortify your- self by resorting to the remedy that has achieved more cures from kidney and liver troubles than any other in the world. An Edinburgh lady, Miss Ann Dick- son, of 81, Lothian-road, whose years measure beyond the three score and ten, slys :—" I am a septuagenarian whose brain is active, and who inherits a temperament that—although not unwholesomely nervous—may per- haps he hest tabulated as " nervous-bilious." Past and present experience of the efficacy of Warner's Safe reme- dies, ensure thoughtfulness with respect to the necessity of keeping Warner's S,fe Cure and Warner's Safe Pills always near me, and I feel verv'grateful for the salutary and beneficial effects derived from their use."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>POLO MATCH AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>POLO MATCH AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Baths, Legh-street, on Tuesday evening, the serni-final match in connectisn with the Liverpool and District Polo Lea.lie took place, when the Warrington team tried conclusions with Liverpool Gymnasium. There was a good attendance. In introducing Professor Arthur Barlow. manager of the baths, to the audience— if it may he called an introduction—Mr. Grace said that although the swimmer was getting on in years he would endeavour to show that he was as efficient in the art as ever. The professor then jumped into the water with the intention of attempting to break the 50 yards' record for a valuable prize to be presented by a Warrington gentleman, hut although he swam well and powerfully, he failed to lower the record. The polo match was then entered upon,theteanis being as follows —Warrington:: Goal, Dixon: backs, Beaman and Turner; half bark. Scetson ; forwards, Grieghton, Locker and Ashton. Liverpool Gymnasium : Goal, E. C. Savage; backs, T. Coole, A. E. Perkins ; half hacks, J. S. Gomarsall ; for- wards, 11. Condor, F. D. Nevin, and J. Nicholls. It was 8 35 when the men entered the water, Warrington to de- fend the deep end. The first goal was obtained by Nevin in a couple of minutes. A fierce struggle now ensued, each team playing remarkably well, and it was not long , before Nevin sent in a second goal, Season, at 8.50. WESLEYAN CHAPEL TJARVEST FESTIYAL.—This annual scored,amid cheers, the first goal for Warrington, in fine festival was held on Thursday evening last. The chapel style, and shortly afterwards half time was called, the had been richly decorated with flowers and fruits. The -score reading—Liverpool, two goals Warrington, one. Rev. C. de P. Glidden, of Runcorn, preached from the Warrington had now, of course, a decided advantage in words, "Are not two sparrows sold for one farthing?" having to defend the shallow end. Five minutes having and Are not five sparrows sold for two farthings ?" clanged, kghtoc scored a goal for Warrington, thus Special hymns and the anthems, " Praise, 0 Praise our equalising matters. Ashton followed up his success by God and King" (Hall) and "Ye shall dwell in the land" scoring two other goals. Locker now added a point. for (Stainer), were well rendered by the cnoir. Mr. R. B. the locals, and when the whistle sounded time the result ignagreoe, of Runcorn, the Frodshani Parish Church was a win for Warrington by five goals to Liverpool coganist, efficiently accompanied. wo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Z.ettero to tbe etiitor.</head>
        <p>Z.ettero to tbe etiitor.</p>
        <p>MR.. COUNCILLOR SKELTON AND HI3 VOTE. Sic—Referring to Mr. Councillor Skelton's claim at the Warriagton Revision Court,l shall be glad if you will kindly reprint the report of the case referred to which appeared in your column of September 17th, 1892, a copy of which I enclose.—Yours truly, JOHN CLAYTON,</p>
        <p>Liberal Agent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>[COPY.]</head>
        <p>[COPY.]</p>
        <p>CLAIN TILESSRS. SITELTON.—A LIMITED COMPANY AND TKEIR REGISTERED OFFICE. MT.W.P.Skelton applied that himself and his brother, r. W. Skelton, might be placed on the list with respect to premises in Horsemarket-Bt. Mr.Hampson supported and Me. Browne opposed the application. In reply to Mr. Browne, the claimant said the concern was registered in Martin as a limited company, but the company had not yet taken poesession of the prernises.—Mr.Browne : And yet ties word " limited" is up over the door, and the words "registered office" of the company on a brass plate, and then you say they haven't taken possession. —The Claimant: I say they have not taken possession of ether the business or the premises.—Mr. Browne: Although it has been registered five months, and you bays put over your door "W.P.and W.Skelton,Limited." the registered office of the company there P—The Claimant: Tee registered office of the company is there. —Mr.Browne : In what name are the premises rated? --The Claimant: The prospectus has not been issued yet.—Mr. Browne said this was an extraordinary case. BETE were some gentlemen turning their business into a limited company and putting the words "limited "and " registered office" over the door and nobody could tell Who WRS the company.— The Claimant: Simply 'because it is compulsory to put that up from the date of the register.—His Honour said the company might have a registered office there without being tenants. The company had no actual existence at the present moment. He allowed the claim.</p>
        <p>A HUMBUGGING WORLD. Sir,—" Tis a mad world, my masters, and motley is the only wear." With this sentiment I cannot alto4e- Vier agree. At least, if the world is mad,there is a kind of method in its madness. Carlyle thought it to be largely a foolish world; and certainly the man who 'speculates upon the basis of its containing a large per- seentage of folly is not likely to lose much. But be this as it allay one thing is certain—the world consists largely .of humbugs and the humbugged. For instance, here we have " boa" complaining of our member having, so far, neglected to fulfil some of big ,prernises, as though he was not aware of the fact that " Lurnhug " is the chief stock-in-trade of the poli- tician—to he witliont which ensures the lacking one of the certainty of remaining as a permanency in the in- teresting tout unsatisfactory stage of political chrysalis —an unsuccessful candidate. . . . Yes they all do it. The p, litican. the business man, the doctor, and the divine. they all do it. The Doctor with his pie cebo meclicineand manner, and the divine with his pro- s:cases of a reversionary interest in a world to Come if .only we will take suffitieht pains in this. indeel if you want to see this most interesting quality in fund pw, seek it am ,ng the theologians and the *masa sail. And what are called recognition meetings ace fertile fields for tip production of thl4 amiable, use- ful and lucrative quality. In your report of the Bold- et., function, if we neglect the somewhat fulseme local" trumpet blowing we find little else than humbuL: pure and simple. At least wbatever we may think of the first, the latter adjective tfits it to a " tee," dm simple and easily seen through a deal of it was. As for tire rev, gentleman who was "On view " its is ttut recipient of our unsolicited symrathies ; foe as he himself sugeested, it is a painful position for a Marl to he in when helms to make an exhibition of him- self, with the added embarrassment of having to " hum- tug" the new fl at risk ef depreciating-the old one. 'To a man of f 4,115. mi sensibility it must be very trying to be hound to retail atil maintain the crude imaginings a an uncritical and credulous age before the people of the North so folly rhar,:ed with "sense" and" brains"! 'That his ministry in Warrington may be blessed by an ; increase in these mrett desirable qualities is my fervent ' bope. We shall see. Meantime would it be "captious" to ask what size of a grain of salt is required to swallow I Adam and Eve ? Our good friend, Mr. R. Garnett, deplores the mech. anical Christianity of the day. Now I love to agree with Mr. R. Garnett whenever I find it possible. Herein we are entirely at one. The Christianity of the day is mech- anical—it is cranky ! " Sabbath ' observances, too, were mechanical. Quite so, but does Mr. Garnett's engine, his tools and his workmen "rest "on the Sabbath —the Holy Seventh Day ? He regrets also the absence of that enthusiasm which he remembers of old—forget- ting that "ignorance is the mother of devotion" and that the " schoolmaster is abroad." The singing too ! Well I think myself some little thought might be given to the refection of hymns, as to their appropriateness to the occasion and the singer. For instance to set an elderly gentleman in whose countenance placid content- ment with this world, and its dealings with himself, shines most conspicuously, to sing "I live only to mourn my sins" is, one would think, enough to upset the gravity even of the most densely pious.—Yeurs,kc., N :EBBS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>-SENSATIONAL MURDER TRIAL AT SAN F RAN CISCO.</head>
        <p>-SENSATIONAL MURDER TRIAL AT SAN F RAN CISCO.</p>
        <p>-- GRUESOME DISCOVERIES IN A CHAPEL. The "San Francisco Chronicle "just to hand announces that the Durant trial, one of the most sensational of recent times, had just entered upon its eighth week. Theodore Durrant, the superintendent of the Emmanuel Baptist Library and Sunday school, is charged with the murder of two young ladies, Miss Minnie Williams and Miss Blanch Lamont, members of the congreeation, whose bodies wire found in the chapel, last Easter. About this time the janitor of Emmanuel Chapel went into the building to prepare for the service, and was astonished to find open the door ef the Sunday school library, which as a rule, was not only closed, but locked. Entering the room a ghastly sight presented itself. On the floor lay the body of a young lady with her clothes diearranged,gaping wounds on several parts on her hod y,and a handkerchief tied tightly round her neck. The floor was covered with blood,and by the side of the corpse was a broken table knife with which the terrible wounds bad been inflicted. The horrified official raised an alarm, and the girl was almost immediately recognised as Miss Minnie Williams, a member of the cangregation,who had left home on the previous evening with the intention of asaisting in the Easter deaorations of the chapel,and also of changing a hook at the library. The police made inquiries,and suspicion naturally fell on the officers of the church,but most of these were speedily able to clear themselves. Then the fact was recalled that about ten days previously another young lady of the congregation, Miss Blanche Lamont, had mysteriously disappeared, leaving no clue behind, and it was decided to make a thorough search of the building. Every nook and cranny was carefully gone over without result, and the last place to be visited was the belfry. The door at the bead of the stairs was found to be locked, and some hesitation was felt at breaking it open, as it was be.. tiered to be an act of sacrilege. At last, however,orders were given for the doors to be broken open, and that being done, Miss Lamont's mysterious disappearance was at once explained. On the Boor, with every article of clothing removed, lay the young girl's body, the hands folded across her breast, and the head supported on a wooden block. She also had been strangled, and the body afterwards posed in the manner in which it was found. Further inquiries were made, and the re- sult was the arrest of Theodore Durrant, the superin- tendent of the library and Sunday school. He, it was alleged, was intimately acquainted with both young ladies, and had been in the company of Miss Williams on the evening before her body was found. Miss Lamont, who was about twenty years of age, was staying with relatives at San Francisco while she was being trained as a school teacher, and she was on very friendly terms with.Durrant. He met her as she lel t the class on the evening when she disappeared, and several people saw them walk away together. Durrant has shown the utmost callousness since his arrest, and when taken to the scene of the murders displayed not the slightest in- terest in the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>HARVEST THANKSGIVING HERVICFA.—These services in connection with Weston Church were held On Friday and Sunday. For the occasion the church had been artistically decorated by Mesdames Bagnall, Simcock, Mather, and Joseph Bank es ; Misses Kelly/right, Mitchi- son, Bankes, Armstrong, Farrel. Gregory, Aaron,Hough, Basnett, T weddle, Moore, and Peacock, assisted by the Rev. W .Sta hles,Messrs.Ja mes Ford,Pin.,John Mather, A. Peacock, Whitby, and Wale. at Friday evening's service the preacher was the Rev. W. Hodgson, vicar of Widnes, who based the discourse on " We are God's Husbandry." On Sunday morning the Rev. Canon Woed occupied the pulpit, in the afternoon the Rev. W. B. Stables conducted a children's service. la the even- ing the preacher was the Rey. H. B. Blogg, vicar of Frodaham. Special anthems and hymns were sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>FRODSHAit.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAit.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 28. 1895.</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR).</p>
        <p>The harvest festival which the Salvation Army celebrated during the early part of last week, and the latter end of the preceding week, was of a per- fectly successful character, from every point of a Salvationist's view. hxtraordinary preparations were made, and the barracks presented the appear- ance of a florist's shop on a large scale. To help forward the affair, a captain and lieutenant, two ladies who were stationed at Earlestown on the in- ception of the corps, were brought down from Scot- land, and there is no doubt their presence was of great assistance to the local members. The services were conducted throughout with great heartiness, and several people were persuaded to pay a visit to the penitent form. The only drawback, from my point of view, was the band, which turned out on the Sunday morning at the unearthly hour of half- past-six, at a time when peaceful citizens are en- joying a final nap. However, the bandsmen no doubt do their best, and what they lack in musical ability they make up in zeal. Next week the self- denial week in connection with the Army takes place, when all sympathisers by denying them- selves some little luxury, and giving its equivalent' in money to the Army, will assist in the good work they undoubtedly perform.</p>
        <p>The water question still agitates many of the good people in this district. I say " many " ad- visedly, because the remainder don't care a rap how the question is settled, so long as they get a good supply of water at a ctietin rate. The agitation is chiefly, if not entirely confined to Newton, and its representatives, who seem to think that they can secure a better supply at as cheap a rate as it is ob- tained from the London and North Western Rail- way Company. Certain correspondence has passed between the Council and Mr. Webb of the London and North Western Railway Company from which it appears that there was an intention to lay down large mains but because of the agitation which has sprung up the work has been delayed. What the intentions of the Council are lam not in a posi- tion to say, but I think such a question as this should be approached with some caution. It it is proposed to lay down pumping plant, this means a serious outlay of money,and if it is intended to con- nect with the Liverpool Corporation pipes, I don't think there is any positive assurance that the water will be of any better quality, or the supply any more regularly maintained. Any way,l don't th nk any departure should be made from existing ar- rangements, without the sanction of a majority of the ratepayers.</p>
        <p>I have not heard auytuing lately of the Commons Protection Society, which it was said was inaugur- ated in Earlestown a few weeks ago, beyond the announcement that such a society had been formed. Doubtless they are working quietly and do not intend to proclaim their doings with the blare of trumpets or from the housetops. All the same, I should like to know what their intention is. They tell me that it is proposed to abolish the races and make the Common into a recreation ground and 'lark, and he grand stand into a beautiful conser- vatory stocked with choice plants. But will this ever be? lam afraid not.</p>
        <p>I hear a movement is on foot to form a Liberal Club, and doubtless before these notes reach sour readers this will have become an accomplished fact. And why net? There is a splendid site, that which during the election, was occupied as the Central Liberal Committee rooms, and I should say the building is admirably adapted for the purpose. Properly organised, and capably managed, there are sufficient Liberals in the town to support the club and make it a success. The need of such a club is much felt,and I cannot predict anything but a promising future for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>REVISION COURT.</head>
        <p>REVISION COURT.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Mr. James T. Foard, Revising Barrister,held a court at the Town mall, Newton, on Thursday, for the purpose of revising the Parliamentary voters lists for the town- ships of Newton in-Makerfield, Burtonwood, Croft, and Houghton-with-Arbury. Mr. R. W. Renshaw and Mr. Henry Taylor represented the Liberals, and Mr. W. Holland Owen, solicitor, and Mr. W. Mavrdsley appsared for the Conservatives. The first township dealt with was Newton-in-Maker- fiel , including Earlestown and Newton, and was taken in wards. There were a large number of claims and objections,but with one or two exceptions they were only of the ordinary kind. Daniel 0 wen ,133,Wargrave-road,E arlestow n , preferred an ownership claim in riepect of the house which he on- ' :upied. The Conservatives objected. Mr. Holland Owen said the house was built by the Earlestown Co-operative Socitty, and was allotted to the claimant by ballot. The Society held the deeds, and until he paid the mortgage the Society WAS the owner of the property.—Mr.Renshaw said the whole purchase money was a mortgage.—The Barrister: The Society do not surrender their rights until the whole amount is paid.—Mr. Renshaw : He has been buying this house all along.—The Barrister said the Society did not surrender their rights until the mortgage money was paid. The claimant was in the position of a mortgagee paving off certain sums of money to obtain possession. The ownership lay in -the Co-operative Society, and until the money was paid off he did not become the owner.—Mr. Holland Owen said it was quite clear that the Society was the owner. Thsy did not part with the deeds until the who7e of the mortgage was paid.—The Barrister perusing the book in which the repayments were made,said the book did not suggest that the Co-operative Society was the owner,or that they kept the deeds. He w-mid like to see a copy of the rules. A copy being handed up to hiin,he said it looked totim very much like an ordinlry mortgage. The Society were the mortgagees and Mr. Owen was the mortgagor,and he allowed the vote. . . . .</p>
        <p>John Goff living in King-st., was objected to by the LiberaTs for a j,int ownership CiAIM in respect of seven houses in Flawthnrn Terrace, Crow-lane, Nawton. Mr. George Goff appeared to represent his father, and stated that he owned two of the houses, and his father the other five. . . . .</p>
        <p>The Barristers said they were not joint owners in that sense. It was admitted that the deeds for each lot of houses were separate and distinct, and the claim was disallowed.</p>
        <p>The Misses Greenall, St. Flelens-road,Newton, &amp;aimed as joint owners of property in St. fielens-road, and were objected to by the Conservatives. They did not appear to support the rlaim. which was disallowed. Thomas Latimer claimed as owner ot a house in Cross. lane, Earlestown. The Liberals objected. There was a mortgage with the Earlestown Perfect Thrift Building Society on the house, and it being shown that lie had not sufficient interest in the property, the claim was dis- allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>EVENING SITTING.</head>
        <p>EVENING SITTING.</p>
        <p>A court was held in the evening for the convenience of wotkinz mea.S,-!veral uninterestinv claims and objections were dealt with. Much amusement was caused over the lists of removals. The Revisina Barrister called out Thomas Potts. " Dead " replied the agent. "Oh," said the Barristor, " That will not satisfy me,l want to know where ha is." This was a problem which nobody attem- pted to solve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>KICKING CASE AT CROFT.</head>
        <p>THE KICKING CASE AT CROFT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court, on Tuesday, before the Rev. G. F. Roberts, William Collier, labourer, Croft, was charged with having on the 3rd August committed grievous bodily harm upon James Waywell. Mr. Browne appeared for the pri- soner, and Mr. Knowles for the prosecutor.—The latter stated that he was a labourer living at Little Town, Croft. On the evening in question he left the General Elliot Hotel at closing time, and on the way home, in a quiet spot, he overtook the pri- soner, who struck him across the nose and knocked him down. While on the ground Collier kicked him until be (witness) became unconscious. He Aid not recollect anythiug until the next morning at four o'clock, when he bound himself at home. He did not go to work until a fortnight after the affair. He still felt the effects of the injuries. lie had no given Collier any provocation.—By Mr. Browne: He bad been convicted four or live times for various offences, lie had been sent to prison without the option of a fine. —lsaac Main, labourer, Croft, deposed to seeing prisoner "thump" Way- well and kick him on the back several times. lie told prisoner to leave off, but he took no notice and kicked prosecutor again. Witness then went away. —Other evidence was called, and ultimately, alter a lengthy hearing, the prisoner was committed for trial.</p>
        <p>A CARGO OF APPLE.;</p>
        <p>„ .... ... A small schooner, the Sarah Jane, of 53 tons net regis- ter, has arrive,' at the Pomona D )cks, laden with a full cargo, soma 80 tons in all, of apples from Levivier, Jersey. This is interesting as being the first full cargo of apples ever imported to Manchester, although many consignments have been received by the reeular steamers from France and Holland. It is also, it is stated, the first fruit to arrive by a sailing vessel. Th.' shipment is of an experimental character, and, should the expecta- tions of the importers be realised, will be followed by others on a semew hat extensive scale.</p>
        <p>HOUSR/SOLD LINE•SS hemmed and marked free ofaeharge.— and W. Dutton. Warrington.-4 dra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-28_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Contineed from page 3. THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The inquest was held in tile Parade room of the Police I, Station on Weducsdoy afternoon by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner. Mr. T. Birtles was the foreman of the jury. Mr. J. Lyou Wu=le (town clerk) was present on uehalf of the police, and Mr. Hatton (Robert Davies and Cu.) represented Messrs. tireeuail, Wuitley, and Co., the owners of the Stakespeare Inn. Mr. S. Hunt (chief constable) was also present. The interest which was telt 10 um case was suown by the large crowd which as- sembled in the street outside the police station: Previ- ous to meeting in the parade room, the coroner and jury . visited Cue scene of the tragedy so as to be familiar with toe arrangements of use house and its surroundings. Alter the jury had been sworn, the Corwoer said that they had met to make inquiry into the death of William Green, whose body the jury nail seen. roue was a luau in custody cuarged with having caused the death of the deceased, a Mall whose name seemed to be &amp;Sward John Widocks—nut Edward Cartwright. • The Town Clerk watched the proceedings on behalf of the police, and Mr. /lotto's fur the owners of the house. The Coroner then turned to the prisoner and said :—Are you repre- sented legally? .No sir.—You have had the opportunity et getting legal assistanae it you thought tie? U yes.— You know the jury are making inquiry into the death of William Green. You are in custody cnarged with hav- ing caused his death. Have you not thought it advisable to nave a legal man? I did not think so.—l should be more pleased it you nail been professioually represented; if your interests had been looked after oy some solicitor.</p>
        <p>liliS. GREEN'S EVIDENCE:</p>
        <p>Margaret Greta, Cue wile of the deceased man, was then called. Sue said that her husband was tLe licensee ot the 6u4kespeire fun. He was 35 years at age. The Corouer : Du you know Edward John Vviaocks. Mrs. Green shook tier head.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : I inean the man who goes by the name ,of Edward Cartwright? 0 yes.—He iniorms us that his name is Edward Jona Wictocks. Is he a lodger at your house? Yea.—Huw long has he lodged with you? I should say about five or six months.—VVill you give me names of the tainny who live there? Have you any other lodgers besides Cartwright? There is Mr. Charles Critchley.—Auy other lodgers? No sir, we have a ser- vant Ehea Vernon.—How many children? There are four.—Just take your mind back to Monday last. When did your husband go out? He went out with Critchley and Cartwright batweee eight and nine o'cleck.—What time did they return? .uout ten o'cloa.—Did they come back tegetner ? kes.—.Were you downstairs at the time? I was in the parlour.—Where did they go to? I think they went into the snug.-13 that the place wnere your husband was supposed to be when these injuries were received? Yes.—The stairs divide the snug from the kitchen? Yes.—Tuat was the last time you saw your husband before this happened? Yes. It VMS about 11.30, I should say. My husband, Mr.:Critchley and Mr. Cartwright were playing rings.—You did not see him again then until this happened? No. They had their supper in the snug. I had my supper in the kitchen.—At the time this happened were you up- stairs or downstairs? I was upstairs. I should say it took place about a quarter past twelve.—You went out of the kitchen and upstairs without see- ing your husband? Yes sir. 1 left them all down ' atairs.—Was there anybody dowstairs besides your has- , band, Mr. Critchley and Mr. Cartwright? The sentaut was in the kitchen.—What did you du when you bawd the noim of the shots? I ran downstairs.—Wno was the first person you met? Mr. Critctalay.—What did Mr. Critchley say to you? He told me to go back.—Was that all the conversation ? He said that trom the report he had heard something had taken place. 1, however, went downstairs, and found my husband at the bottom. —When you saw Mr. Critchley did you think he had been downstairs? He came out of his bedrooni.—How did you find your husband ? He was lying at the bottom of the stairs between the bar and the kitchen door on his back.—Where abouts was his head ? His head was opposite the kitchen door and his feet were in the snug.—What happened then ? I shouted for the police.—Where did you go ? I rushed round the vault , and the neighbours got hold of me. The house was ! filled with people. The door being open, they rushed iu. ' Mr. Cartwright rushed out to fetch the police.—Who opened the door? I cannot say. The neighbodrs were in , the house.—Did they get your husband from the position ' you found him? I did not see him because the neigh- bours got hold of me. I did not see him alive again.— Will you tell us when you came downstairs where you first saw Cartwright ? He seemed as though he had , been coming eut of the yard.—How did he get into the yard? Through the snug.—He was coming up the lobby towards where your husband was Icing? Yes.—So that if he had been in the snug at the time this happened, in order to get to the place he was he would have to get past Taus husband? Yes.—ln other words, he was on the other side of your husband's body when you came came downstairs? Yes, • and I shouted to him to fetch the police and the doctor.—Will you look at the revolver and tell me whether you have ever seen it in your hus- band's possession ? I have seen it in a drawer six or eight months ago in the parlour.—Do you know where he got it from ? No.—lt is not Cartwright's revolver ? Mr. Cartwright has no revolver.—Have you seen it since? No, sir.—Do you know whether your husband carried it about? No—About Cartwright, was deceased on good terms with him? Yea, sir. They were always very friendly.—Was there any reason why he should be other- wise than friendly? No. sir. He was rather jealous minded, was my husband.—Do you know why he was jealous? I cannot say. He did not like me to talk with any of the customers.—Was he jealous of Cartwright ? He was jealous of anyone he saw me talking to.—l did not ask you that. I asked was he jealous of Cartwright? I think he was.—Was that without cause? Without cause, sir.—When you come into the snug room and get to the far corner, there is a seat ? Yes, sir.—and there Is a place where a drawer used to be? Yes.—Are you aware that this morning about half-past ten o'clock in the hole where the drawer was, Inspector Spinks in corn- nany with Dr. Langsdale found two teeth? I saw Inspector Spinke take something, but he did not tell me what it V7lol.—The inspector found in the hole or recept- acle this morning two teeth which had been blown out of your husband's month by a shot. Do you know who placed them there? No, sir.—Did you place them there? No. sir.—Do you know anybody who did? _ . _ _ .</p>
        <p>By the Town Clerk : Tell me exactly the time Cart- wright came to lodge with you? About five or six months ago.—Any longer? May be longer.—Was your husband jealoua of Cartwright in particular? No, sir, because he was jealous of anyone.—What did your hus- band say when he got the revolver? Re said that it would he very useful in a publichouse.—Was that all he said ? He said it would do in case someone broke in.—Flow was your husband dressed on the night ef the affair ? In a tweed suit.— Was his .oat on when you went downstairs P—l don't know.—Was Cartwright near to yonr husband?—l don't know. There were a good many people in the house.— Where was the servant P—She was rushing about. —Did you hear vnur hasband speak ?—Nn, sir.—You have heard of no quarrel between your husband am' Cart- wright P—No. sir.—Hid the servant say to you what your husband had said to her P—No, sir. Examined by the Coroner : A little while ago you had to put some razors away out of your husband's possession ?—Yes.—flow long ago P—Not long age. May be three months.—Singe Cartwright came to lodge with you ?—Yes —What was his state of late ?—lie has been drinking, and he said that he would cut his throat, and one night I slept in the servant's bedroom.—l don't want to pre,a you unnecessarily, hut it is absolutely es- sential that we should know the relations between Cart- wright, your husband, and yourself, and I must put it I Plainly to you. Were there any immoral relations be- tween von and Cartwright ?—No, sir.—You are clear of that ?—Yes, sir.—Did your husband think so ?—N'— You did not think that he was under the impression that there were immoral relations between Cartwright and yourself ?-11n, sir. I would not have allowed Mr. Car- twright to have remained if I had thought that.</p>
        <p>Ftv 7ir. Hirt : Had he had any drink that nieht ? YOR, sir.—Was he able to take care of himself ? 0 yes. —Did you hear the first shot ? Yes. it was like a train. —How !on e was it between the first and second shot ? —Almost immediately.—Ancl the third ? I did not hear the third. . . .</p>
        <p>By Mr. Cartwright : How long has your husband been in the habit of drinking? He has always drunk since we hays been married, but not as much as he has done since we went to the public-house.—Do you remember me coming to coo saying. "For God's sake come down, Willie has shot himself "? I cannot remember it at all. I was so excited. The Coroner : Now that you have had time to collect your thoughts, you must be able to tell ttte jury whether Mr. Cartwright did come upstairs. Mrs. Green : Yes, he did. now I remember. The Coroner : You must be careful. I very carefully put the question to you when was the first time you saw rartorright in the transaction. You said you first saw him when you got downstairs. Now you Fay that be earns upstairs. Mrs Green : I was so excited that I had forgotten. Mr. Cartwright: You never saw us quarrel ? Ns.— flea Mr. Green ever threatened to shoot himself ? Yes. It may be two or three months ago.—ls it unusual for me and deceased to sit up late together ? No, you very often did.—Did Mr Green ask me to come and lodge with you ? Yea, he did. The T‘awn Clerk : Although your husband was jealous of CartwriOit, you allowed him to continue to lodge with you ? He was no more jealous of him than of any- body eke. He did not like me to talk to anvone.—Has Cartwright been werking lately ? He has been away en his holidars.—Was he in work before he went for his holidays P—No, he was not. The Coroner : How long is it since Cartwright had his holidays ? It may be three weeks or a month.—Did you go with him ? No, sin—Were you with him at any time ? No.sir.—You say that. Were you at home when he was haring his holidays P No, sir. Not all the time. , —During the time Mr. Cartwright wrs away for his holidays did you: see him anywhere ? No,sir.—Did Mr. Green know that he was away from the "Guardian" Office ? Yes. .</p>
        <p>_ ._ __ _ __. TIM BIRTANT'S EVIDEITCIL Ellen Vernnn. the servant was next called. She said that she hail been at the Shakespeare Inn for about seven roantbs.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : What was the last time you saw Mr. Green before this happened ? The last time I saw him was in the snug when he was playing rings at about twelve o'clock.—How many people were in the snug P There were three. I did not see them separate, as I went to sleep on the sofa in the kitchen.—And you were ar,used by shots ? Yes. I was rather amazed and thought that something had fallen in the yard, and took no more notice. The next thing I heard was Mr. Green scream and rush into the kitchen. He came in at the door where I was, with blood flowing out of his mouth, and ever his right shoulder. He then put his hands on I the table and said" Oh my God, what have I shot my- I self for " ? I rushed out of the kitchen with fright and left him there. I had to pass close by him, and went to Market-gate for a policeman.—Did you see Mrs. Green I then ? No, I did not see her or Cartwright or Critchley. I noticed no one.--Have you seen the revolver produced? No, sir. I only know about it from what Mrs. Green has told me.—What did she tell you ? She told me on I Friday that Mr. Green had threatened to shoot himself, I and he had left his revolver somewhere, she did not I know where.—Can you tell us why Mr. Green had' threatened to shoot himself ? I cannot exactly say. He had been drinking.—Was he jealous of Cartwright? As far as I can see, I think he was.—Was that why he was going to shoot himself ? I cannot say.—Did he ever say he was going to shoot himself because he was jealous of Cartwright? No, sir.—Were Cartwright and Green always on friendly terms? Yea, sir. The Town Clerk : Did Green come straight into the kitchen? I cannot say.—Did you see anybody when he came into the kitchen? No, sir.—Did you hear any noise? I only heard a shot, and then I heard Green rush to the kitchen.—Was there a light in the snug ? Yes, sir, but there was no one in.—Did you hear anyone moving upstairs ? No, sir.—Have you ever heard any quarrel between Green and his wife? I have only heard Mrs. Green say she would go away and leave him, if he did not stop drinking.—You have reason to believe he was jealous of Cartwright? He said it was of no use, as he could see that Cartwright was thought more of than he.—Have you seen anything between Mrs. Green and Cartwright? No.—How does Mrs. Green address Cartwright P She calls him "Tod."—The deceased was in his shirt sleeves that night. By Cartwright: Deceased and myself were always on friendly terms were we not? Yea.—Was I the only one she addressed by my Christian name ? No, she called many customers by their Caristian names.—Did Green mention me when he came into the kitchen? No, not at all.—Did you see me when the shot was fired? No, I did not see you until you had brought the police. —Did you hear me go upstairs and ask Mrs. Green to come down? No, I did not.—Have you ever seen any- thing to lead you to suppose that there was anything any more than friendship between me and Mrs. Green ? No.--In your opinion, did Mr. Green take much drink? Yes.—Have you heard anyone remark about it? Yes, many of the customers remarked about it.—Did I often take him to bed ? Yes. _</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Did You find the teeth in the snug? Witness: No, sir.—There are two teeth evidently be- longing to the deceased which were found in the place where a drawer should be. Did you put them there ? No, sir.</p>
        <p>A_ LODGER'S TESTIMONY.</p>
        <p>Charles Critchley said that be was a brick- layer who lived at Liverpool-road, Bold. He had lodged at the Shakespeare Inn a week on Tuesday, and had frequently stayed on Saturday nights. He knew Cartwright and also Mr. and Mrs. Green. Witness, the deceased, and Cartwright went for a walk on Monday night at about eight o'clock. They got back from Stockton Heath by the quarter past ten 'bus. They had called at the Mulberry Tree Inn. They were all on good terms during the journey, and there had been no quarrel. They stopped in the house until closing time, and had supper in the snug. He went to bed at a quarter past twelve and left Cartwright and Green behind him. Cartwright at that time was going into the yard. There had been no quarrel. He had not got into bed, when he heard a shot. He came out of the room, and met Mrs. Green standing at the top of the stairs in her 'day clothes, He went downstairs and foura the deceased man lying with his head opposite the kitchei door and feet at the bottom of the stairs. He was lyi gO3 his back. He was certain that there was no qtrArrtl, And could not say whether Green was jeal- ous of Cartwright. .</p>
        <p>By the Coroner : From anything you have seen have you reason to think he was jealous of Cartwright ?—I don't really know —Did you see anything between Cart- wright and him which led you to believe that he was jealous of Cartwright as far as his wife was concerned ? —No.—Was there any talk ?—There was talk about the town and in the house. It has been the subject of gossip:</p>
        <p>By the Town Clerk: Hew long was it after you had heard the shots before you came on the stairs P—About five minutes.—So after the first report, Cartwright must have been five minutes with the deceased before you came downstairs ?—He must have been.—Did you hear Mr. Green say anything P—l did not hear him speak.— Did you know deceased went into the kitchen ?—I was not aware that deceased went into the kitchen after he was shot.—Where did you see the revolver P—l saw it in the policeman's band. I never 84W it before.—Did you put the teeth in the dresser P—No, I don't know who placed them there. By the Coroner: Do you know a man named James Dean, bookmeller, 2, Walt,on-st., Liverpoel, who lodges at Id, Dolman's-lane ?—No.---Do you recollect Mrs. Green corning into the snug, saying something to Cartwright, you being present ?—No.—Do you remember Mrs. Green saying to Cartwright " Ted, he is saying you shot him ? "—Yes.—Did you hear Mrs. Green say " Come and see him ? "—No.—Did Cartwright make any reply to the first statement ?—No.—lf Dean gives avid- ence,he will say that Mrs. Green said to Cartwright" Ted, he is saying you shot him," and that when Cartwright made no reply that Mrs. Green said "Come and see him," and Cartwright replied " No." By Cartwright : Was I not always friendly with Green P—l never heard you quarrel.—Was I treated by Mrs. Green any differently from any other customer?— No.</p>
        <p>By a juryman : Cartwright was not quite sober on Monday night.</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE BY DR. BOWDEN.-THE WOUNDS SELF-INFLICTED. Dr. Ihwden, house surgeon at the Warrington Infir- mary, said that deceased was admitted to that institu- tion at one o'clock on Tuesday morning. He Idied in about half an hour.He was dying when he was admitted. Witness asked him what was his name, and he said Green. Witness asked him what had happened to him. He replied--</p>
        <p>The Coroner (interrupting) Do you think ibe knew he was in actual danger of death. Dr. 13,9wden : I cannot actually say. I did not tell him.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : I must be satisfied that deceased knew that he was in actu4 I danger of death, and there was no hope of recovery. Unless that was proved, there would not be evidence to satisfy any judge or magistrate. I must have positive proof that he knew he was dying. You are not able to say whether he knew that he was dying?</p>
        <p>Dr. Bowdtn : I cannot say that. The Clroner : Then you had better not say what passed between you. Dr. Bowden (continuing his evidence) said that he had made a post-mortem examination that morning. He found it to be the body of a well nourished man. His face and lips ware bloodless. There were two bruises on the front ef the shins which were some days old. On the lower lip were two lacerated wounds,one sin. in.,and the other one inch long. Above the left corner of the mouth were two clean cut wounds, one below the lobule of the right ear. The second was an inch and a half above that,and the third was an inch and a half behind the ear. About an inch and a half away there were marks evidently caused by gun powder. Two teeth had been blown out by one shot and the teeth subsequently found by Inspector Sninks were the teeth which were missing. The brains and membranes were uniujured. The cause of death was manifestly from shot wounds. The Coroner: Do you in your opinion think that the shol.a were nelf-inflicted or not ?</p>
        <p>Dr. Bowden I think they were self-Inflicted because they are all close together in the same direction. The weapon was also held close to,because of the singed hair and powder in the skin. The Coroner : Do you think it is, possible that they could have seen inflicted by any other person ? Dr. Bowden: No, not unless the head was held in a vice, or very firmly by another person. The Coroner then addressed Cartwright and asked him whether he would like to make any statement on oath. He thought that after the doctor's evidence that was a favourable opportunity of asking him. He must un- derstansl that if he did give evidence, what he said would be taken down in writing and might be used against him if he were sent to trial. He would also have to undergo cross-examination by himself (the coroner), thp jury, and the Town Clerk just like other witnesses. He again asked Cartwright would be present himselt as a witness ? Cartwright : I would like to do what is best. _ _</p>
        <p>The Core-ner : I will give-yOu-timeTowthinl;bout it. Mr. Moores (a juryman) to Dr. Bowden : Do you think the decessed was conscious enough to understand any questions ? Dr. Bowden : He knew exactly what he was saving. The Coroner: What he said at the Infirmary will not now be taken into consideration. Mr. Moores : But he used some remarks before he was taken away. I wanted to know whether he was really in his right mind. The Coroner : His statement cannot be taken down unless he knew he was dying, and the doctor cannot positively say that.—To Cartwright : Are you I)l'M:tared to give evidence. If you are innocent you can do your- self no harm in presenting yourself bnfore the jury. Cartwright: I will be sworn. TIIR ACCUSED MAKES A STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Cartwright was then sworn. He said t'ht;t his right name was Edward John Widocks. He was a compositor at the "Guardian " Office until the last Sunday in July. He had teen lodging at the Shakespeare Inn in Dolman's- lane. On Monday night he left the Shakespeare lon in company with the deceased and Critchlev at about 8 10, when they went to Stockton Heath. They called at the Mulberry, and stayed there until 10 15, when the 'bus left. They had three drinks each. They Came straight beck to the Shakespeare Inn. • They all three went into</p>
        <p>the parlour, where they stayed until closing time at 11 o'clock, after which deceased proposed that they should have a game of rings. Critchley left at about a quarter past twelve, and at the same time witness went into the back yard,and deceased went with him. When they came back, deceased said, "Where is Charley " ? Witness said, "He must be gcne to bed," and witness said that he thought be would go too. Deceased then said, "Let us have another game." This was not unusual as de- ceased had not to get up very early in the morning. They then threw three rings each, and witness then remarked about the draught. He said he would close the door and see that the windows were fastened. Witness went to close the window when he heard a report; and turning round he saw blood streaming on the shoulder of Mr. Green. Witness looked at him and saw him stagger through the door,and just then two more shots went off. Witness went towards him and deceased dropped into his arms. Witness put him at the bottom ot the stairs, and then rushed upstairs and called Mrs. Green, saying, "For God's sake come down. I believe Will has shot himself." Witness then rushed downstairs, and in the meantime " Nellie " must have opened the door, and a number of people came in. Witness then went for a doctor, and near Academy-st. he met Sergeant Cheney and a constable. He told the constables to go to the "Bang-up," as the "boss" had shot himself. The police went in that direction and witness went for Dr. Cannell, afterwards returning to the Shakespeare Inn. The Coroner : Did you place the teeth of deceased where they were found ? No, sir.—Has there been any complaint about your conduct towards Mrs. Green ? No, sir.—Flas Green never said anything about you and Mrs. Green? No, sir.—Never ? Only once at supper, when we were talking together, he said that I was the second boss. It was only in a jocular manner.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT -CHERRY'S EvIDENcE. P.S. Cheney said that he met the last witness in But- termarket-st. about one o'clock and was told by him to go to the " Bang-up" as the boss had shot himself. Wit- ness went there with P.C. Corcoran and saw P.C. Wil- liamson in company with several others in the act of taking the deceased man into the kitchen. Blood was oozing from his mouth. When Dr. Cannell arrived he ordered him to be taken to the Infirmary. While witness was waiting for the ambulance the deceased clasped his hands and opened his eyes and said " I am dying. I have been murdered. It was Ted Cartwright who did it." Witness then stooped down and got close to him and asked him why he had done it. tie replied "I don't know what he did it for, but he did it." He also further said that Cartwright was a compositor at the " Guar- dran " office and lodged in the Shakespeare Inn. The deceased was then taken to the Infirmary. Witness went into the snug and saw the prisoner there. He told him Green had accused him of murdering him, but he made no reply. Later on in the morning on being charged with being suspected of murdering William Green, he also made no reply. Since that time he had made a voluntary statement in which he said that he was 23 years of age, and was a native of Essex. Until about six weeks ago, he was employed at the Warring- ton " Guardian " office. He had always been on friendly terms with Mr. Green and was in his company from 8.10 p.m. until the time he heard the report of the pistol, and that occurred while his back was turned. Ile then gave an account which practically agreed with the evidence which he had given that night before the jury. By Cartwright: The deceased appeared to be perfectly conscious when he made the statement with reference to Cartwright murdering him. Cartwright appeared to have been drinking, but he was not drunk. He was very excited. . In reply to the Coroner, the Town Clerk said that all the other evidence which remained to be called was mainly corroborative, and it was therefore decided to close the case.</p>
        <p>The Coroner in summing up said that he thought that the jury were now in a position to find a verdict. They had to consider whether there was sufficient evidence before them to feel justified in sending Cartwright for trial. They must decide whether the death was caused by the gun shot wounds, and then whether they were self inflicted or were inflicted by anyone else. They had had the opportunity of examin'ng the wounds for themselves in the presence of Dr. Bowden, and they would have to find whether they could say positively whether the wounds had been inflicted by the deceased man himself, or whether they were inflicted by Cart- wright, for there were only Cartwright and the deceased together when the wounds were inflicted. It seemed to him that if they assumed that Cartwright had inflicted the wounds, they assumed something which was very improbable, and from the doctor's evidence, deceased's head would have had to be held in a vice or by some- thing which would act like a vice. Take away the state- ment of the deceased, and they would not have any evi- dence at all to justify them in assuming that Cartwright had caused the death of the deceased. But before the words of a man could be accepted as evidence the judge must be perfectly satisfied that the person who made it at the time knew that there was no hope of recovery. To get to the true value of the statement of the deceased they must remember that the deceased had been drink- ing for some time. There was nothing to lead Cartwright to do it. On the other hand, if the deceased was a morose man, and thought his wife and Cartwright were unduly intimate, he may have shot himself and then for revenge he could easily say that the man Cartwright had done it. Witnesses did not tell all they knew. When they said something, there was always a good deal be- hind it. It was admitted that there had been common gossip about deceased,his wife and Cartwright,and that there had been talk in the housa.lt WAR therefore possible that deceased being jealous might have taken this course out of revenge. They must remember that Cartwright was to be considered as well as the deceased. It would he unfair to him if the jury thought that the deceased had committed suicide to return an open verdict. They would have to consider whether the deceased had inflicted the injuries upon himself in which case their verdict would be suicide. If they could not make up their minds, they could return an open verdict, while if they believed that Cartwright had inflicted the injuries, he would have to be committed to trial for murder. The room was then c/eared, and after ten minutes con- sultation in private, the jury decided that the deceased had come by his injuries by his own act,and that, he had committed suicide. They had not sufficient evidence to show the state of his mind. They, therefore, returned a verdict that the deceased had committed Puicide, but as to the state of him mind there was no evidence to show. The Town Clerk said that under the circumstances, he would aoply for the discharge of Cartwright that night. Upon hearing the verdict Cartwright swooned away, but he was quickly attended to and soon recovered. The Coroner then addressed him and said that he thought it WAR only fair to him to say that he thoroughly agreed with the verdict of the jury. He (Cartwright) had been relieved of a very unpleasant position, and as far as he (the coro-er) was concerned, be was satisfied that Cartwright had had nothing to do with the death of the deceased.</p>
        <p>As the jury were leayinv the court, one of the jury- men ask the coroner to relieve the present jury from service for twelve months. The Coroner said that ha could not do that, but ha thought they ought to be excused for the remainder of the year.</p>
        <p>Several jurvmen : Thank von. THE PRISONER DISCHARGED. Just after the close of the inquest, two magistrates, Alderman Sutton and Alderman Wright arrived,and the prisoner Cartwright was charged before them with being suspected of causing the death of William Green. the landlord of the Shakesneare Inn, Dolman's-lane,on Tues- day morning, the 24th inst., while on the licensed premise., by shooting him with a revolver. On being asked whether he pleaded guilty or not, he replied "Not guilty."</p>
        <p>, The Town Clerk produced the verdict of the jury and read it to their worships anl said that upon the streneth of that verdict, he applied for the defendant's dis- charge.</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton : I suppose that there is no objection on the part of anyone else. We therefore discharge the prisoner. The proceedings then terminated, after extending up- wards of three hours and a half.</p>
      </div>
