        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S GITILD.—The above Guild in connection with St. Marv's Church, held the first meeting of the season in the Penketh Mission-room on Thursday afternoon. The Rev. J. R. Jones opened the meeting, and there was a good attendance. There is every prospect of a suc- cessful season.</p>
        <p>REVISION COunt—On Wednesday, at tts Conserva- tive Club, Mr. J. T. Foard, barrister, revised the voters' list for the townships of Penketh, Sankey. Cuerdlev and Bold. The Liberals were represented by Mr. Renshaw, and the Conservatives by Mr. Owen. There vere also nresent the assistant overseers of the different parishes. There was little business. Four new claims were pre- sented, but only one, that of Mr. F. Dale,was sustained. There was one objection by the Conservatives against Mr. W. Longshaw,who however sustained his claim and was allowed 25. 6d. expenses. PENKETIC MISSION...—On Wednesday night the harvest thanksgiving services were continued at St. Mary's Mission Room. The mission was tastefully decorated with flowers, fruit, and suitable mottoes. Harvest hymns were sung,and the anthem" Let the earth bring forth fruit " was rendered by the choir. The Revs. E. S. Jackson and J. R. Jones were present. A very good sermon was delivered by the Rev. J. J. Langham, of Padgate. who is a favourite with Panketh congrega- tions. The collection amounted to £8 Bs. 7d. Mr. E. Foster presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>CrYCLllS.—Gantarth, Sole Agent, Bridge-st., Warring on.</p>
        <p>1 SEWING MACHINES Hand £2 18s. Bd. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. Durros 18 and 18 Sankey-et., Warrinston.—Adet. LAWN MOWERS. -GARLAND, Ironmonger, Warrington and Earlesto.m. AMERICAN ROCKER CHAIRS, sale pries, 12s. 6d. BAMBOO AISLES. with two trays, sale price, 4s. Ild,—J. and W. DUTTON, Tankey.st., Warrington.—Advt. Robert Wilkinson, a telegraphist in the Post office ser- vice was on Tuesday committed by the Manchester city magistrates to the assizes for trial on charge of forging and uttering a telegraphic mosey order for £lO.</p>
